/* Machine translations of Logik Dohna-Wundlacken (uncorrected OCR), in the manner of Guyer and Wood: see translation-briefs.js. Not the translators' own work. */
window.KANT_TR = window.KANT_TR || {};
KANT_TR['dohna/guyer'] = {"dohna-s02-001":["[First hour, the 23rd of April, from 7 — 8]",[]],"dohna-s02-002":["Prolegomena",[]],"dohna-s02-003":["[All things between heaven and earth happen and act according to rules, e.g. bodies according to the laws of gravity.]",["OCR \"zAvischen\" silently read as \"zwischen\"."]],"dohna-s02-004":["One must think over one's thinking, i.e. do it according to rules. Every language is bound to certain particular rules. This now takes place preeminently with dead languages, where one can properly designate the rules. But one can also use the rules without properly making them nameable. These rules one learns [*exercitat*] gradually through attempts. The first are faulty, finally one arrives at skill. Among the rules of thinking there are universal ones, which go to particular objects without distinction. So there are also universal rules of language. Such a grammar contains not words, not *copia vocabularum*, but only the form of the language. We will be able to represent to ourselves a universal doctrine (of thinking). This universal doctrine of thinking is called logic, doctrine of the understanding. It is preparation to think about objects.",["OCR \"Regem\" read as \"Regeln\".","nachdenken rendered \"think over\"; Lehre = \"doctrine\"; Verstandeslehre = \"doctrine of the understanding\".","allgemein rendered \"universal\" throughout; Gegenstände = \"objects\" (Objekt also \"object\"; see later passages).","\"Fertigkeit\" rendered \"skill\" (not in the glossary)."]],"dohna-s02-005":["The understanding is the faculty to think (to understand something), of rules, power of judgment the faculty to subsume, reason to infer (to have insight into something), what belongs thereunder. A rule is a concept, under which much, a manifold of representations, is contained.",["OCR braces \"{etwas zu verstehen)\" read as parentheses.","The clause \"was dahin gehört\" and the genitive \"der Regeln\" are left as syntactically loose as in the German.","einsehen = \"have insight into\"; Mannigfaltigkeit rendered \"manifold\"."]],"dohna-s02-006":["We can distinguish:",[]],"dohna-s02-007":["1. the necessary [*necess.*] universal rules of thinking, without distinction to what it goes. Were they not universal, they would not be necessary.",[]],"dohna-s02-008":["[2. the contingent ones.]",[]],"dohna-s02-009":["These rules concern only the form of all rules of thinking.",[]],"dohna-s02-010":["[E.g. If human beings are mortal, then they also have (once) indeed … been. If the one proposition is true, so is the other too. Such a judgment, a hypothetical, conditioned judgment.]",["\"wohl t gewesen\" is garbled in the OCR; the missing word is not supplied and is marked by an ellipsis."]],"dohna-s02-011":["The content of a cognition is the object or the matter and is distinguished from the form. A universal logic must abstract from all objects of thinking.",["Objekt and Gegenstand both \"object\"; here Objekt."]],"dohna-s02-012":["The necessary universal rules of thinking must further be derived *a priori*, not from experience. Empirical is called everything that comes from experience (*ex cognitione oriuntur*) and is opposed to the (transcendental) cognition *a priori*. One does indeed give to the latter empirical knowledge as an aid. But it is never good, rather always better, if one cognizes everything from reason.",["Latin \"ex cognitione oriuntur\" kept as printed.","\"Kenntnis\" rendered \"knowledge\" (glossary), distinct from Wissen, also \"knowledge\".","\"dem Erkenntnisse a priori\": a priori could attach to the cognition or to the opposition; left as in German."]],"dohna-s02-013":["The logical rules are not those according to which we think, but according to which we ought to think. [Logic contains no matter at all, only form of thinking.] Logic must contain principles *a priori*. [Hence logic is a science, and grammar is not, because its rules are contingent.] Consequently every logic in which rules from experience occur is no longer pure. [The rules about the sensibly perfect are contained in aesthetics.]",["\"das sinnlich Vollkommene\" rendered literally; Ästhetik = \"aesthetics\"."]],"dohna-s02-014":["Universal is that which is universal according to reason, empirically universal only insofar as it is always so in experience. *Paedeutica* (child-rearing) [the Greek word is garbled in the OCR]. Doctrine of practice is no longer pure logic, but only the finer exercise, application of the same. [The distinct concept is that of which I am conscious of the marks. Without these universal rules (even if one is not conscious of them) thinking in general is not possible — hence logic is propaedeutic of all sciences, the supreme touchstone that something be in conformity with the laws of the understanding.] All psychological remarks must be excluded from (pure) logic. All rules provable in logic in general are nevertheless still always in need of a ground from which they are derived. Many propositions (e.g. that of contradiction) can be proved neither *a priori* nor empirically at all.",["Greek word after Paedeutica is garbled (\"TrouSsuTiKri\"); not reconstructed, replaced by a bracketed remark.","\"Universell\" and \"allgemein\" are both rendered \"universal\".","Übungslehre = \"doctrine of practice\"; Probierstein = \"touchstone\".","The final sentence's \"gar nicht weder … noch\" is kept as double negation in sense."]],"dohna-s02-015":["[Is there a natural logic? *Logica est scientia. Omnis scientia vero est artificialis. Ergo* — ]",[]],"dohna-s02-016":["A proof that is made entirely *a priori* [at the same time intuitively with insight into the necessity], one calls demonstration. What now is not capable of any demonstration (*dignum*) is empirical.",["A stray angle bracket before \"(dignum)\" in the OCR was dropped as an OCR mark.","\"Einsehn\" = \"insight\"."]],"dohna-s02-017":["We can consider this science, whether it be doctrine or critique [ — goes to use — ] or both together.",["Doktrin = \"doctrine\", Kritik = \"critique\"."]],"dohna-s02-018":["[Logic as doctrine can be called a canon of the understanding and of reason. A canon is demonstrated critique (in Greek it indicates: rule).]",[]],"dohna-s02-019":["Logic is doctrine, it gives (*praebet*) rules to hand, it is demonstrated theory.",["OCR \"«(praebet)\" read as \"(praebet)\"."]],"dohna-s02-020":["Doctrine [is a sum total of rules, where the rules must precede the product (critique is the use of the power of judgment, where the product precedes the rule)] contains the ground of the estimation whether something is true or false. It can have twofold use, once only as critique, the other as organon. The first time e.g. doctrine of taste, only critique [serves to assess something, e.g. a poem], teaches us not to make something tasteful ourselves. [Critique requires that the product already be there, even if the technical rules can already be given beforehand.]",["Beurteilung = \"estimation\" and beurteilen = \"assess\" per the glossary; Urteil is \"judgment\".","\"Das erstemal\" / \"das andere\" kept elliptical.","\"Organon\" kept untranslated."]],"dohna-s02-021":["Mathematics is doctrine, but at the same time organon [through which a cognition becomes possible as to content] of great domain, namely e.g. arithmetic. Is logic also organon? No, it serves only for critique, like grammar (which has much similar with logic), from which one cannot properly learn the language, wealth in words belongs to it too.",["OCR \"nänilich\" read as \"nämlich\".","\"Umfang\" = \"domain\".","\"dem Inhalte nach\" = \"as to content\"."]],"dohna-s02-022":["Doctrine is demonstrated discipline [institution, principle of production]. Logic does not reach to organon, it does not have objects. If, however, a logic is nevertheless misused for organon, one calls it dialectic. [The art of speaking *pro et contra* in the case of every object. Unintentionally reason can deceive itself, if it transgresses the laws of logic.] But if it is to serve merely for estimation, it is called analytic or logic of truth. Dialectic, by contrast, only logic of illusion.",["OCR \"Un vorsätzlich\" read as \"Unvorsätzlich\".","Schein = \"illusion\"; Beurteilung = \"estimation\"."]],"dohna-s02-023":["[The use of logic is analytic if it is used only for canon, dialectical if it is also used for organon, then it is logic of illusion and deceives us. Logic as canon is called analytic (logic of truth). Zeno of Elea, a great dialectician.]",[]],"dohna-s03-001":["[2nd hour]",[]],"dohna-s03-002":["Thinking means representing something to oneself in a concept. Logic can be called doctrine of reason, not merely rational doctrine [science of reason not according to matter, because its object alone is human reason. Mathematics, physics, morals are indeed also sciences of reason, but only according to form, not according to matter.] Logic occupies itself only with the formal rules of thinking. [The object is the matter, the subject the form.]",["Vernunftlehre = \"doctrine of reason\"; vernünftige Lehre = \"rational doctrine\"."]],"dohna-s03-003":["Can instruction in the fine arts be doctrine? The rules of the same can serve only for estimation. Aesthetics permits no doctrine, only a critique. Sciences, however, permit doctrine. For a touchstone of the correctness of the judgment of taste, fine art has no rules, but doctrine has rules.",["Beurteilung = \"estimation\"; Geschmacksurteil = \"judgment of taste\"."]],"dohna-s03-004":["By barbarism one understands not only the spread of total ignorance, but also preeminently the corruption of taste. This we would have to fear, if we did not still have such splendid models from antiquity. [Namely poets, fine minds, e.g. Horace etc. Taste is to be formed and maintained only through examples, not through rules. Remark: Polyclitus, a famous sculptor of antiquity, made a statue that was afterwards always used as a model. *Archetypon*, original image, lies in everyone's head, which must approach it.]",["\"Einreißen\" rendered \"spread\" (literally a tearing-in).","\"schöne Geister\" = \"fine minds\" (Geist = mind).","The syntax of the last clause (\"was sich ihm nähern muß\") is left ambiguous as in the German."]],"dohna-s03-005":["Fine art therefore needs models (*exemplar*), not so science, it needs rules. A doctrine, insofar as it can be demonstrated, is called canon. Logic is canon, not merely critique, it is also only means of estimation. [Canon is an inviolable law. So it could not be applied to taste, where everything depends on habit and the liking of others.]",["\"Muster\" = \"model\"; Beurteilung = \"estimation\".","\"sie ist auch nur Mittel der Beurteilung\" is kept as printed, though its sense with \"nicht allein … auch nur\" is awkward."]],"dohna-s03-006":["Is logic as canon also a means to the acquisition of science? No. Logic abstracts from all content, thus also from all cognition, [it only makes our thinking correct] and it is no organon. But mathematics is not only canon, but also a splendid organon. [Yes, certainly the greatest, for it occupies itself with matter, with the measurement of time and of space.] Mathematics also belongs to natural science.",["Erkenntnis = cognition; Inhalt = content; Mittel = means; Wissenschaft = science; Naturwissenschaft = natural science; Zeit = time; Raum = space.","The last sentence (\"Auch zur Naturwissenschaft gehört Mathematik\") is rendered in natural English order; the German is word-for-word more awkward."]],"dohna-s03-007":["Logic is now divided:",["Eintheilen = divide (Einteilung = division)."]],"dohna-s03-008":["1. into the *logica naturalis* (natural) and (only a complement),",["\"Complement\" is the German Complement, a Latin loan."]],"dohna-s03-009":["2. into the *logica artificialis* (artificial). This is the logic that is really *explicite*, not *implicite*, use of our understanding. [At bottom there is only an artificial one, for natural logic there is not at bottom — natural grammar.]",["Verstand = understanding; Gebrauch = use. *explicite*/*implicite* are Latin in the source and are italicized."]],"dohna-s03-010":["It is presented 1. scientifically, as a science is always presented, 2. scholastic-popularly, in conformity with the schools, adapted to the subjective ground of the faculty of cognition of each. [Popularity detracts from perfection.] All science must be presented in conformity with the schools. This is a consequence of perfection.",["Wissenschaft = science; Vollkommenheit = perfection; Vermögen = faculty; Grund = ground; Erkenntnisvermögen = faculty of cognition.","\"Konsequenz\" is rendered \"consequence\" (it is not Folgerung); \"schulgerecht\" = \"in conformity with the schools\" (literal)."]],"dohna-s03-011":["Now we can divide logic:",[]],"dohna-s03-012":["1. into the logic of healthy understanding, *sensus communis*,",[]],"dohna-s03-013":["2. into the logic of speculative understanding, gives rules *in abstracto*.",["The grammatical subject of \"gibt\" is left as in the German (it does not say whether the logic or the understanding gives the rules)."]],"dohna-s03-014":["Common understanding is the faculty of being able to judge according to rules *in concreto*. [Not the plain. This word is absurd.] Common understanding is called healthy if it is correct. Morals must be cognized entirely *in concreto*, but physics also has *abstracta*.",["gemein = common; urteilen = judge (v.); richtig = correct; erkennen = cognize.","\"Nicht der schlichte\" is translated as it stands: the noun is elided in the German."]],"dohna-s03-015":["A logic is to be presented *in abstracto*. It is propaedeutic [preliminary exercise for sciences] of all sciences, as concerns its content, e.g., with regard to physics and morals.",["Propädeutik: Greek-derived, rendered \"propaedeutic\" in roman type."]],"dohna-s04-001":["[3rd hour, April 26th, 1792]",[]],"dohna-s04-002":["Can it also be (regarded) as propaedeutic of philosophy? No. For it is itself a part of philosophy [and indeed the one with which we are to begin], as we shall soon hear.",["Teil = part."]],"dohna-s04-003":["All our cognition is either (according to its way of acquisition)",["Art = way (Erwerbungsart = way of acquisition)."]],"dohna-s04-004":["1. rational, insofar as it is derived a priori from reason (e.g., pure mathematics, metaphysics),",["\"a priori\" could attach to \"derived\" or to \"from reason\"; the English keeps the placement of the German."]],"dohna-s04-005":["2. historical, as such it is:",[]],"dohna-s04-006":["a) subjectively historical, if the cognition in the subject rests on empirical grounds;",["Grund = ground."]],"dohna-s04-007":["b) objectively historical, if the ground of cognition can never be other than historical. Cognitions that are (objectively) rational can nevertheless be subjectively historical. [Logic itself is system. The cognitions conforming to it are partly systematic, partly fragmentary. — To methodical cognition the tumultuary is opposed.] There can be in mathematics a subjectively historical cognition, even if it is certainly objectively rational, which takes place especially with the art of land-surveying. Also, unfortunately, with natural religion [which many believe merely from lazy trust in the reason of others, in order not to break their heads. If they then forget the words, the matter too is forgotten.]",["The OCR \"<( objektiv)\" is read as \"(objektiv)\"; the angle bracket is treated as an OCR slip.","subjektiv/subjektivisch and objektiv/objektivisch are both rendered \"subjectively/objectively\".","Erkenntnisgrund = ground of cognition; Vernunft = reason; \"tumultuarische\" = \"the tumultuary\" (noun use, as in German)."]],"dohna-s04-008":["[What is philosophy?] All cognition of reason is either 1. one through concepts, 2. one through construction of concepts. The latter one calls presenting a concept, *exhibere*. This is cognition of reason only insofar as the concepts correspond with one another (in intuition) a priori. A cognition of reason through concepts is called philosophical. Thus a system through concepts is philosophy.",["Vernunfterkenntnis = cognition of reason; Begriff = concept; Anschauung = intuition; darstellen = present.","OCR braces \"{in der Anschauung)\" read as parentheses. \"a priori\" stands after \"(in intuition)\" as in the German, and may go with \"correspond\" or with \"in intuition\"."]],"dohna-s04-009":["[To construct a concept means to give it a corresponding intuition a priori.] A cognition of reason through construction of concepts is called mathematical. Thus a system mathematics.",["OCR \"ihni\" read as \"ihm\"; \"a priori\" may go with \"give\" or with \"intuition\", as in the German. \"Also ein System Mathematik\" is left elliptical."]],"dohna-s04-010":["A system must always be thought through, a manifoldness in interconnection, a combination of cognitions from a common principle [be]. Hence we can learn much geography, but not in system, only as aggregate. The philosopher can be considered as [an] artist of reason and teacher of wisdom. Philosophy (in the practical sense) is the science of the ultimate ends of human reason. Philosophy as art of reason one calls speculative, as doctrine of wisdom practical. The ancients always called philosophy doctrine of wisdom.",["Mannigfaltigkeit = manifoldness (the glossary gives Mannigfaltige = manifold); Zusammenhang = interconnection; Verbindung = combination; Prinzip = principle (Grundsatz would also be \"principle\").","Vernunftkünstler = artist of reason; Vernunftkunst = art of reason; Weisheitslehrer = teacher of wisdom; Weisheitslehre = doctrine of wisdom; Zweck = end.","OCR \"<lm praktischen Sinn)\" read as \"(Im praktischen Sinn)\"."]],"dohna-s05-001":["[4th hour, the 27th]",[]],"dohna-s05-002":["One can make a distinction between the two expressions to learn philosophy and to learn to philosophize. Learning means to imitate the judgments of others, is thus very different from one's own reflection. [Not to learn philosophy — but to learn to philosophize, otherwise it remains only imitation — but to arrive at it oneself through exercise of the understanding, that is what matters. Only he who is capable of the self-use of his reason is called philosopher. Philosophy is scientific cognition *in abstracto* — it is itself science.] To learn to philosophize means: one must learn to use one's reason oneself, and indeed a use of healthy and speculative reason takes place here. We must exercise it in concepts. Practical cognition, insofar as our understanding aims at formation and end: and practical reason is the ultimate end of human | wisdom]. Philosophy as doctrine of wisdom presupposes a teacher of wisdom. The ancients demanded of a teacher of wisdom that he also be practically a wise man. [Wisdom is the agreement with the final ends of all things. All other cognitions have their utility only as means — only philosophy leads to the ultimate final end — wisdom. (The doctrine of the highest good.) Philosophy — love of wisdom.] The Cynics [the name derives from Cynosarges, a house-site where they usually assembled] took, as they called it, the short way to happiness, by exercising themselves to be able to do without everything.",["Nachdenken = \"reflection\" here (not Überlegung); Ausdruck = expression; Urteil = judgment; Endzweck = final end, letzter Zweck = ultimate end.","The \"|\" and the unmatched \"]\" after \"wisdom\" stand as in the text; the OCR bracket after \"Weisheit\" may be misplaced.","\"Dies/Philosophieren lernen\" and \"Bildung\" (= formation) are rendered literally; \"Hausstätte\" = \"house-site\"; \"Kyniker\" = Cynics."]],"dohna-s05-003":["Now we want to speak something of philosophy as sum total of information and of its history.",["Inbegriff = sum total; Kenntnisse = information (glossary also: acquaintance, knowledge)."]],"dohna-s05-004":["The first philosophers were poets. For time belonged to it, to find out words for abstract concepts, hence at first one represented the supersensible thoughts under sensible images. Pherecydes first wrote philosophy in prose. [The founder of the Ionic school is Thales. To the Eleatic belong Parmenides, Xenophanes. Because of poverty of language one could at that time philosophize only in poetry.]",["OCR \"WTorte\" read as \"Worte\"; \"Thaies\" as \"Thales\".","übersinnlich = supersensible; sinnlich = sensible; vorstellen = represent."]],"dohna-s05-005":["The most famous sect is the Platonic, then the Aristotelian. These were called *Peripatetici* [stayed in the Lyceum, this was a colonnade, where teaching was done *ambulando*], the first Academics. The Stoics (under Zeno of Citium) had the name from στοά, *porticus*. The Epicureans — (*Horti*) [Epicurus taught therein].",["OCR \"die erstem Akademiker\" read as \"die ersten Akademiker\"; the reference of \"the first Academics\" is left as ambiguous as in the German.","OCR \"crroa\" read as στοά; braces read as parentheses."]],"dohna-s05-006":["The so-called Academy (i.e., the school of Plato) was at first wholly dogmatic, but afterwards degenerated into mania for doubt, skepticism. Carneades cannot be regarded as founder of a new school, but only as an extraordinary orator. Arcesilas however founded a new one.",["Zweifelsucht = \"mania for doubt\" (literal); \"Arcesilas\" kept as spelled."]],"dohna-s05-007":["[Anaxagoras was the first philosopher who also taught theology.] The ancients divided the whole of philosophy into three parts, namely logic, physics and ethics. Only it is still to be considered whether here a dichotomy would not be better than a trichotomy. [Practical | philosophy | : 1. ethical, Socrates never gave himself out as a philosopher. His fragmentary doctrines Plato brought into a system. 2. political, rules of prudence, Pythagoras. With regard to him everything still lies in darkness. A member of the subdivision comes into the upper division.]",["OCR \"Trichometrie\" is read as \"Trichotomie\" (\"trichotomy\"); doubtful reading.","Lehre = doctrine; Obereinteilung = \"upper division\"; the \"|\" marks are carried over where they stand; \"him\" (Pythagoras or Socrates) is ambiguous in the German as well."]],"dohna-s05-008":["We have now actually divided philosophy:",[]],"dohna-s05-009":["1. into the formal, this is logic.",[]],"dohna-s05-010":["2. into the material. This one can divide into the theoretical and practical.",[]],"dohna-s05-011":["Ethics has in more recent times been called doctrine of virtue. Physics can also be considered in two ways.",["Tugendlehre = doctrine of virtue; Betrachtung/betrachten = consideration/consider."]],"dohna-s05-012":["1. as *physica rationalis*, pure doctrine of nature [pure is the opposite of empirical], this has since Aristotle been called metaphysics.",["Naturlehre = doctrine of nature; rein = pure."]],"dohna-s05-013":["2. as *physica materialis* or as metaphysics and empirical physics. All cognition is pure if no influence of empirical information is with it.",["Kenntnisse = information (see s05-003); \"dabei\" = \"with it\"."]],"dohna-s05-014":["The *physica empirica* one can again divide",[]],"dohna-s05-015":["1. into the doctrine of bodies, *physica specialis* and",["A stray \"f\" after \"specialis\" in the OCR is omitted as a probable footnote sign or OCR slip."]],"dohna-s05-016":["2. into the doctrine of the soul, *psychologia specialis*.",["OCR \"ps}rchologia\" read as \"psychologia\"; stray \"f\" omitted as in the preceding passage."]],"dohna-s05-017":["If we regard philosophy as the sum total of several sciences, then we want first to look at the so-called 7 free arts: 1. grammar, 2. rhetoric, 3. dialectic, 4. arithmetic, 5. music, 6. geometry, 7. astronomy. [This division was made by Hrabanus Maurus (at the time of Charlemagne) for the sake of theology. — At that time all sciences were divided into a) upper faculty: 1. preservation of blessedness, 2. of freedom and property, 3. life and health, in general the *esse*; b) *melius esse*, the lower faculty].",["OCR \"Mir\" corrected to \"wir\"; \"esse;b)melius\" separated as \"esse; b) melius\".","Inbegriff = sum total; Wissenschaft = science; Fakultät = \"faculty\" (not Vermögen, which is also \"faculty\")."]],"dohna-s06-001":["[5th hour, April 30th] *Tractatio ipsa*",[]],"dohna-s06-002":["Logic is the science that contains the formal rules and principles of thinking. It is usually divided:",["Wissenschaft = science; Prinzipien = \"principles\" (the same English as Grundsatz)."]],"dohna-s06-003":["1. into the theoretical,",["\"Logic\" (Logik) is supplied by gender agreement in the German (die theoretische); the English noun is not repeated, as in the German."]],"dohna-s06-004":["2. into the practical, but there is no such one, for in that case it would have to be applied to objects, which does not take place at all, for it contains only the formal rules of thinking.",["Gegenstände = objects."]],"dohna-s06-005":["One can therefore rather | divide | it:",["The signs | are carried over as they stand in the Academy text."]],"dohna-s06-006":["1. into the doctrine of elements, contains the rules in general,",["Elementarlehre = doctrine of elements; the grammar (\"contains\" without subject) is kept as in the German."]],"dohna-s06-007":["2. into the doctrine of method, contains the principles of science.",["Methodenlehre = doctrine of method."]],"dohna-s06-008":["The doctrine of method is the sum total of items of knowledge, insofar as they are made into a system [ — it contains an instruction for the way in which a system of cognition is to be obtained]. It constitutes the so-called practical part.",["Kenntnisse = \"items of knowledge\" (plural of Kenntnis); Erkenntnis = cognition."]],"dohna-s06-009":["In the history of logic that is the most excellent phenomenon, in that it contended with itself whether indeed any cognition is certain. Whoever now believed that it could be completely proved was called a dogmatist, but the doubters skeptics. [Skeptics wanted to prove that one can obtain certainty about nothing at all, and thus fell into contradiction with themselves — a *kathartikon* that cancels itself. — Their acuteness with which they attacked the scholastics is to be admired.] They were in the end a useful sect. [Pyrrho was the oldest of all skeptics — afterward Arcesilas, Carneades.]",["\"sie\" in \"daß sie ... bewiesen werden könnte\" is left as \"it\"; its antecedent (Erkenntnis or Logik) is ambiguous in the German.","\"Kathartikon\" kept as a foreign word, in italics; Widerspruch = contradiction."]],"dohna-s06-010":["We have no one who has surpassed Aristotle or increased his <pure> logic (which is also at bottom impossible in itself), just as no mathematician has surpassed Euclid.",["Angle brackets around \"pure\" (rein) are carried over from the Academy text."]],"dohna-s06-011":["| [Something on the history of logic. The Greeks, among all oriental peoples, have the greatest merit for the cultivation of philosophy. Their study afterward declined almost entirely in the times of the Middle Ages, until in the 12th century the scholastics arose in Paris. Properly all their distinctions concerned only theology, hence also the many useless rules and barbarous expressions. One helped oneself in the end by drawing forth the old classics from the scholastic rubbish and the corruption of language (resulting) from it. — Locke became famous through his essay on human understanding — he speaks therein of the origin of concepts, this (however) properly does not belong to logic, but to metaphysics. The result of his investigations: Everything stems from experience. But this does not at all follow from it, that concepts can (only) be set forth in experience. — Leibniz now stepped onto the track. Although he properly wrote no logic, he has nevertheless effected much toward the illumination of concepts (he wrote in defense of his countrymen against the Englishman Locke). He voiced in his works ideas that subsequently moved Wolff to his systematic logic. One finds an extensive description of this in Reusch, *Systema logicae* and in Corvinus. Crusius gives the understanding much nourishment. ]|",["OCR \"eigentHch\" read as \"eigentlich\"; \"Verstande\" = dative of Verstand = understanding.","The opening and closing | and the brackets are carried over as printed; \"Aufhellung\" rendered \"illumination\" (not in glossary).","\"Reusch\" and \"Corvinus\" as printed; \"Wust\" = rubbish."]],"dohna-s06-012":["A main point in logic will rest on the criterion of truth. Now we begin.",["Kriterium = criterion."]],"dohna-s07-001":["I. The Doctrine of Elements",[]],"dohna-s07-002":["[It deals with the concepts of the understanding in general. The division of all thinking — which has various forms that can be exhausted and brought into a system. The general that lies at the basis of all cognition is representation — an inexplicable fundamental concept. Cognition is relation of the representation to an object — combined with an *actio* in the mind — consciousness (representation of our representation), which the obscure representations lack.]",["OCR \"Hegt\" read as \"liegt\"; Gemüt = mind (Geist is also \"mind\" in the glossary; see s07-003).","Gegenstand = \"object\" here; Objekt (s07-004) is also \"object\"."]],"dohna-s07-003":["The ancients divided it into 1. *Apprehensio simplex*, 2. *Judicium* and 3. *ratiocinium*. Two pieces belong to our cognition, intuition and concept. Representations can also be related to something other than cognition, namely to the feeling of pleasure and displeasure (the way in which we are affected by things). All our intuition belongs to sensibility, every concept to the understanding. Sensibility contains two faculties, sense and imagination [this is the magic power of the human mind] or the faculty of intuition of an object, insofar as it is not there, sense however that insofar as the object is there.",["Geist (\"mind\" in \"menschlichen Geistes\") and Gemüt (s07-002) are both rendered \"mind\".","\"sie\" (\"teilten sie\") left as \"it\"; the antecedent is not stated in the passage.","Vermögen = faculty; Anschauungsvermögen = faculty of intuition; Kraft = power."]],"dohna-s07-004":["[All our representation is: 1. sensation — relation of the same to the subject, 2. cognition — relation to the object. 1. e.g. pleasure — lies merely in me — not in the object.]",["Objekt = \"object\" (as is Gegenstand elsewhere); Empfindung = sensation."]],"dohna-s08-001":["On the Consciousness of Representation or Apperception",[]],"dohna-s08-002":["This is the faculty of representation in relation to an object [that belongs to our state], which in apprehension is not yet there at all, but is as it were produced (grasped). In the thought that the human being can say: I am — there lies exceedingly much. The consciousness of our concepts is always difficult. [It is chiefly required for this. From the lack of consciousness animals too are incapable of concepts — intuition they have. — Consciousness is a quite separate dimension of the faculty of cognition (hence no gradation from animals to the human being takes place).] The concept contains that which is general to many {(different)} representations. A concept is called:",["\"{(verschiedenen))\" is garbled in the OCR; rendered as \"{(different)}\" with the braces as they stand, reading doubtful.","\"Es wird vorzüglich dazu erfordert\": \"It\" and \"this\" left unresolved as in the German.","Zustand = state; fähig = \"capable\" (Fähigkeit = capacity)."]],"dohna-s08-003":["clear, of which one is conscious. (The savage would ask: What is a house?)",["\"dessen man sich bewußt ist\": genitive relative, \"of which one is conscious\"; Wilde = savage."]],"dohna-s08-004":["distinct, if one is conscious not only of the partial concepts but also of the total concepts. Distinctness we need in all concepts that we want to communicate to others. One can demand of no concept that contains no manifoldness (partial representations) in itself (distinctness). E.g. time. [With this concept one can demand only clarity — it is only a simple representation.]",["The placement of the parenthetical \"(Deutlichkeit)\" at the end is kept as in the German, so the construction stays as loose as the original.","deutlich = distinct; Deutlichkeit = distinctness; Klarheit = clarity; Mannigfaltigkeit = manifoldness; einfach = simple."]],"dohna-s08-005":["If a representation clear on the whole is *particulariter* obscure, then it is indistinct. [Indistinct representations one calls confused (*confuse*). They come, however, properly from the weakness of the representation. But if there is order in the | representations |, then it comes from the confusion. Only if the representation is composed can it be confused.] Concepts of which one is not conscious are obscure. This holds, however, only of obscure representations. These are either:",["OCR \"ScliAväche\" read as \"Schwäche\"; \"j\" after \"Vorstellungen\" read as the sign |.","The sentence \"Ist aber Ordnung ... so kommt's von der Verwirrung\" is translated as it stands; the sense is obscure.","undeutlich = indistinct; verworren = confused; Verwirrung = confusion; dunkel = obscure."]],"dohna-s08-006":["1. merely clear, and indeed either",[]],"dohna-s08-007":["a) simple or",[]],"dohna-s08-008":["b) composite, or",["zusammengesetzt = \"composite\" here, \"composed\" in s08-005."]],"dohna-s08-009":["2. merely distinct. This distinctness is:",[]],"dohna-s08-010":["a) distinctness of intuition,",[]],"dohna-s08-011":["b) distinctness of concepts. (In the author only this occurs, but the corresponding intuition, presentation, *exhibitio* is equally necessary.)",["Darstellung = presentation; Autor = author (the textbook, Meier)."]],"dohna-s08-012":["[Order in the representations and concepts is of the greatest importance. There are three relations:",["Verhältnis = relation (Beziehung in s07 is also \"relation\")."]],"dohna-s08-013":["1. subject to predicate,",[]],"dohna-s08-014":["2. dividing members to the divided concept,",["einteilend / eingeteilt: division terms (Einteilung = division)."]],"dohna-s08-015":["3. the relation of ground to sequel.]",["Folge = \"sequel\" per the glossary (Folgerung = consequence)."]],"dohna-s08-016":["It has not succeeded even the philosophers in setting out the concept of virtue completely. One has it in oneself, but cannot give it out from oneself. —",["\"auseinander zu setzen\" = \"setting out\" (literally, setting apart)."]],"dohna-s08-017":["[6th hour, May 1st]",["OCR \"lten\" read as \"1ten\"."]],"dohna-s08-018":["§ 15 and § 16 in our author properly do not belong to logic at all. [K 11. 12]",["\"[K 11. 12]\" is carried over as printed; its reading is doubtful in the OCR."]],"dohna-s08-019":["§ 17. Rational cognition, a distinct way of cognizing something from grounds. Properly a cognition from concepts is cognition of the understanding. To think means to represent something to oneself *discursively*, distinguished from intuition, to represent something to oneself *intuitively*. Reason, the faculty of inferring, to this belong judgments, and these cannot occur without concepts. Receptiveness, *receptivity*, is distinguished from spontaneity [self-activity]. Understanding is the higher faculty of cognition. [For it is the faculty of rules, and these are already concepts.] Then one comes from representations to concepts (*facultas concept* . . .), then to judgments, then to inferences.",["vernünftig = rational; Verstandeserkenntnis = 'cognition of the understanding'; Obererkenntnisvermögen = 'higher faculty of cognition'; Schließen/Schluß = infer/inference.","'discursive' and 'intuitive' are Latinate adverbs in the German; rendered 'discursively' and 'intuitively' (anschaulich = intuitive).","Empfänglichkeit = 'receptiveness'; 'Receptivität' is in roman type in the source and is italicized.","'(facultas concept . . .)' is broken off in the source and left so.","A stray 'f.' after 'geschehen' (apparently a footnote mark in the OCR) is omitted; 'Urteüen' read as 'Urteilen'."]],"dohna-s08-020":["[The higher faculty of cognition contains:",["The square bracket is left unclosed as in the source; it closes in dohna-s08-023."]],"dohna-s08-021":["1. Understanding,",[]],"dohna-s08-022":["2. Power of judgment,",["Urteilskraft = (power of) judgment."]],"dohna-s08-023":["3. Reason.]",[]],"dohna-s08-024":["The understanding is the faculty of the representation of the universal as such. [E.g. definition of the human being in general.]",["Mensch = 'human being'."]],"dohna-s08-025":["The power of judgment is the faculty of representing the particular as contained under the universal [Cajus is a human being] or faculty of subsumption.",[]],"dohna-s08-026":["Reason the faculty of the derivation of the particular from the universal or cognition *a priori*. [All human beings are mortal. Sempronius too is a human being. Sempronius is mortal.",["The square bracket is left unclosed; it closes in dohna-s08-027."]],"dohna-s08-027":["In the first two, understanding and power of judgment, one can teach, in the latter only practice.]",["'letzterer' (feminine, 'the latter') is left as ambiguous as the German; the context suggests reason."]],"dohna-s08-028":["The definition of logic, *cognitio rationalis*, is not the best, could hold only then if it has for its object the use of the rules of the understanding and of reason.",["Object = 'object' (the German here has the Latinate Objekt)."]],"dohna-s08-029":["All cognition must be rational. The author should therefore say cognition of reason. What now is not cognition of reason is historical cognition, rests on empirical grounds. [Scientific cognition is that of which rules *in abstracto* are possible. Common is the cognition insofar as it is not scientific.]",["vernünftig = 'rational'; Vernunfterkenntnis = 'cognition of reason'.","'Seientifisches' read as 'scientifisches' (OCR slip); gemein = 'common'."]],"dohna-s08-030":["One must endeavor to philosophize, not merely to learn philosophy. For if we did the latter our cognition would be merely historical and not drawn from ourselves. A sum total of information as system is science, in which a chief principle, from which everything else is derived, lies at the ground. A cognition is called common insofar as it is not science. It can at times be very vast, but has no system, no unity of the whole. As to matter, he who possesses such a cognition is not opposed to him who has science, but as to form. Indeed, he can even be richer as to matter, but everything is thrown together in confusion. We can count philosophical cognition as belonging to erudition, insofar as we give a philosophical history of what all philosophers have said from of old. But whoever can do this is on that account still no philosopher. One is this only when one can philosophize. Historical cognition as science is erudition. A teacher of reason can be philosopher and mathematician. Whoever possesses all historical cognition as science is called a polyhistor.",["Kenntnisse = 'information' (glossary also allows 'acquaintance', 'knowledge'); Inbegriff = 'sum total'.","'Heget' read as 'liegt'; 'vast' left as in the source (a Latinism).","Vernunftlehrer = 'teacher of reason'; Gelehrsamkeit = 'erudition'; Polyhistor kept as in the source.","'durcheinander geworfen' = 'thrown together in confusion' (a literal 'thrown through one another')."]],"dohna-s08-031":["In the concept of the philosopher one can still think of a twofold distinction:",[]],"dohna-s08-032":["1. as artist of reason, 2. as sage or legislator.",["Vernunftkünstler = 'artist of reason'; Weiser = 'sage'."]],"dohna-s08-033":["To philosophy proper (which is not to be merely art of reason) belongs nothing but metaphysics and morals. These serve as means to cognize our existence as rational beings and to cognize the final end, our goal [ — the highest good — ], the most sublime that we have. From this it now becomes clear that the physicist has no science at all, although one always counts physics among the sciences.",["Existenz = 'existence' (Dasein is also 'existence' in the glossary); letzten Zweck = 'final end'.","The bracketed dashes are reproduced as in the source."]],"dohna-s08-034":["Our author now treats",[]],"dohna-s08-035":["Of beautiful cognition",[]],"dohna-s08-036":["[Beautiful cognition there actually is not at all. In cognition all that matters is the relation to the object — if it is hit, then it is true, but only the presentation can be beautiful.",["The square bracket is left unclosed here; it closes in dohna-s08-039.","'ist er getroffen': 'er' refers to Gegenstand; rendered 'it'. Darstellung = 'presentation'."]],"dohna-s08-037":["Sensibility is the faculty of intuition:",[]],"dohna-s08-038":["a) Sense, faculty of intuition in presence,",["'in der Gegenwart' is rendered 'in presence' to match 'Abwesenheit' (absence) in the next item; 'in the present' is also possible."]],"dohna-s08-039":["b) Imagination, faculty of intuition in the absence of the object.]",[]],"dohna-s08-040":["When I call something beautiful, I thereby express my satisfaction, my pleasure (the relation of the object to the subject, which brings forth an agreeable representation, which determines the mind itself to its own preservation) in the object. The basis of the definition. In everything beautiful we understand only the relation of cognition to the subject, not the object itself at all. E.g. in the description of beautiful regions one describes only how one has been affected by them. It is impossible to portray the object itself. A beautiful cognition there is therefore not at all.",["Wohlgefallen = 'satisfaction' (not in the glossary); Lust = 'pleasure'; Gemüt = 'mind'.","'Die Basis der Definition' is a sentence fragment and is left as is."]],"dohna-s09-001":["[7th hour, the 3rd of May]",[]],"dohna-s09-002":["One divides perfection",[]],"dohna-s09-003":["1. into logical perfection in the agreement of the faculty of cognition with the object,",["Übereinstimmung = 'agreement'."]],"dohna-s09-004":["2. into aesthetic perfection, | it | consists in the agreement of the object with the faculty of cognition of the subject.",["The | signs are carried over from the source."]],"dohna-s09-005":["[Aesthetic perfection is the subjective agreement of the understanding with sensibility - — which enlivens the representation of an object. Because the agreement is only subjective, it will also be possible only through sensation. There arises thereby a feeling of pleasure, just as with sensation of conflict a feeling of displeasure.]",["The ' - —' is reproduced as it stands in the source; Widerstreit = 'conflict'; Unlust = 'displeasure'."]],"dohna-s09-006":["Imagination and understanding are the two only active faculties of cognition of the human mind. But the senses are entirely passive, they necessarily require an object, imagination creates objects for itself.",["Gemüt = 'mind'; Sinne = 'senses' (Sinn = 'sense')."]],"dohna-s09-007":["To all our cognitions belong the following two pieces [two elements, of which one without the other gives no cognition]:",["A stray 'I' at the start of the OCR line is omitted."]],"dohna-s09-008":["1. Intuition (the interpretation of the concept, of thinking);",[]],"dohna-s09-009":["2. Concept. The pure concept like the pure rule gives as yet no distinctness of cognition, intuition belongs to it as well. Conversely intuition without concept is likewise nothing. For without, it would be just the same as if it had seen nothing. E.g. tasteful poetic descriptions of regions, which produce only intuition, serve not at all for cognition, are also only for toilets.",["'Denn ohne wäre es ...': the object of 'ohne' is not stated in the German and is left unstated; 'sie' ('it had seen') is feminine and refers back to Anschauung.","Deutlichkeit = 'distinctness'; 'Toiletten' rendered 'toilets' (dressing tables / grooming is possible)."]],"dohna-s09-010":["But if I merely look solely at beauty, then I demand not teaching but only pleasing entertainment. This satisfaction the mind can arouse itself, it remains thereby in spontaneity. Taste is supposed to be the faculty of having, from the subjective agreement of the powers of cognition, satisfaction in the object.",["Belehrung = 'teaching'; Erkenntniskräfte = 'powers of cognition' (Kraft = power); Geschmack = 'taste'."]],"dohna-s09-011":["[Since beauty can never be cognized through concepts, only through feeling, but this is not as communicable as concepts, which, since they go to the object, the same in all subjects — thus no objective rules can be given about it. Yet the beautiful distinguishes itself from the agreeable in that the former holds subjectively universally for everyone, the latter however extends merely to individual subjects. One will in matters of the agreeable not expect of anyone to pass the same judgment — as with objects of the beautiful, where the judgment of taste is universally valid. — The aesthetic beautiful is to be clarified not through rules but merely through examples — hence the worth of the classics.]",["'die, da sie aufs Objekt gehn, bei allen Subjekten dieselben' has no verb in the German; left without one.","'jenes ... dies' rendered 'the former ... the latter'; ansinnen = 'expect of'; allgemeingültig = 'universally valid'; erläutern = 'clarify'."]],"dohna-s09-012":["With regard to the satisfaction in objects the cognition is twofold.",[]],"dohna-s09-013":["1. The beautiful, the faculty of distinguishing the beautiful one calls taste.",[]],"dohna-s09-014":["2. The sublime, the faculty of distinguishing | of the sublime | one calls feeling.",["'Unterscheidungs vermögen' (OCR break) read as 'Unterscheidungsvermögen'; the | signs are carried over."]],"dohna-s09-015":["Taste belongs to the power of judgment. For it is not | a | means of bringing forth objects, but only of assessing them.",["beurteilen = 'assess'; Mittel = 'means'; the | signs are carried over."]],"dohna-s09-016":["Now the power of judgment is also twofold:",[]],"dohna-s09-017":["1. logical,",["The passage breaks off here; the enumeration continues in the next passage."]],"dohna-s09-018":["2. aesthetic. This is taste [understanding in union with the imagination]. An empirical judgment is produced through the immediate impression that the (representation of the) object [with regard to the feeling of pleasure and displeasure] makes on our mind. Logical power of judgment always has fixed rules. But those of the aesthetic power of judgment rest only on empirical grounds. To quarrel over the pleasure that arises from the senses (e.g. to be amazed why the other does not like sauerkraut) would be very foolish. Hence the proverb *Chacun à son goût* would in this case, if it had the meaning, be correct. But since this is not so [for we do not understand here by taste the allure of the palate, but the aesthetic power of judgment], it is false in every other respect. For taste is aesthetic power of judgment and is universally valid. This can put us on the track of where taste is actually to be posited. [Everything that belongs to sensation is subjective, everything that belongs to intuition, objective.] Everything that rests not on the relation of our faculty of representation to the object, but to the subject, to the representing faculty, is aesthetic. Insofar as we find imagination and understanding harmoniously enlivened through a certain feeling, we have taste. [The freedom of the imagination in agreement with the concept of the understanding. The latter would accomplish nothing without the former, which gives it intuition — the object would vanish for it. The understanding comes to the aid of the imagination, and it brings unity into its products.]",["OCR 'Aväre' read as 'wäre'; 'Chacun ä son gout' read as 'Chacun à son goût'; 'zuhilf e' read as 'zuhilfe'.","Ästhetisch rendered 'aesthetic'; Urteilskraft = 'power of judgment'; Gegenstande/Objekt both 'object' (Gegenstand in 'Gegenstande', Objekt later).","'Daher würde ... wenn es die Bedeutung hätte, richtig sein': the antecedent of 'es' (the proverb) and the sense of 'die Bedeutung' (i.e. the meaning just specified) are left as in the German; the clause may be corrupt or elliptical.","'Gründen' = 'grounds'; 'Eindruck' = 'impression'; 'Gemüt' = 'mind'; 'Anschauung' = 'intuition'; 'Empfindung' = 'sensation'."]],"dohna-s09-019":["|[Taste is art. The understanding and the imagination, which must unite for it, are like two friends who cannot stand each other and yet cannot let go of one another — for they live in a constant quarrel and yet are mutually indispensable. There are, as follows from the above, no beautiful sciences, but only an art of the beautiful. To produce it, genius is required — e.g. a good poem cannot be ordered, it succeeds only when the poet has the mood — a fortunate disposition of mind — for it; rules are not enough for it. There were poets before there were rules of poetics.] |",["'Wissenschaften' = 'sciences' (*Wissenschaft*). The signs | are carried over as they stand."]],"dohna-s09-020":["[8th hour]",[]],"dohna-s09-021":["The logical perfection of representations consists in this, that they represent particular representations as in the universal [ — or formal — (just as the aesthetic is material) completeness is the requisite of logical perfection — it seeks the particular under the universal.] But with the aesthetic it is precisely the reverse. For there universal representations are represented in the particular. In a logical judgment I look, then, only to this, that particular representations be represented in the universal. [With the aesthetic judgment it is again precisely the reverse.] Logical perfection requires bringing intuition to concepts, the aesthetic, concepts to intuition. Logical perfection consists in the agreement of the cognition with the object, the aesthetic in that with the subject and its faculty of cognition.",["OCR 'unigekehrt' read as 'umgekehrt'; 'demio' read as 'dem'.","Vollkommenheit = 'perfection'; Erkenntnis = 'cognition'; Erkenntnisvermögen = 'faculty of cognition'; Vollständigkeit = 'completeness'. *Requisit* is Latinate in the German; rendered 'requisite'.","'im allgemeinen' / 'im Allgemeinen' rendered 'in the universal'; 'allgemein' = 'universal' throughout."]],"dohna-s09-022":["Our author opposes to beauty ugliness (*deformitas*) [ugliness is just as much positive as beauty — an object that I hate, the maximum of disgust], but there is, he says, yet a middle word, dryness <(*Jejunum*> [it is a degree of perfection that mathematics possesses, because it has dryness], and indeed dryness and beauty can very well subsist together [e.g. in a sermon that begins dryly, but in the sequel contains many beauties.]",["The angle bracket before *Jejunum* is carried over as printed, without its closing mate (OCR/apparatus as given). 'Folge' = 'sequel'.","'Mittelwort' rendered literally 'middle word'. 'Gegenstand' = 'object'."]],"dohna-s09-023":["Beauty can go only to the presentation, to intuition, not quite properly to the concept. The great business of propagation is spread through the whole of nature. — The expression beautiful cognition is not at all fitting.",["'Darstellung' = 'presentation'; 'Ausdruck' = 'expression'. The sentence on propagation (*Fortpflanzung*) is given as it stands, with no apparent connection to its neighbors; possibly a transcriber's slip.","'schöne Erkenntnis' is mentioned, not used, in the German; the method would put it in quotation marks only where the source does so, and none appear."]],"dohna-s09-024":["The faculties of our mind can be brought into the following three classes:",["Vermögen = 'faculty'; Gemüt = 'mind'."]],"dohna-s09-025":["1. Faculty of cognition,",[]],"dohna-s09-026":["2. Feeling of pleasure and displeasure,",[]],"dohna-s09-027":["3. Faculty of desire.",["Begehrungsvermögen = 'faculty of desire' (OCR 'Begehrungs vermögen' rejoined)."]],"dohna-s09-028":["A representation is our cognition, and indeed",[]],"dohna-s09-029":["1. In relation to the faculty of cognition it is called logical perfection [in relation to the understanding — truth is a judgment, not a feeling].",[]],"dohna-s09-030":["2. In relation to the feeling of pleasure and displeasure aesthetic perfection [in relation to the power of judgment].",[]],"dohna-s09-031":["3. That it agree with our faculty of desire — practical perfection [in relation to reason].",[]],"dohna-s09-032":["On the perfections of cognition",[]],"dohna-s09-033":["We can have perfection in cognition:",[]],"dohna-s09-034":["1. in quantity — universality —",["Allgemeinheit = 'universality'."]],"dohna-s09-035":["2. with regard to quality — distinctness through concepts [i.e. through consciousness of the marks],",["Deutlichkeit = 'distinctness' (*deutlich* = 'distinct'); Merkmale = 'marks'; Bewußtsein = 'consciousness'."]],"dohna-s09-036":["3. with regard to relation — relation (of the representation) to an object. Truth, agreement of the judgment with the way of thinking. [Truth is here the logical perfection.]",["'Denkart' = 'way of thinking'; 'Beziehung' = 'relation'. The sentence 'Wahrheit Übereinstimmung ...' lacks a copula/punctuation in the OCR; rendered with a comma."]],"dohna-s09-037":["4. with regard to modality (rests on whether it is contingent or necessary). [Here it is the necessity cognized *a priori*.]",["zufällig = 'contingent'; notwendig = 'necessary'. 'a priori erkannte' could attach to 'Notwendigkeit' or be read adverbially with 'erkannte'; kept as 'cognized *a priori*'."]],"dohna-s09-038":["Logical perfection <(according to quantity) consists in the universality of representations, that the concepts become distinct. This universality was objective [and went to representing the particular in the universal *in abstracto* — ]. But aesthetically it is subjective, and this subjective universality is called popularity. [Thus aesthetic perfection goes, on the contrary, to representing the universal *in concreto* — this popularity — to making concepts of the understanding appropriate to the *sensus communis* (i.e. to healthy common understanding). — ]",["The angle bracket before '(der Quantität nach)' is carried over without a closing mate, as in the text. 'dem gesunden gemeinen Verstände' = 'healthy common understanding'; *gemein* = 'common'.","'Allgemeinheit' as 'universality'; the sentence 'daß die Begriffe deutlich werden' is left as an appositive clause as in the German."]],"dohna-s09-039":["The 2nd logical perfection is distinctness through concepts. This is effected through few marks [of which, however, I am also conscious in the partial representations]; the aesthetic through many marks. Logical distinctness arises from this, that in many things I represent little, the aesthetic from this, that in (few) I represent much. Aesthetic distinctness is called liveliness in the combination of many representations. For logical distinctness abstraction is required. To the aesthetic belongs a swarm of side-representations.",["OCR 'D e u 1 1 i c h k e i t' read as 'Deutlichkeit'; 'Neben Vorstellungen' rejoined. 'Teilvorstellungen' = 'partial representations'.","'Verbindung' = 'combination'. 'in viel Dingen' (sic) rendered 'in many things'; 'in (wenigen) vieles' left elliptical, with 'few' in parentheses as in the German."]],"dohna-s09-040":["The 3rd perfection is relation to the object — truth [agreement with the object]. Aesthetically true is that which is valid according to the appearance, the agreement of a judgment with the way of thinking of the subject. Thus the poet goes to the universal illusion, to subjective truth. The poetic is always aesthetically, seldom logically true. E.g. the thunder rumbles. [Truth is agreement of the judgment with the way of thinking.]",["Erscheinung = 'appearance'; Schein = 'illusion'; 'Denkungsart' and 'Denkart' both 'way of thinking'. 'Das Dichterische' = 'the poetic'."]],"dohna-s09-041":["Now the 4th (perfection). Modality rests on whether it is a necessary or merely contingent judgment. An aesthetic necessity, namely when it is needful, according to laws of the imagination, to represent the thing to oneself, not according to laws of reason. Aesthetic necessity is only subjective necessity. Aesthetic perfection is condescension of the understanding to sensibility.",["OCR 'istnur' read as 'ist nur'. Gesetz = 'law'; Sinnlichkeit = 'sensibility'; Einbildungskraft = 'imagination'."]],"dohna-s09-042":["[9th hour]",[]],"dohna-s09-043":["Through intuition an object is given, through the concept thought. If intuition and concept agree together for the enlivening of the cognition itself, they produce in us a liking, and then one calls it beautiful cognition. One must make an effort that understanding and imagination agree together in one business. But this is no longer play. In a beautiful product no new cognition is required, but only the enlivening of the powers of cognition. In general, production of the beautiful is not science, but free play of the imagination. Imagination and understanding are two friends who cannot do without one another, but also cannot stand one another, for always the one does damage to the other. The more universal the understanding is in its rules, the more perfect it is, but if it wants to consider things *in concreto*, then | it | can absolutely do nothing at all without the imagination.",["'Wohlgefallen' = 'liking'; 'Erkenntniskräfte' = 'powers of cognition' (*Kraft* = 'power'); 'Wissenschaft' = 'science'. 'Geschäft' = 'business'.","The signs | around 'er' are carried over as they stand."]],"dohna-s09-044":["The imperfections of cognition are 1. either imperfections of lack (*defectus*) or 2. imperfections of deprivation (*vitium*), better of transgression (*reatus*, *transgressio legis*, *eristreus*).",["OCR 'Un Vollkommenheiten' rejoined as 'Unvollkommenheiten'. *eristreus* is kept as printed; the reading is doubtful and may be corrupt (OCR or transcriber).","'Mangel' = 'lack'; 'Beraubung' = 'deprivation'; 'Übertretung' = 'transgression'."]],"dohna-s09-045":["[Ignorance is like a *tabula rasa* — error like a scribbled-over tablet. Here one has, on top of that, the trouble of wiping away the false in order first to come again to ignorance.",["The opening square bracket is not closed in the passage; carried over as it stands. 'Unwissenheit' = 'ignorance'; 'Irrtum' = 'error'."]],"dohna-s09-046":["Truth Ignorance Error",["Three words standing in a line, probably a diagram whose layout the OCR has lost; rendered as they stand."]],"dohna-s09-047":["+ a a — a]",["Rendered as it stands; a fragmentary mark or formula in the OCR, not interpretable here."]],"dohna-s09-048":["For even if one does not say everything true, still everything that one says must be true. The opposite (*Widerspiel*) is not merely something negative, but really something positive, which in mathematics would have the sign minus. E.g. if a physician not only | does not | replace for the sick person the lack of health, but wholly ruins it for him. From this one sees even that among all imperfections of human existence one must guard oneself most against that of the opposite, because it has the dreadful property that it not only does not benefit but even harms. For if one has learned something false, it costs unspeakable effort first to put oneself again into the state in which one was when one knew nothing of it, in order thus to get to know the truth.",["Widerspiel: not in the glossary; \"opposite\" is used for it.","Existenz = existence; Zustand = state; kennen lernen = \"get to know\"; wissen = \"know\".","The bars around \"nicht\" are the editors' apparatus, kept where they stand."]],"dohna-s09-049":["[10th hour]",["Stunde = hour (lecture hour). OCR \"lOte\" read as \"10te\"."]],"dohna-s09-050":["On the Expansiveness of Learned Cognition",["Weitläufigkeit = \"expansiveness\" (weitläuftig = \"expansive\"), to keep it apart from *extensiv* = \"extensive\"; Erkenntnis = cognition."]],"dohna-s09-051":["[Horizon is the sum total of those cognitions that belong to the end of a human being.]",["Inbegriff = sum total; Zweck = end; Erkenntnis = cognition."]],"dohna-s09-052":["Extensive cognition rests on multiplicity, intensive however on degree. The latter is a magnitude of unity. The former the author calls the expansiveness of learned cognition.",["Vielheit = \"multiplicity\"; Grad = degree; Größe = magnitude; Einheit = unity."]],"dohna-s09-053":["On the horizon of human cognition. — The horizon [horizon, the circle that bounds all objects] is the congruence {agreement) of the bounds of any cognition with the bounds of human perfection. This is bounded, would, if it extended beyond the bounds, fall into chimeras. Nothing can be invisible to us above the horizon. Hence this way of speaking is false. Only that which is under our horizon, we cannot know. We can divide our horizon:",["OCR \"Mir\" corrected silently to \"wir\". The mismatched brace and parenthesis in \"{Übereinstimmung)\" are kept as printed.","Grenze = bound; begrenzen = to bound; Objekt = object; Vollkommenheit = perfection; Redegebrauch = \"way of speaking\" (literally \"speech-use\").","\"Dieser\" (\"This is bounded\") has no clear antecedent in the German; left unresolved."]],"dohna-s09-054":["[Only this belongs in our field. One divides: things that are above, outside and below our horizon — outside it: what we do not need to know, below it: what we ought not to know. This already goes to end, to the practical.]",["OCR \"N\\ir\" read as \"Nur\". \"Man teilt ein\" = \"One divides\" (einteilen = divide). Zweck = end.","The bracket at the opening is rendered as printed; the reference of \"dieser\" is not given in the passage."]],"dohna-s09-055":["1. logical, the determination of our logical horizon [ — relation to the faculty of cognition requires distinctness of abstraction. This would harm the imagination. Aesthetics therefore needs only clarity].",["Deutlichkeit = distinctness; Klarheit = clarity; Einbildungskraft = imagination; Erkenntnisvermögen = faculty of cognition; Bestimmung = determination."]],"dohna-s09-056":["2. aesthetic, according to the agreement with our taste.",[]],"dohna-s09-057":["3. practical, according to the suitability of our cognition to our ends.",["Angemessenheit = \"suitability\"; Zwecke = ends."]],"dohna-s09-058":["The logical horizon we can divide:",[]],"dohna-s09-059":["1. into the historical, and this can be a) objective [e.g. study of nature, history], b) subjective.",["Naturkunde = \"study of nature\" (not in the glossary)."]],"dohna-s09-060":["2. into the rational [objectively rational, insofar as it can be seen into a priori].",["eingesehn = \"seen into\" (einsehen, \"have insight into\"). *A priori* is in the German, kept as it stands."]],"dohna-s09-061":["The historical is incomparably more expansive than the rational [either that of pure reason or metaphysics or 2. that grounded on experience or physiology]. In mathematics the latter, in philosophy the former is the most expansive. The field of mathematics is unsurveyable. Critique is the determination of the (rational->) horizon of human cognition. [There is a general horizon of the cognitions of the human race. The horizon of human cognition, as regards experience, one calls physics. Physics also has its bounds insofar as we do not see with reason whence this or that object.]",["\"(Rational->Horizonts\" is an editorial mark in the source; rendered \"(rational->) horizon\". \"Physiologie\" = physiology.","\"woher dies oder jenes Objekt\" is elliptical (no verb); left so.","\"Erkenntnisses\" in \"Horizont des menschlichen Erkenntnisses\" is the printed genitive form; \"der der reinen Vernunft\" = \"that of pure reason\".","\"das Feld\" = field; \"unübersehbar\" = unsurveyable; \"weitläuftig\" = expansive, as in the heading."]],"dohna-s09-062":["To determine the rational horizon of human cognition is one of the noblest and most difficult occupations of the human mind. Here metaphysicians are accustomed to lose themselves. — Only metaphysics or its (foundation, the) critique of pure reason can show where the bounds of reason begin and where all its faculty ceases. One can occupy oneself | with | the general horizon of humanity and treat of that of individual human beings. Certain cognitions, some assert with complete injustice, we can indeed know, | they | would however not benefit us. [Many cognitions and sciences would not have reached the level if one had asked right at the beginning after their utility. This often manifests itself only then, when they have attained perfection.] *Quaerit delirus et non respondet Homerus.* The human race must learn all cognitions useful to us [in order thereby to cultivate its understanding. — No knowledge of any kind can ever be wholly contrary to end (in general)]. To be sure for individual human beings much may perhaps be dispensable. People divide the information into various fields. For each there are found amateurs, according as they have inclinations to this or that thing.",["Geist = mind; Vermögen = faculty; Wissen = knowledge; Kenntnisse = \"information\" (Kenntnis is rendered consistently thus; wissen = \"know\").","\"Gewisse Erkenntnisse, behaupten einige ... gänzlichem Unrecht, können wir zwar wissen\": the parenthetical clause is kept in place. The sentence is not smoothed.","\"Sache\" rendered \"thing\"; \"zweckwidrig\" = \"contrary to end\"; \"Liebhaber\" = amateurs. The Latin is printed as in the source.","The bars around \"mit\" and \"sie\" are editorial apparatus, kept where they stand."]],"dohna-s10-001":["[11th hour, the 10th of May]",["OCR \"Ute\" read as \"11te\" and \"lOten\" as \"10ten\"; the reading of the date is doubtful."]],"dohna-s10-002":["[One can also divide the horizon of human cognition into:",["The bracket is left open, as in the source."]],"dohna-s10-003":["1. horizon of healthy reason,",["gesunde Vernunft = healthy reason."]],"dohna-s10-004":["2. of the sciences.]",["Wissenschaften = sciences."]],"dohna-s10-005":["We can to a certain extent say, the beginning of the world is there where the art of writing arose. There is no information at all that we ought not to know, for in general it is our duty to know everything that we can only know. But there certainly is information that we can dispense with and therefore may not know [that is outside our horizon according to the above explanation] (as e.g. the physician can dispense with Hebrew, thus may not know it).",["Kenntnisse = \"information\"; wissen = \"know\"; Welt = world.","Erklärung = \"explanation\" (the glossary also gives \"definition\"). \"dürfen\" with \"nicht\" = \"may not\", which can mean either \"are not permitted to\" or \"must not\"; left as ambiguous as the German.","\"gewissermaßen\" = \"to a certain extent\"."]],"dohna-s10-006":["The manner in which information is taught at universities one calls factory-like, because here, namely, there are many who work in common on one end.",["Manier = manner; Zweck = end; gemeinschaftlich = \"in common\"; Kenntnisse = \"information\"."]],"dohna-s10-007":["Magnitude of cognition. What matters here is not the multitude but the degree. [*Non multa, sed multum.* The external extensive magnitude of cognition indicates the general spread of it among many human beings. — This is the present state enlightenment — the degree of culture — which according to the talents of the subject makes the human being capable for his ends as human being and as citizen.]",["Menge = multitude; Grad = degree; Größe = magnitude; Aufklärung = enlightenment.","\"Zustand Aufklärung\" has no \"of\" in the German; kept as it stands. \"Bürger\" = citizen."]],"dohna-s10-008":["Practical rules with regard to the horizon of human cognition. One must not determine the horizon of one's cognition too early, for one does not know for what one will have particular talent in the future. Moreover the inclination can change. [First learn quite a lot, in order then to see for what one has the most desire — yet not desultorily — from one thing to another. — All studying according to rules cultivates the understanding — hence grammar is useful also to the future artist. One usually learns only when it is too late to act in accordance with one's talents, inclinations and circumstances, to know them at all.] But if one has once determinately adopted the horizon of one's cognition, one must also not change it lightly. One must not measure the horizon of others by one's own. Mainly one must not at once pass off as useless what seems dispensable to us in a certain aim. If one had always wanted to grasp the utility with one's hands, one would not have risen high in any science. Can there be things of which one can say, we do not want to know them? — praiseworthy ignorance? Ignorance is never praiseworthy. [So there is indeed an *ignoratio inculpabilis* (blameless), but never an *ignorantia laudabilis*.] Not it, but the information about one's ignorance is praiseworthy [and it requires much information]. Whoever comes to this makes the step from the field of science to the field of wisdom. Those who are puffed up by conceit usually call others idiots. [So one calls human beings who, even if they have much information — yet not science, ignorant, whoever does not have that either. In general he is called ignorant who is unknowing with regard to the science that he professes. — Idiot is against the polymath what the *Laicus* is against the *Clericus*, or what *Literatus* is, one who can read and write — then much!] Polyhistory is historical much-knowing, rational much-knowing would be polymathy [to which philosophy and mathematics belonged] and (both taken together) all-knowing (if it could be thought) would be called pansophy.",["OCR: \"Noch mein'\" is doubtful (perhaps \"Noch mehr\" or \"Auch\"); rendered \"Moreover\". \"desxütorisch\" read as \"desultorisch\"; stray \"f\" after \"Verhältnissen\" omitted; \"inculpabüis\" and \"laudabihs\" read as *inculpabilis*, *laudabilis*. The source's \"ignoratio\" (against \"ignorantia\") is kept as printed.","\"bestimmt ... angenommen\" (\"Hat man aber einmal bestimmt den Horizont seiner Erkenntnis angenommen\"): \"bestimmt\" may be adverb (\"determinately\") or participle (\"has determined, adopted\"); the first reading is rendered.","The sentence \"So nennt man Menschen ... Ignorant, wer auch jene nicht hat\" is grammatically loose in the German; kept loose. \"Idiot ist das gegen Vielwisser, was ...\" and \"damals viel\" kept literal.","Kenntnis = information; Wissenschaft = science; Unwissenheit = ignorance; Vielwisserei / Allwisserei = \"much-knowing\" / \"all-knowing\"; Polyhistorie, Polymathie, Pansophie = polyhistory, polymathy, pansophy; profitiert = \"professes\".","\"in gewisser Absicht\" = \"in a certain aim\" (Absicht = aim); \"mit Händen greifen\" = \"grasp with one's hands\"."]],"dohna-s10-009":["The polyhistor must 1. be a humanist, i.e. he must be a connoisseur of the ancients and of the fine arts (poetic art, oratorical art).",["Kenner = \"connoisseur\" (cognate of kennen, \"know\"); \"Dicht-, Redekunst\" = \"poetic art, oratorical art\"; \"Polyhistor\" and \"Humanist\" kept as in the German."]],"dohna-s10-010":["[The *humaniora* are preeminently for the acquaintance with taste and for participation in the sensations of others. This latter, namely the faculty of communicating one's sensations (items of acquaintance t and feelings) to others, makes precisely what is characteristic of the humanist. — The humanist is so by his cognition being science, scholarship. — ]",["Kenntnis = acquaintance (here and in 'Kenntnisse'); Erkenntnis = cognition; Wissenschaft = science; Vermögen = faculty; Empfindung = sensation; Gefühl = feeling.","'Kenntnisse t' : the stray 't' is an OCR residue (perhaps an apparatus mark); left as it stands.","'Humaniora' is Latin in the source; italicized as a foreign word."]],"dohna-s10-011":["The counterpart to the humanist is the belletrist. But he does not go, as the former does, to the self-standing beautiful, but only to the changeable according to the variety of taste, which conforms itself to fashion.",["'Pendant' rendered 'counterpart'; 'selbständig' rendered 'self-standing' (also 'self-standing' in s10-014 context: 'selbständige')."]],"dohna-s10-012":["[The belletrist is only the ape of the humanist — a mere dilettante. One can resolve only on the one or the other. In order not to become a pedant, the belletrist pursues merely gallant *studia*. The gallant manner is nothing other than popularity with taste. Scholastic perfection (conformity to the rules (to the object)) always remains the first requisite of cognition, just as with language grammatical correctness is demanded first — then elegance. Popularity (conformity to the subject) must be a condescension of a cognition, insofar as it already has scholastic perfection. Whoever holds it | scholastic perfection | (in social intercourse) to be the only one is called a pedant: he can communicate himself always only to someone who has been through the school. Gallantry is just as much a fault.]",["Vollkommenheit = perfection; Erkenntnis = cognition; Objekt = object; 'Requisit' kept as 'requisite'.","The parenthesis '(Angemessenheit zu den Regeln (zum Objekt)' is not closed in the source; I close it after '(zum Objekt)' as the sense requires—the German leaves the second bracket unclosed.","The signs | ... | are the Academy apparatus and are carried over as they stand."]],"dohna-s10-013":["The polyhistor must be",["Fragment; breaks off. The '1.' presumably preceding is not in this passage."]],"dohna-s10-014":["2ndly a linguist, a connoisseur of the ancient languages, because here what matters is self-standing, lasting models.",["'Kenner' = connoisseur (cf. kennen = know; be acquainted with); 'selbständig' = self-standing."]],"dohna-s10-015":["3. The litteratorist, connoisseur of books (Magliabecchi, a famous bookkeeper at Florence, had a horrendous memory). Philology is the sum total of all instruments of scholarship, the humanist the connoisseur of the formation of taste. Humanity is always at the same time popular. Only dead languages can become models of taste, living ones not, for these change all too often, and words whose meaning was noble have a low meaning. In order to have a lasting taste, one must study the ancients. If the ancients should be lost, one must fear the breaking in of barbarism. In Hindostan there is also a language formerly customary there and at present likewise dead [whose origin is unknown] (Sanskrit), which is very perfect, but is spoken only by a few Brahmins. The belletrist one calls the dilettante, he finds pleasure and taste in the fine arts.",["Inbegriff = sum total; Bedeutung = meaning; Geschmack = taste.","'Buchführer' is the German; it ordinarily means 'bookkeeper' (or perhaps 'book dealer'); the original may be an error for 'Bücherführer'/'Buchhändler'. Kept literal.","'Litteratorist Bücherkenner' has no punctuation between the terms in the source; kept so with a comma only for English."]],"dohna-s10-016":["We can divide the talent of the human being, in that it is inclined:",["'indem' rendered 'in that'."]],"dohna-s10-017":["1. to science or",["Wissenschaft = science."]],"dohna-s10-018":["2. to art [all culture rests on these two pieces].",["'Stücke' rendered 'pieces'."]],"dohna-s10-019":["This | art | one can now divide:",["The signs | ... | are the Academy apparatus."]],"dohna-s10-020":["a) into free arts, *artes liberales* or *ingenuae* [free art is the skill that pleases immediately],",["Latin kept in italics although printed in roman type in the source."]],"dohna-s10-021":["b) into mercenary arts, *artes mercenales serviles*, which can please only as means to some self-interested end [mercenary arts please always only mediately, for other intentions, likewise all sciences. Almost always they please only on account of their usefulness etc. Mathematics pleases, to be sure, immediately, but to that extent one could also count it among the free arts. A mercenary art lets itself be commissioned — free art not — their products | we | cannot make according to rules as those.]",["'Lohnkünste' = 'mercenary arts' (literally 'wage-arts'); Mittel = means; Zweck = end; Absicht = intention.","'Wissenschaften' = sciences. The sentence 'ihre Produkte können | wir | nicht nach Regeln machen wie jene' is rendered as it stands; 'jene' ('those') is ambiguous in reference."]],"dohna-s10-022":["This division of talent is very delicate. The beautiful is that which pleases through itself. In this one does not ask at all about utility. If one already asks about utility in an art, then it is bread-art.",["'Brotkunst' = 'bread-art'."]],"dohna-s10-023":["As concerns the sciences, these can be divided into:",["Wissenschaft = science."]],"dohna-s10-024":["1. historical and",[]],"dohna-s10-025":["2. sciences of reason.",["Vernunft = reason."]],"dohna-s10-026":["The sum total of the historical sciences is scholarship. The sum total of the sciences of reason has no particular name, for their parts philosophy and mathematics are all too greatly different. The sum total of all sciences is called polyhistory, of which we have already spoken above.",["Inbegriff = sum total; besonder = particular; 'Philosophie' rendered 'philosophy' (not Weltweisheit); Gelehrsamkeit = scholarship."]],"dohna-s10-027":["The author now speaks of the pedant. He is 1. school-pedant, 2. courtesy-pedant. E.g. someone speaks like a book, always under the constraint of rules. [One finds the like at court, e.g. chamberlains.]",["'Höflichkeitspedant' rendered literally as 'courtesy-pedant'."]],"dohna-s10-028":["On pedantry",[]],"dohna-s10-029":["[p. 24. § 65.]",[]],"dohna-s10-030":["By a pedant we do not understand one who is ignorant (hence the author's definition is incorrect). But he is nevertheless laughed at. Whence does that come? The word pedant comes from the Italian and properly signifies *magister pedanius*. This one was now merely a schoolman, not at all for the world, because he did not come to the table of the masters, but was for the most part with the servants.",["Erklärung = definition (explanation/definition/declaration); 'heißt' rendered 'signifies' for 'heißt eigentlich'; literally 'is called'.","'pedanius' is as printed (OCR/text may intend 'pedagogus' or similar); left as it stands.","Welt = world; 'Herrschaft' rendered 'masters' (could also be 'the lord and lady')."]],"dohna-s10-031":["[Pedantry is painstakingness in the form. No nation inclines so much to it as the German. Pedantry takes place in all faculties, above all things in the military and among jurists. — Charlatan, the ostentator of a wretched cognition.]",["Peinlichkeit = painstakingness (a fixed rendering through s10-032–033); Form = form; Erkenntnis = cognition.","'Fächern' rendered 'faculties' (could also be 'fields')."]],"dohna-s10-032":["Pedantry is the useless exactness (painstakingness) in formalities. The purposive exactness (punctiliousness) in formalities is thoroughness. Thus both have some similarity. In many cases there is much that is arbitrary in the formalities. They are useful in a scholastic intention, but not in a popular one, i.e. in social intercourse. Painstakingness and punctiliousness are thus well to be distinguished.",["Absicht = intention (glossary gives 'aim; intention'); Gründlichkeit = thoroughness; Pünktlichkeit = punctiliousness; 'zweckmäßig' = purposive.","'unnützliche' rendered 'useless'."]],"dohna-s10-033":["That the transition from punctiliousness to painstakingness is very easy is indeed true. One cannot possibly learn everything according to formalities, e.g. to write a letter. Hence one sees the great difference between the wooden letter of the pupil and the flowing one of his sister [although this one may not be orthographic at all]. Thus the muses nevertheless need the society of the graces, and these consist in this, that the school dust, the scholastic, be wiped away from cognition and it become popular, generally comprehensible. [Yet for that reason the ground of cognition must be school-proper.]",["OCR 'Avegge wischt' corrected to 'wegwischt' ('wipes away'); the grammar ('wischt ... weg' as subjunctive 'be wiped away') is as in the German, which reads awkwardly; the subject of 'wischt' is unclear.","Grund = ground; 'schulgerecht' = 'school-proper'; 'allgemeinfaßlich' = 'generally comprehensible'."]],"dohna-s10-034":["Pedantry is peculiar not to the learned estate alone but to all estates. A *Pinsel* is one who can draw nothing from himself but can merely imitate, and, if he wants to bring forward something of his cognitions, cannot depart at all from the school formulas. [D. *umbraticus* — the designation of a pedant. — Gallantry signifies a beautifying of that which for itself is no beautiful object.] Charlatanry is boasting and ostentation. Belletrism belongs to the fashionable study, which changes so very often. The more a science is spread out (not) according to content but according to space, the shallower it becomes.",["'Pinsel' (literally 'brush'; a simpleton) left in German italics; 'D.' presumably an abbreviation for *Doctor* or 'Dominus', kept as it stands.","OCR 'Baume' corrected to 'Raume' ('space'); 'modemäßigen' corrected to 'modemäßigen' read as 'modemäßigen' = 'fashionable'. The '(nicht)' is as printed, apparently an editorial insertion.","Gegenstand = object; Inhalt = content; Wissenschaft = science; bedeuten = signify."]],"dohna-s11-001":["On the magnitude of learned cognition",["Größe = magnitude; Erkenntnis = cognition."]],"dohna-s11-002":["Extensiveness is the extensive magnitude, which consists in the multiplicity, in the manifold [to know of many things]. The intensive magnitude goes to the degree [to know much of one thing. — *Non multa, sed multum.* The magnitude of the domain weakens the degree — brings forth shallowness. On the whole it has more utility if a few have deep cognition in the science — who treat it with thoroughness — than if they are generally dispersed.]",["Vielheit = multiplicity; Mannigfaltige = manifold; Grad = degree; Umfang = domain; Gründlichkeit = thoroughness.","'Weitläufigkeit' rendered 'extensiveness' (also 'prolixity'); 'sie' in the last clause is ambiguous (the sciences/the cognitions/the few); kept ambiguous."]],"dohna-s11-003":["Mathematics is e.g. extensive magnitude. One must restrict the multiplicity of cognitions in order to bring forth a unity.",["Einheit = unity; 'restringieren' rendered 'restrict'."]],"dohna-s11-004":["The author always speaks of a learned cognition. But this expression is improper. The cognition can be called scientific when it is scientific and systematic. To this there now belongs a chief principle, just as one assumes, e.g. in history, civil constitution as the chief principle. History can thus also be regarded as science, if it is brought to a system, which one derives from a chief principle.",["'scientifisch' (kept as 'scientific') and 'wissenschaftlich' (also 'scientific') are two German words for one English word; Ausdruck = expression; Prinzip rendered 'principle' (Hauptprinzip = chief principle).","'was man von einem Hauptprinzip ableitet' kept literal; 'was' is ambiguous (relative to 'System')."]],"dohna-s11-005":["The whole doctrine of elements is nothing further than common cognition, and so the whole of logic ought really to be treated, so that the common man can understand it without other cognitions. With regard to the objects a cognition can be extensively large, with regard to the objects — their plurality and manifold application, as in mathematics. Popularity too does not go to how much <(*quantum*)>, but to how many kinds <*quot*> we know. This serves to use it in society. In an age enlightenment can be extensively large, if namely it extends to all, but intensively small, because namely no one has quite much of it, but only all have something little, they are so-called *bureaux d'esprit*, like e.g. Madame Geoffrin, who was famous for that. Just as when one beats money very flat, it does indeed take up a large space, but everywhere it is thin and can easily be damaged. The extensive magnitude of enlightenment one can also call the subjective, in that it extends to all subjects, but the intensive the objective, which goes to the degree.",["Elementarlehre = 'doctrine of elements'; gemein = 'common' (gemeine Erkenntnis, gemeine Mann), distinct from allgemein = 'universal'.","Vielheit (not in the glossary) rendered 'plurality'; vielfältigen rendered 'manifold' (adjective), not Mannigfaltige.","OCR: 'em' read as 'ein'; the angle brackets around quantum and quot are damaged in the OCR ('<(quantum),' and '<quot>') and have been regularized.","'Ansehung der Objekte' is repeated in the German; 'Erkenntnis' is neuter here (ein Erkenntnis)."]],"dohna-s11-006":["The author now speaks again: On the fruitfulness and importance of a cognition",["Wichtigkeit = 'importance'; Fruchtbarkeit = 'fruitfulness'."]],"dohna-s11-007":["*Fecunditas et dignitas*. Both go to the intensively large. The former rests on the multitude (of the sequels), the latter on the [importance of the sequels]. We also have cognitions that are fruitful and important at the same time. [Everything is logically important that cultivates the understanding. Hence mathematics is so in a quite excellent degree, on account of its clarity, distinctness, certainty. — Distinctness: where the marks of the concept are clear. If it is the marks of the marks that are so, Wolff called it completeness — meanwhile the partial concepts can still have others under them and here there is therefore | no | boundary up to the simple representations.]",["Folgen rendered 'sequels'; Menge = 'multitude'; Wichtigkeit = 'importance'; Vollständigkeit = 'completeness'; Grenze = 'boundary'.","The brackets and the signs | | are carried over as they stand.","'Wenn die Merkmale von den Merkmalen es sind': 'es' is left as in the German ('so')."]],"dohna-s11-008":["We now come to a matter that already belongs to logic proper, namely",["Materie = 'matter'. The sentence breaks off and is continued in the next passage."]],"dohna-s11-009":["On the truth of learned cognition",["gelehrt = 'learned'."]],"dohna-s11-010":["Logic begins with the clear concepts, but gives no rules for how they are made clear. For the consciousness of the representations logic presupposes. Thus logic really teaches only of the distinctness of concepts.",["OCR 'Idaren' read as 'klaren'.","'das Bewußtsein der Vorstellungen setzt die Logik voraus': both nouns can be nominative or accusative, so the German does not itself mark subject and object; the English word order is chosen to keep this open (logic presupposes consciousness / consciousness presupposes logic)."]],"dohna-s11-011":["We have now spoken of the quantity and quality of cognition and now come to the",["Breaks off; continued in the next passage."]],"dohna-s11-012":["III. Relation of the representations in our cognition. The agreement of the representations with their object is called truth. — We now want to have in this chapter a criterion of truth that holds in all cognitions without distinction. [The skeptics said: All judgments would be made at random — SiaÄXr|Ar), always what is grounded on the ground and this again on what is grounded, thus in a circle.]",["The Greek word after the dash is too garbled in the OCR to be restored; it is left as printed (perhaps a form of the Greek word for 'circular' / diallelus).","Übereinstimmung = 'agreement'; Kriterium = 'criterion'; aufs Geratewohl = 'at random'."]],"dohna-s11-013":["[There is, however, a universal formal criterion of truth, the agreement of the same with itself — a material one, agreement with the object, cannot be universally possible — for it would have to (if it were universal) also obtain when I abstract from all matter, — and the agreement of my cognition of the object with my cognition of the object would be <a> tautological criterion. This the skeptics therefore overturned, that one they could not attack.]",["OCR 'WTahrheit' read as 'Wahrheit'; '<ein)' regularized to '<ein>'.","'derselben' (of the same) is left without a resolved antecedent; 'Dies' and 'jenes' are left as the bare demonstratives 'this' and 'that one'.","allgemein = 'universal'; Materie = 'matter'."]],"dohna-s11-014":["The universal criterion of truth will really always be only formal, abstract from all content and difference of cognition. It will therefore never tell me whether my cognition agrees with the object, but whether my cognition agrees with myself. The universal criterion of truth will not be an *Organon*, but only the *conditio sine qua non*, the unavoidable condition of truth. This is that we are in agreement with ourselves in thinking. How could a cognition that is <at odds with itself agree with the object?> The criterion must consist in the rule with which alone the understanding in thinking agrees with itself. It is not sufficiently material, but it is nevertheless unavoidably necessary. A material criterion of truth cannot possibly be universal and hold for all objects. One can well have material criteria of truth, but they cannot be universal. It would be those that we have of the constitution of the object with regard to the senses.",["The OCR reads '{sich selbst uneins ist, ... stimmen ?)'; the brackets are restored as angle brackets, which is doubtful. A stray 'f.' after 'allgemein sein' is omitted as a probable footnote or apparatus mark.","hinreichend (not in the glossary) rendered 'sufficiently'; unumgänglich = 'unavoidable'; Beschaffenheit = 'constitution'.","'mit mir selbst' is literally 'with me myself'; 'with myself' is used. Organon is Greek, italicized as foreign."]],"dohna-s11-015":["[Hour]",["'Stunde' (a lecture hour; the mark of a new session) rendered literally."]],"dohna-s11-016":["Through intuition the object is given and through concepts thought. The universal criterion of truth is merely negative. The skeptics one can divide into Pyrrhonists and Academics, the latter of whom drove it furthest. The principle of contradiction is the first formal criterion of truth. For truth it is not enough that a judgment be possible — truth one could call logical actuality. If one says: *posito* a triangle has 6 sides, this is a problematic judgment; if one says: a hexagon has 6 sides, it is a proposition.",["Satz des Widerspruchs rendered 'principle of contradiction'; the last 'Satz' is rendered 'proposition' (glossary: Satz = proposition; sentence; principle).","Wirklichkeit = 'actuality'; Anschauung = 'intuition'."]],"dohna-s11-017":["The first criterion of truth (after the principle of contradiction)",["Fragment; continued in the next passage."]],"dohna-s11-018":["is the principle of problematic judgments — possibility. The",["Breaks off at 'Das'; continued in the next passage."]],"dohna-s11-019":["2. is the principle of assertoric judgments, in which the logical actuality [— truth].",["The sentence is left as it stands (no verb after 'worin')."]],"dohna-s11-020":["The 2nd principle is the principle of sufficient ground.",["'Prinzip' and 'Satz' both appear and are both rendered 'principle'; zureichend = 'sufficient', Grund = 'ground'."]],"dohna-s11-021":["[Cause is that which lies at the ground of all appearances.]",["Ursache = 'cause'; Erscheinungen = 'appearances'."]],"dohna-s11-022":["3. Every cognition has some sequel and if only one sequel is false, then the cognition too is false. If all sequels are true, then the cognition is true — but who can know all sequels? For that omniscience is required. One can therefore infer from the sequels to the grounds. From the falsity of the sequels one can infer only to the falsity of the ground, but from this to the truth of the opposite. [ — 106]",["Folge = 'sequel'; schließen = 'infer'; wissen = 'know'; Gegenteil = 'opposite'.","'[ — 106]' (apparently a reference to a section number) is carried over as printed."]],"dohna-s11-023":["[ad § 109.] A false judgment is always an error. But in this case one must prove that it conflicts with the criterion of truth. For error it is required that we take a false judgment to be true. We are unfortunately filled with errors, but in order that we forestall them, we must first know whence they have arisen.",["'für wahr halten' rendered 'take to be true' (cf. Fürwahrhalten); Irrtum = 'error'; Beweis = 'proof' (beweisen = 'prove')."]],"dohna-s11-024":["[Truth and error are impossibly contained in intuitions — but rather in judgments. Hence in an academy of sciences more errors than in a village full of peasants, because there more is judged — whoever never judges will also never err.]",["'sind in Anschauungen unmöglich enthalten — sondern in den Urteilen': the syntax is kept as is ('unmöglich' may be read as 'cannot be' or 'impossibly'); 'Daher in einer Akademie ...' has no verb in the German."]],"dohna-s11-025":["Neither truth nor error rests in the concepts of our understanding, but only in its <judgments>. Judgment is an action of the understanding. Truth, however, according to its formal criterion is the agreement of a judgment with the laws of the understanding. Error, however, is a deviation of the judgment from the laws of the understanding. — [If I therefore isolate the understanding, so that I take away the sensibility, then it could not err (for no force can do an effect without according to certain laws) — only when I take an influence of sensibility for an *actus* of the understanding, then this is error. All our errors are steerings of the judgments into the diagonal — into the transverse (the word perhaps comes from the English square — Quadrat) — ].",["The OCR reads '(Urteilen)'; restored as '<judgments>', which is doubtful.","'ohne nach gewissen Gesetzen' is elliptic in the German (no verb) and is kept so.","Kraft = 'force'; Wirkung = 'effect'; Handlung = 'action'; Sinnlichkeit = 'sensibility'; Lenkungen = 'steerings'; in die Quere = 'into the transverse'."]],"dohna-s11-026":["How is it possible that a force departs from its own laws? — The limitations of the human understanding are not the ground of errors. They are indeed grounds of a great deficiency of our cognition (— of ignorance (which we cognize only after much science of reason)), but not of contradiction. Since it is now nothing negative, and the understanding alone by itself cannot err, it must be something positive — sensibility. The *oppositum* of the understanding, however, does not judge at all. Now, however, we have no other source. We see, therefore, that it happens through combination of the understanding and sensibility [we call thus the subjective of our representations]. Insofar as this ground lies in sensibility, we call it illusion. This is usually explained: the subjective, which is falsely taken for something objective. [The ground of all error a subjective ground of our judgment, which we regard as an objective one. A human being readily believes what he wishes. Wish a subjective | ground, | but for belief one must have an objective one.]",["OCR: 'Sehern' read as Schein (illusion); the stray '<' before '— der Unwissenheit' read as an opening parenthesis. The bracket and | marks are kept as they stand, although the job text also asks that editorial apparatus be left out.","Kraft: 'force' by the glossary ('power' is the alternative); Irrtum = error; Schranken = limitations; Verbindung = combination; glauben/Glauben = believe/belief; ansehn = regard (as).","The bracket after 'Sinnlichkeit' ('so nennen wir das Subjektive...') is syntactically loose in the German, and the English keeps it so."]],"dohna-s11-027":["Sensibility also has its own laws. Combined in a judgment they can indeed agree with one another. Solely in the combination of two dissimilar determining grounds. The matter of errors is therefore neither true nor false.",["Bestimmungsgrund = determining ground; ungleichartig = dissimilar. The second sentence has no explicit subject for 'sie', and the third has no verb. Both are left as they stand."]],"dohna-s11-028":["[*Porismata*: All errors are due to fault — for all judgments are voluntary and only through judgments are errors possible.",["verschuldet = 'due to fault' (literally incurred through guilt); willkürlich = 'voluntary' (could also be 'arbitrary'). The opening bracket is never closed in this passage."]],"dohna-s11-029":["No error is a wholly false judgment. The judgment can be correct in form.]",["Form = form; richtig = correct."]],"dohna-s12-001":["[Hour, Friday, May 18]",["'Stunde' rendered 'Hour'."]],"dohna-s12-002":["Every error must be explained. We have inner marks of truth, which are grounded in our understanding, and secondly outer marks. To the former belong the proposition of contradiction (the logical criterion of possibility (problematic)) and the second proposition, that the judgment as proposition must always be grounded (the logical criterion of actuality (of assertoric judgments), principle of sufficient ground). The third (the logical criterion of truth (necessity)), that if one cannot cognize the truth of a proposition from the grounds, it still proceeds from the sequels. [One cannot judge here by a sense of truth — as little as duty by moral feeling. One can never judge through the senses, only through the understanding. — ]",["OCR: braces '{' read as parentheses; 'Wirkhchkeit' read as Wirklichkeit; 'erstem' read as 'ersten'.","Folgen = 'sequels' (glossary Folge); Folgerung would be 'consequence'. Sinn = sense; sittliches Gefühl = moral feeling; Merkmal = mark; Kriterium = criterion; Grund = ground; zureichend = sufficient.","'Der 3te ... daß' has no verb of its own, and it is left so. 'Wirklichkeit' is rendered 'actuality'."]],"dohna-s12-003":["The outer criteria (marks) of truth consist in the agreement of the judgments of others with our own. This is properly no logical, but a psychological criterion. Merely by comparing one's judgment with that of another does one cognize whether the determining ground of our judgment is subjective or objective: the latter is universally valid, | the | former not. [(Many exceptions to a rule indicate its deficiency.)]",["OCR: 'kern' read as 'kein'. The German 'man ... erkennt' is rendered 'one cognizes'. allgemein gültig = universally valid; Mangelhaftigkeit = deficiency. The | marks are kept."]],"dohna-s12-004":["Sensibility is called in logic the subjective of our cognition.",["OCR: stray 'f' before 'Erkenntnis' dropped."]],"dohna-s12-005":["[*Principium rationati*: *nihil sine ratione*, everything must have its ground. This is the criterion from the sequels. It is negative and certain. If the sequels are false, then the ground too is certainly so. One cannot infer thus conversely. If the sequels are true, etc. For there can be more grounds. *Principium rationis*: every proposition is itself ground, has its sequels.]",["Folgen = 'sequels' throughout; schließen = infer; Grund = ground. The Latin terms are the lecturer's own."]],"dohna-s12-006":["[It is an impulse of nature, a calling, to communicate one's cognitions to others — hence the freedom of communication a right of humanity (only then, perhaps, if it could be entirely contrary to the interest of the state, might a prohibition on the part of the authorities take place.)",["Beruf = 'calling' (to be distinguished from Bestimmung = vocation). The opening bracket is not closed in this passage, and the parenthesis is closed as in the German."]],"dohna-s12-007":["Possession of the power of judgment is the greatest gift of nature. A deficiency in it can occur with an otherwise good understanding.]",["Urteilskraft = power of judgment; Mangel = deficiency."]],"dohna-s12-008":["In a certain way one can say of all cognitions that one understands them properly only when one can make them oneself, i.e., not merely copy but derive from oneself.",["verstehen = understand; 'recht' rendered 'properly'."]],"dohna-s12-009":["No judgment would be found that would be wholly false. This cannot take place so long as our judgments are derived from the understanding.",["The subjunctive 'wäre zu finden' / 'wäre' is kept as a conditional."]],"dohna-s12-010":["One cannot properly convince anyone of the opposite of his opinion if one does not tell him how he came to it, from his particular standpoint, so that his judgment is thus not objective but merely subjective. Thus, according to our subjective judgment, our final end would be happiness, that pleases us, that is what we want. But if we go to what we ought, to the objective, then morality, duty must take up this place. Through the method just indicated one gains in critical faculty of the understanding. Thus it is said that in a large book there would be great errors, and this is very natural.",["OCR: 'Verstandes vermögen' read as one word, Verstandesvermögen. überzeugen = convince; Meinung = opinion; besonder = particular; Zweck = end; Vermögen = faculty.","The subjunctives 'wäre', 'wären' are kept as 'would be'. The sentence 'So wäre ...' is obscure as to its logic, and the English follows the German."]],"dohna-s12-011":["Now follows a distinction between",["Fragment, continued by the next passages."]],"dohna-s12-012":["1. the partial (*tolerabiliter* true, e.g. in measurements) [since, namely, the assumption of the partially true as the totally true does not contradict the end — although this comprises a certain incorrectness in itself. — ] and",["OCR: 'Hch' read as 'lich' (nämlich); 'partial Wahren' kept as printed. Latin *tolerabiliter* italicized."]],"dohna-s12-013":["2. the total truth.",[]],"dohna-s12-014":["Someone can tell the truth, but not the whole truth. But one must see especially to it that it never runs contrary to the ends of cognition.",["'sie' (it) is left without a noun, as in the German, and could refer to the truth or to the telling of it. Zwecke = ends."]],"dohna-s12-015":["Now the author speaks of the",["Fragment, continued in the next passage. 'Autor' is probably Meier."]],"dohna-s12-016":["precise and coarse cognition. The latter does not yet indicate that it contains something false, but only that it is not wholly true. Every cognition is either *cognitio vaga* or *cognitio determinata*. Thus, e.g., *medium tenuere beati*, what is good and what is too much and too little, such people do not know. This is a *regula vaga*, which does not determine at all, merely tautological wisdom. Be wise, make yourself perfect! Now, in what does that consist? One takes it this way, the other that. Determinate rules too can be *regulae late* and *stricte determinatae*. Of the first kind are many definitions. A cognition is called exact if no latitude for error is contained in it. A cognition is called precise with regard to the object, is called subtle with regard to the subject, for which much attention belongs. Whoever, contrary to purpose, makes the small into the use of his cognition is called a micrologist. If something is practical, one cannot blame subtlety.",["OCR: 'genauen und groben Erkenntnis' joins the preceding passage; '»Subjekts' read as 'Subjekts'. genau = precise, exakt = exact (two German words, distinguished here); wissen = know; Erkenntnis = cognition.","'Erklärung/Definition': 'Definitionen' = definitions. The Latin phrases and the Horatian-style tag *medium tenuere beati* are kept in Latin. 'unzweckmäßig' rendered 'contrary to purpose'. The antecedent of 'Letzteres' and the reading 'tun zu' in the next passage are as in the German."]],"dohna-s12-017":["[Hour, the 21st of May]",[]],"dohna-s12-018":["Casuistry consists in a multitude of useless subtleties, where one has thought up cases that do not occur at all in common life. There were ages in which subtleties and micrology counted for an astonishing amount.",["Menge = multitude; Subtilitäten = subtleties; Mikrologie = micrology."]],"dohna-s12-019":["There is a certain pettiness of mind, which one does not always remark. The people, namely, who possess it can never make application from one piece of cognition to the whole system of it. This is a great touchstone, from which one can infer at once the mind of the human being. There are differences between head and mind. Head, the faculty of the understanding, but mind the faculty of principles.",["Kleingeisterei = 'pettiness of mind' (Geist = mind; Gemüt would also be 'mind'). Kopf = head; Probierstein = touchstone; Prinzipien = principles."]],"dohna-s12-020":["[Aesthetically true is that which agrees with the universal illusion, e.g. the rising and setting of the sun — it is thus very different from logical truth. From error one does not go straight to truth, but first through the consciousness of ignorance.]",["Schein = illusion; Bewußtsein = consciousness; Unwissenheit = ignorance. 'Auf- und Niedergehn' rendered literally as 'rising and setting' (going up and down)."]],"dohna-s12-021":["An acquaintance precise with regard to the object (*exacta*) is opposed to the crude (*rudi*). *Cognitio vaga*, do to no thing too much, to none too little, that is yet almost nothing said. One calls the Latitudinarians, who believe that the lawgiver commands too strictly, that between his laws and their transgression there is still a latitude. In this there is, to be sure, nothing firm, and we must always take pains that our cognition be strict, precisely determined. This is an advantage of geometry.",["Kenntnis = 'acquaintance'; the Latin *exacta*, *rudi* are the lecturer's own and kept in Latin. 'tun zu' is as in the German, and the sentence is elliptical; 'die da glauben' follows the transcript. Spielraum = latitude; Vorzug = advantage (could be 'preeminence'). Gesetz = law."]],"dohna-s12-022":["Then one also has a *cognitio crassa* — over Pausch and Bogen, an estimate of the whole, without particular assessment of the individual. [The crass cognition not only has no *exactitudo*, but it also corrupts human beings, in that it gives occasion to errors.]",["'über Pausch und Bogen' is an idiom (roughly 'in the lump') and is left in German. Beurteilung = 'assessment' here, to keep 'judgment' for Urteil. Irrtümer = errors."]],"dohna-s12-023":["Truth can be used substantively and adjectivally, e.g., when we think truth in general or the truth of a proposition. A cognition determined with regard to the object is called truth. This rational cognition is at all times apodictic, because we are conscious not only of its truth but also of its necessity. An apodictic cognition is either through concepts or through construction of concepts. In the first case it is dogmatic, in the latter mathematical. An apodictically certain cognition from concepts is called *Dogma*, an apodictically certain cognition from the construction of concepts *mathema*. —",["\"Substantive und adjektive\" is rendered \"substantively and adjectivally\"; \"Erkenntnis\" is \"cognition\" throughout, and \"Rationalerkenntnis\" is rendered \"rational cognition\".","\"ein apodiktisch gewisses Erkenntnis\" has neuter gender in the German; I render it \"cognition\" as elsewhere.","\"mathema\" is printed as a truncated word in the source (probably for *mathema*, or *mathemata*); I leave it as it stands, with no completion."]],"dohna-s12-024":["[An apodictic proposition: Everything is contingent — (has a cause) — 2-4 = 4. This is intuition, the former only concept. Both are objectively equally certain, but subjectively there is great difference. The mathematician can rest in his work, intuition remains for him — this is not the case with the philosopher, hence his work is far more wearying.]",["\"2-4 = 4\" is as printed in the OCR; the reading is evidently corrupt (perhaps \"2+2 = 4\") and I have not emended it.","\"Anschauung\" = intuition; \"Begriff\" = concept. The square brackets are as in the source.","\"zufällig\" = contingent."]],"dohna-s12-025":["The propositions of morals and metaphysics are all *dogmata*. —",["\"Dogmata\" is Latin in the source (plural of *dogma*); \"Sätze\" = propositions."]],"dohna-s12-026":["Because physics is only application of those cognitions, it has an apodictic certainty. System is a whole (of dogmatic truths) from principles. [System differs from aggregate in that the latter is the whole to which the parts precede.]",["The OCR \"{dogmatischer Wahrheiten)\" is read as \"(dogmatischer Wahrheiten)\".","\"Prinzipien\" = principles (the glossary's \"Grundsatz\" is not used here).","\"dem die Teile vorhergehen\" is kept literally; the German could be read as the parts preceding the whole."]],"dohna-s12-027":["The principle of history is time — chronology.",["\"Prinzip\" = principle; \"Zeit\" = time."]],"dohna-s13-001":["[Hour, the 22nd of May]",["\"Stunde\" is rendered literally as \"hour\" (i.e., the lecture hour)."]],"dohna-s13-002":["In itself one cannot say of any error that it is unavoidable, but one surely can say this of ignorance. For we do not need to judge about everything, and if we do, it may also be done only problematically. [There is no *error invincibilis* — but in practice errors hard to avoid — mark: the incompatibility with the judgment of others.] An unavoidable error always presupposes the necessity of the judgment. [For if the judgment were not necessary, one could easily avoid the error by not judging at all. — ]",["OCR \"kernen\" read as \"keinen\".","\"Merkmal\" = mark; \"urteilen\"/\"Urteil\" = judge/judgment; \"Unwissenheit\" = ignorance.","\"Dies ... läßt sich sagen\" is rendered with \"this\" as in the German; \"es ja tun\" is kept as \"if we do\"."]],"dohna-s13-003":["He who accepts an obviously absurd judgment acts tastelessly. [What even the *sensus communis* already recognizes as such — which does not even have the illusion on its side. — (Logically considered no error is dangerous — here we do not look to the matter) — if one once declares someone's judgments | for | tasteless, thus denies him understanding thereby, then no discourse helps to bring him away from it. Whoever therefore engages in it acts tastelessly himself.]",["\"abgeschmackt\" is rendered \"tasteless\" (\"absurd\" renders \"ungereimt\"); the choice is consistent within the passage.","\"Schein\" = illusion; \"Materie\" = matter; \"Verstand\" = understanding. \"discours\" is kept as in the German, in the French form.","The signs | are carried over as printed, falling between \"Urteile\" and \"für\" and after \"für\"."]],"dohna-s13-004":["With regard to the obviously false no error is possible, only in such judgments in which the falsity lies hidden. Mathematics has this alone about it, that one perceives the falsity of a proposition in it instantly. To remove the hidden ground of error is called to develop the illusion. If someone asserts something whose falsity he well sees and still does not depart from it, then the blame goes more to his will than to his understanding. A gross error is that whose avoidance is easy — it is not the same as the tasteless one. A dangerous error, if it is not merely a matter of extending our cognition for us, but, e.g., we ask always first about the use [which we then perhaps cannot yet see. This is usually done by the shallowest heads.] He who, when he is to indicate the falsity of a cognition, shows its disadvantage, is called a *Consequentiarius*, because he always concludes from the consequences to the ground [ — he begins with the practical]. —",["\"Nutzen\" is rendered \"use\" (Gebrauch would also be \"use\"); the German word is Nutzen.","\"Ein gefährlicher Irrtum, wenn ...\" is evidently incomplete or elliptical in the source and is kept so; the construction of \"wenn uns nicht bloß darum zu tun ist\" is literal and obscure.","\"schließt\" is rendered \"concludes\" (schließen); \"Folgen\" = consequences (not Folgerung); \"Grund\" = ground.","\"Schein entwickeln\" = \"develop the illusion\"; \"einsieht\" = \"sees\"."]],"dohna-s13-005":["The author now speaks in the 5th section",["The passage breaks off, continued by the next heading."]],"dohna-s13-006":["Of the clarity of cognition",["\"Klarheit\" = clarity; \"Erkenntnis\" = cognition."]],"dohna-s13-007":["Clarity is consciousness <not> only of the representations as a whole, but also of their partial representations. A mark is a partial representation, insofar as it is a ground of cognition of the whole representation. A ground of cognition is twofold:",["\"<nicht>\" is an editorial insertion in angle brackets, carried over as printed.","\"Teilvorstellung\" = partial representation; \"Erkenntnisgrund\" = ground of cognition; \"Merkmal\" = mark; \"Bewußtsein\" = consciousness."]],"dohna-s13-008":["1. a ground for cognizing a thing in itself, or the inner ground of cognition;",["\"an sich\" = in itself; \"erkennen\" = cognize."]],"dohna-s13-009":["2. a ground for cognizing a thing from other adjacent things, or the outer. —",["\"Nebendingen\" is rendered \"adjacent things\" (literally \"side-things\")."]],"dohna-s13-010":["In comparison with others we cognize either",["\"Vergleichung\" = comparison."]],"dohna-s13-011":["1. through the *nota* [*nota*, a partial representation that lies at the basis of the concept as mark] *identitatis* [mark, characteristic] and",["\"Kennzeichen\" is rendered \"characteristic\"; \"zum Grunde liegt\" = \"lies at the basis\". Latin terms kept in italics."]],"dohna-s13-012":["2. through the *nota diversitatis* [sign of distinction, e.g., between human being and animal].",["\"Unterscheidungszeichen\" is rendered \"sign of distinction\"."]],"dohna-s13-013":["Partial representations as grounds of cognition can be partial concepts and partial intuitions. The latter do not occur in logic.",["\"Partialvorstellungen\", \"Partialbegriffe\", \"Partialanschauungen\" are rendered \"partial representations\", \"partial concepts\", \"partial intuitions\"; \"Anschauung\" = intuition."]],"dohna-s13-014":["A concept is the representation of that which comprises several representations in itself. The author makes a distinction between mediate and immediate representations. All combinations are either combinations of coordination or of subordination. The former create a whole, which is called aggregate. They arise through connection of the manifold. Insofar as it is subordinated to one another, it is called a series. There there is now a *terminus a priori* and an *a posteriori*, in logic only the *a parte ante*, not the latter *a parte post*. [A mediate mark is *nota notae*, a mark of the mark. If one finally comes in the series of representations subordinated to one another upon an indivisible one, this is called the *terminus a parte ante*. This can be divided, e.g., *quid*, *a parte post* one cannot prove, only illustrate.]",["\"Verbindung\" = combination; \"Verknüpfung\" = connection; \"Mannigfaltige\" = manifold; \"Reihe\" = series.","\"Sofern es einander subordiniert ist\" is kept literally; the referent of \"es\" is ambiguous (the manifold or the whole).","\"Dieser kann eingeteilt werden, z. B. quid\" is kept as it stands; \"unteilbare\" and \"eingeteilt\" (Einteilung = division) are as in the German.","\"mittelbar/unmittelbar\" = mediate/immediate; \"faßt in sich\" = \"comprises in itself\"."]],"dohna-s13-015":["The marks are divided here:",["\"Einteilung\" = division; \"eingeteilt\" = divided."]],"dohna-s13-016":["1. into affirmative and",["\"bejahend\" = affirmative (bejahen = affirm)."]],"dohna-s13-017":["2. into negative. But this belongs properly more to the judgment than to the marks. [Negative marks, through which I designate something as error, which otherwise no one would have fallen upon, are superfluous — likewise affirmative ones that say nothing new or unknown — tautological.] The expression negative marks lets one infer the matter and this does not belong to logic. All negations presuppose affirmations. Thus among the savages there is no poverty, for this would take place only in comparison with the well-to-do. Since they now do not know the positive, they make for themselves no representation of the negative. Thus also no blind person (born so) can make for himself a representation of darkness, because he does not know the light.",["\"verneinende\" is rendered \"negative\" (\"Verneinung\" = negation); \"Bejahung\" = affirmation. OCR \"Wüden\" read as \"Wilden\" (savages) and \"kern\" as \"kein\".","\"kennen\" = know (noscere) in \"nicht das Positive kennen\" and \"das Licht nicht kennt\".","\"Ausdruck\" = expression; \"Materie\" = matter; \"Vorstellung\" = representation."]],"dohna-s13-018":["A mark is important if it is a ground of cognition of great consequences. [The marks in comparison with others (according to diversity or identity) are not as important as those through derivation, inner marks. The importance is: 1. logical, e.g., 3 *termini* in the syllogism; 2. practical. — Thus logical, grammatical incorrectness can be practically unimportant (in a certain respect).]",["\"Syllogismus\" is rendered \"syllogism\"; \"Folgen\" = consequences; \"Wichtigkeit\" = importance.","\"Rücksicht\" is rendered \"respect\"."]],"dohna-s13-019":["If it serves for the distinction of a ground of cognition that is itself great in degree, then this (where the mark is determined in degree) is called intensive, if the mark has reference to multitude, manifoldness [in general to extension] — extensive. The mark is sufficient if it contains the ground of all distinguishing marks [suffices for the derivation], insufficient if it contains only the ground of some signs of distinction. But the insufficient ground is not to be rejected. For it always contains a part of the ground — just as conjectures [without these we never come to certainty].",["\"zureichend\" = sufficient; \"Grad\" = degree; \"Menge\" = multitude; \"Ausdehnung\" = extension; \"Mannigfaltigkeit\" = manifoldness; \"Gewißheit\" = certainty.","\"Mutmaßungen\" is rendered \"conjectures\"; the comparison \"sowie Mutmaßungen\" is kept elliptical.","\"so heißt dies\" has an ambiguous antecedent (the distinction or the mark); kept as in the German."]],"dohna-s13-020":["The marks are further divided:",["\"Einteilung\" = division."]],"dohna-s13-021":["1. into absolutely necessary, unchangeable and",["\"notwendig\" = necessary; \"schlechterdings\" = absolutely."]],"dohna-s13-022":["2. into contingent.",["\"zufällig\" = contingent."]],"dohna-s13-023":["Necessary are those without which the thing absolutely cannot be thought. One obtains them through the mere analysis of the concept. [Essence the sum total of the supreme marks of a thing, *complexus notarum conceptum aliquem primitive constituentium*. First is required *completudo*; 2. that the marks are not derived from others, but are primitive — and inseparable — the logical essence the inner possibility.]",["\"Wesen\" = essence (being); \"Inbegriff\" = sum total; \"möglich\" = possible; \"Zergliederung\" = analysis.","The Latin phrase is kept as printed, including \"conceptum\" and \"completudo\" (apparently for *completudo*/*complexus*), with no emendation; the grammar (\"conceptum\" for \"conceptum\") is doubtful and left as is.","\"erfordert\" is rendered \"is required\"; the sentence \"Zuerst wird erfordert completudo\" is kept elliptical."]],"dohna-s13-024":["In logic there is never any talk of the marks of the thing. In that case it would be occupied with matter. But it deals only with the marks of the concept. What is physically necessary can be only contingent logically. E.g., it is physically necessary that all bodies fall, but this lies only in the thing and is only contingent logically. The *notae logicae necessariae* belong to the *esse* of the concept. These are *ad esse conceptus necessariae*, *pertinent ad essentiam*. [Man is mortal, can err — *extraessentiale* — what does not belong essentially to it.] One cannot cancel these marks without canceling the thing itself. — *Melius esse logicum* (like the *beatitudo juridica*, when it is said *beati possidentes*).",["Merkmal = mark (nota); Begriff = concept; Sache and Ding are both rendered 'thing'.","aufheben = 'cancel' (twice, kept the same).","The OCR sign ■ before 'was nicht wesentlich' was dropped as a scanning artifact; square brackets are kept as in the source."]],"dohna-s13-025":["The necessary marks of the things belong to the thing itself [or *ad esse*]:",["The OCR reads 'od. ad f esse'; the stray 'f' is dropped as an obvious slip. The reading is not fully certain."]],"dohna-s13-026":["1. as *constitutiva*, either like component parts (*essentialia*) [essential pieces] — *originarie* | belonging to the concept | [*originarie*: extension]",["Bestandstücke = 'component parts'; Stücke = 'pieces', kept distinct from Teil = 'part'.","The OCR '«(essentialia)' is read as '(essentialia)'. Ausdehnung = extension."]],"dohna-s13-027":["2. as *rationata*, or as sequels (*attributa*) which belong *derivative* to the concept, e.g., the concept triangle — the sequels are whether the angles are right or etc.",["The OCR 'alsrationata' is read as 'als rationata'; '<attributa)' is read as '(attributa)'.","Folge = sequel. 'recht f oder usw.' is garbled in the OCR; the 'f' is dropped and the reading is doubtful (the German has 'recht', which may be a truncated 'rechte').","*derivative* is kept as the Latin adverb the German prints."]],"dohna-s13-028":["In corporeal things divisibility is a mark, *quod pertinet ad essentiam*, but not as *constitutivum*, but as *rationatum*. [From the mark that the being is corporeal, divisibility can be derived.]",["Wesen is rendered 'being' here, as the glossary offers 'being; essence'; elsewhere in these passages, where the logical or real Wesen is meant, 'essence'."]],"dohna-s13-029":["The former are also called *essentialia*, essential pieces [the sum total of all *essentialia* is called the essence], the latter *attributa* (properties) [they are 1. *communia*; 2. *propria*].",["Inbegriff = sum total; Eigenschaften = properties (Beschaffenheit is also 'property' in the glossary)."]],"dohna-s13-030":["The contingent marks are either:",[]],"dohna-s13-031":["1. *notae internae contingentes* — *modi*, e.g., learnedness | or |",["The signs | or | are carried over as printed."]],"dohna-s13-032":["2. *notae externae contingentes* — *sive relationes*.",[]],"dohna-s13-033":["The tabular division of the marks (predicates) would therefore be",["Einteilung = division."]],"dohna-s13-034":["1. *ad essentiam pertinentia*, without which the thing cannot be thought.",[]],"dohna-s13-035":["a) *ut rationata sive attributa*;",[]],"dohna-s13-036":["b) *ut constitutiva sive essentialia*.",[]],"dohna-s13-037":["[One can often derive one concept from another, reciprocate. That gives the surest mark.]",["'reciprocieren' is an infinitive without an object; kept as it stands."]],"dohna-s13-038":["2. *extraessentialia* [without which the concept | can be thought |], e.g., a triangle — there the angles are undetermined.",["The apparatus signs | are carried over as printed."]],"dohna-s13-039":["a) *externa* — relation, the outer determination, whether he is father or son [the outer determinations of the marks are always contingent];",["Bestimmung = determination."]],"dohna-s13-040":["b) *interna*, the inner determinations of a thing, insofar as they are not necessary, are called *modi*, e.g., learned or unlearned.",[]],"dohna-s13-041":["When we speak of the essence of things, it happens of the logical essence (which consists in the concept), not of the real essence (of nature), of which is dealt with in metaphysics. [Sum total of all those inner determinations that contain the first ground of the existence of a thing is the natural essence. — This is very hard to see, the logical essence very easy, it lies merely in the concept. — ]",["'hegt' in the OCR is read as 'liegt'.","Realwesen = real essence; Naturwesen = natural essence; Dasein = existence. The syntax 'it happens of' follows the German 'so geschieht es vom'."]],"dohna-s13-042":["*Essentia est complexus notarum conceptum aliquem primitive constituentium*. The logical essence is the sum total of those marks that are sufficient to derive that which belongs to the essence. The logical essence is easy to cognize. For one has nothing more to do with it than to analyze the concepts. The real essence we can never see completely, e.g., we can never experience all the marks of water, however high physics may stand. Many logical concepts are nevertheless difficult to define, e.g., the concept virtue. There one has by far not yet got out all the marks. One names the following: a skill in lawful actions, which are exercised freely, but this is not yet enough [it is the moral strength in following them with struggle against obstacles]. Bodies can be defined perfectly — a filling of space shows only matter, but the description of its figure according to the three dimensions indicates the body.",["OCR 'heraus.Man' is read as 'heraus. Man'.","erkennen = cognize; zureichend = sufficient; Materie = matter; Körper = body (singular generic in German, rendered plural in 'Bodies can be defined')."]],"dohna-s13-043":["Now the author speaks",[]],"dohna-s13-044":["Of obscure representations",["dunkel = obscure; Vorstellung = representation."]],"dohna-s13-045":["Suppose the representation is distinct, i.e., its partial representations clear, e.g., right, in which constraint takes place, equity is distinguished from it, this is very hard to see. We can make obscure representations clear through examples, but this making clear is not logical, only aesthetic.",["deutlich = distinct; klar = clear; Recht = 'right' (as in law); the sentence is kept as the German strings it together."]],"dohna-s13-046":["[The obscurity is subjective if the ground lies in the subject — objective if it lies in the object. — An obscure representation — that of which one is not conscious, this seems to be a contradiction — one cannot immediately, but yet mediately through inference | grasp | it.]",["The word between the | signs is rendered 'grasp' (erfassen); the OCR reading of this word is slightly uncertain.","Widerspruch = contradiction; Schluß = inference."]],"dohna-s13-047":["Often one calls a presentation obscure because it is logically distinct, but aesthetically does not have enough clarity — no fitting examples can be given [e.g., body, extended impenetrable figure]. One can distinguish virtue from holiness; for in the latter there are no temptations to evil at all [e.g., the angels]. This one has otherwise forgotten. In our distinct cognition we can still think a perfection, the *completudo* (comprehensiveness — comprehensive distinctness) [in a concept where there are coordinated marks, as well as deep through subordinated marks — if I here have even only a single one, it can be *nota superior* — let themselves be traced back to simple concepts.] The greatest distinctness through subordinated marks is called the depth of cognition. Both together make up thoroughness.",["Vortrag = 'presentation'. OCR 'aiis' is read as 'aus'; 'undurchdringhche' as 'undurchdringliche'.","The bracketed passage is syntactically broken in the German and is left so. Vollkommenheit = perfection; Erkenntnis = cognition; Gründlichkeit = thoroughness."]],"dohna-s14-001":["[nth hour]",["The German 'nte Stunde' is a placeholder for an unspecified ordinal."]],"dohna-s14-002":["The deep distinctness is always harder to understand, because it is more abstract. In order to attain full distinctness, one must always seek out the marks of the marks, until one finally arrives at the highest marks, at the simple concepts. We can compare with this lively cognition or rather way of representation (the consciousness is lively in it). [The more this representation brings about alterations in the subject, the livelier is this science through examples]. One can call this aesthetically greater clarity liveliness. The logically greater clarity is distinctness.",["The OCR has a stray 'f' before 'durch Beispiele'; it is dropped, and the reading there is doubtful. '<(das' is read as '(das'.","Wissenschaft = science; Bewußtsein = consciousness; einfach = simple; Art = way."]],"dohna-s14-003":["[Comprehensibility and clarity are to be distinguished. Through resolution of the marks the concept becomes harder to grasp — because of the subtlety. All making distinct rests on the development of the partial representations (insofar as these are not clear in themselves).]",["OCR 'Subtilität.Alle' is read as 'Subtilität. Alle'. Faßlichkeit = comprehensibility; fassen = grasp."]],"dohna-s14-004":["All our representations can have a twofold relation, first to the subject, then they are called sensations, second to the object, cognition.",["Empfindung = sensation; Object = object."]],"dohna-s14-005":["Cognitions can become lively:",[]],"dohna-s14-006":["1. if they are related to the material, sensibility (sensation) (subj.);",["Materiale = 'the material' (Stoff is also 'material' in the glossary). The OCR braces around Empfindung are read as parentheses."]],"dohna-s14-007":["2. if they are related to the formal, intuition (objective).",["Anschauung = intuition."]],"dohna-s14-008":["[A representation that enlivens must present the object as present.",["The square bracket opens here and is closed in the passage that follows; left unclosed as in the source."]],"dohna-s14-009":["It is different 1. to make a cognition distinct (here nothing is added, only raising to distinctness, this | analytic |,",["The parenthesis is left unclosed as in the source."]],"dohna-s14-010":["2. to make a distinct cognition — where previously there was none yet, this | synthetic | .]",["synthetisch = synthetic."]],"dohna-s14-011":["In logical distinctness the multitude of marks does not matter at all (S. P. 42 . . .), but in the aesthetic it lies precisely in the multifariousness of them (e.g., description of spring). The origin of distinctness is twofold, analytic and synthetic. The mathematician has the latter manner.",["The reference '<S. P. 42 . . .f)' is garbled in the OCR; rendered as '(S. P. 42 . . .)', and the abbreviation is left unresolved.","Menge = multitude; Vielfältigkeit = 'multifariousness'; Manier = 'manner'."]],"dohna-s14-012":["Now the author speaks",[]],"dohna-s14-013":["Of the comprehensible and incomprehensible",["Begreifliche / Unbegreifliche, from begreifen = comprehend."]],"dohna-s14-014":["Feelings can never bring forth a cognition. [The word representation must not be explained at all. For one would also precisely thereby already have to presuppose the concept of it.]",["Gefühl = feeling; erklären = explain."]],"dohna-s14-015":["One begins 1. with representing. The 2nd is then to perceive something — *percipere* — to represent something to oneself with consciousness, the 3rd — to be acquainted with something <(*noscere*), i.e., to cognize it in such a way that one is acquainted with it in comparison with others according to their identity and diversity. To understand something (*intelligere*): to represent something to oneself through the understanding, through concepts [so that one can explain oneself about it, can give the marks of it]. To cognize the thing from reason [i.e., *a priori* through the understanding, even if it were not given], from general principles according to its grounds, is called having insight into [having insight into, *perspicere*]. Thus . . . to have insight into *a priori* is: to cognize not only that it is so (as, e.g., the dissolution of salt by water), but that it must be so (e.g., an eclipse of the sun (mathematical) [even if we had never seen it]). The last step is to comprehend, to have sufficient insight into something (for a certain aim). To comprehend absolutely (in every aim), *comprehendere* (is impossible for us) [not the moral rules]. One can comprehend perfectly, e.g., various hydraulic phenomena of water, and have insight into this *a priori* [as soon as we assume the fluid as *hypothesis* — having insight requires throughout consciousness of the truth]. But that which is fluid, one cannot have insight into *a priori* (not).",["The source reads “Einteiligere)”, an evident OCR slip; silently read as “(*intelligere*)”. The opening “<” before “(*noscere*)” is kept as printed, with no matching closing mark.","kennen = “be acquainted with” (*noscere*); erkennen = “cognize”; wissen (other passages) = “know”. einsehen (not in the glossary) = “have insight into” (*perspicere*); begreifen = “comprehend” (*comprehendere*); verstehen = “understand” (*intelligere*).","zureichend = “sufficient”; Absicht = “aim”; Grund = “ground”; Merkmal = “mark”.","“Also . . .” is kept with the ellipsis as printed. The final “(nicht)” stands as in the source, after “*a priori*”; whether it negates “einsehn” or is an insertion is left open."]],"dohna-s14-016":["The example contributes to understanding only aesthetically. [“Geist,” “Geischt” (still in Switzerland), “Gescht,” “Dunst.”] We often believe we have insight into something that at bottom we can only explain to ourselves — understand according to its possibility.",["The German words in the bracket are mentioned, not used, and are left untranslated; the bracket is an insertion in the source.","einsehen = “have insight into”; erklären = “explain”; verstehen = “understand”; Möglichkeit = “possibility”."]],"dohna-s14-017":["One always understands best that which one can make oneself.",["verstehen = “understand”."]],"dohna-s14-018":["The distinct concept becomes complete when all marks are contained in it [thoroughly distinct, insofar as it contains all coordinate marks.] [Totality with regard to the subordinate marks the author calls completeness.] One must not make one’s concept superabundant, for one makes it hard for oneself when one takes up superfluous | marks |. The concept in which all dispensable marks are left out is called precise. — The concept that contains not too much, not too little, is called appropriate (adequate) [since one cognizes not only the marks themselves but also the marks of the marks, but the concept does not fit adequately any sooner than when the series is brought entirely to its end.]",["The two | signs are carried over as printed (apparatus of page/line break).","angemessen (adäquat) = “appropriate (adequate)”; later “adäquat” = “adequately/adequate”. Totalität = “totality”; Vollständigkeit = “completeness”; complet = “complete”; präzis = “precise”; Merkmal = “mark”.","“ausführlich deutlich” rendered “thoroughly distinct”; ausführlich is not in the glossary. “superabundant” kept as the German’s own Latinate word."]],"dohna-s14-019":["The first degree of subordination is a mark of a mark. The adequate concept rests on coordination.",["Grad = “degree”; Merkmal = “mark”; adäquat = “adequate”."]],"dohna-s14-020":["[If the sum of the marks does not contain too much — precise — if it does not contain too little — adequate. — Distinctness of the understanding is that through concepts. The distinctness of the power of judgment that of examples through intuition. Distinctness of reason that through mediate marks (just as distinctness of the understanding through immediate ones). All concepts not precise, indeterminate, vague.]",["Summe rendered “sum” (not Inbegriff). Urteilskraft = “power of judgment”; Anschauung = “intuition”; Deutlichkeit = “distinctness”.","The sentence “Die Deutlichkeit der Urteilskraft die der Beispiele durch Anschauung” is elliptical and is kept elliptical; “vag” is rendered “vague”."]],"dohna-s14-021":["A bright head, that always knows what it wants.",[]],"dohna-s15-001":["Sixth Section On the Certainty of Cognition",["Erkenntnis = “cognition”; Gewißheit = “certainty”."]],"dohna-s15-002":["Here the discussion is not of truth but of taking to be true. [Objectively all propositions are certainly true or certainly false.] This is the judgment in relation and reference to the subject. We want to know in this connection not precisely the grounds of truth, but only those of taking to be true. The steps are: 1. to opine, 2. to believe, 3. to know. One can call these three concepts the modalities of the *sensus veri et falsi*.",["Fürwahrhalten = “taking to be true”; meinen = “opine” (noun Meinung = “opinion”); glauben = “believe”; wissen = “know”.","Stufen = “steps”; Sätze = “propositions”; Gründe = “grounds”."]],"dohna-s15-003":["To opine is a taking to be true insufficient with consciousness both subjectively and objectively. An insufficient taking to be true is what does not suffice to exclude the opposite. Whoever only opines is still open to counter-grounds.",["zureichend and hinreichen/hinreichend both = “sufficient/suffice”; the German difference is not marked in the text.","The source prints “Für wahrhalten” in two words; rendered as elsewhere."]],"dohna-s15-004":["But if (one) believes, then this is no more. [It is an assertoric taking to be true, which we nevertheless do not cognize with consciousness of necessity (thus must nevertheless concede that alterations are possible).] Belief is a taking to be true that is indeed subjectively <sufficient,) yet with consciousness objectively insufficient. Thus believing is distinguished from knowing in that it is not capable of proof. Believing is a private taking to be true, sufficiently certain only for me. Hence I cannot yet say that I know it.",["“(man)” is as printed (possibly an OCR/editorial parenthesis); “<zureichendes,)” is kept with its odd brackets, as printed.","“so ist dies nicht mehr” is kept as obscure: what “no more” refers to is left open.","beweisfähig = “capable of proof”; Beweis = “proof”; Abänderungen = “alterations”."]],"dohna-s15-005":["Knowledge is a taking to be true sufficient both subjectively and objectively. [I hold the opposite to be impossible. A taking to be true insufficient with consciousness both objectively and subjectively is opining. One grasps the concept of belief best in a practical aim (although this influence on the will does not belong in logic).] It can be empirical through experience and apodictic *a priori*. The taking to be true of an apodictic proposition can be apodictically certain.",["Wissen as noun = “knowledge”; hinreichend = “sufficient” (as zureichend elsewhere). Absicht = “aim”.","“Es” in “Es kann empirisch . . . sein” has no stated antecedent in the German; left as “It”."]],"dohna-s15-006":["What I opine, the judgment I say only problematically, hence the taking to be true is 1. problematic, namely when it is accepted in such a way that it is still to be demonstrated. It is called problematic because it serves only to find the sufficient one among several grounds.",["A stray “f” after “erweisen” in the OCR is dropped as an OCR slip.","erweisen rendered “demonstrate”; “Was ich meine, das Urteil” is kept in its loose construction."]],"dohna-s15-007":["Now the taking to be true is 2. assertoric in belief. For I say that it is sufficient for me (subjectively), but whether it is also objectively sufficient, I do not settle. Very many representations of a future state are mere products of the imagination, many ideas that please us very much (e.g., of male and female light) only ideas in the subject.",["ausmachen rendered “settle”. Vorstellung = “representation”; Zustand = “state”; Einbildungskraft = “imagination”; Idee = “idea”."]],"dohna-s15-008":["The taking to be true is 3. apodictic in knowledge, [*intuitio* in mathematics. Conviction. *Persuasion* is the illusion of conviction, uncertain whether subjective or objective.]",["Überzeugung = “conviction”; Schein = “illusion”. “Persuasion” is the Latinate word as the source prints it (cf. Überredung = “persuasion”), italicized here."]],"dohna-s15-009":["The propositions of taking to be true can be empirical and the taking to be true apodictic. The taking to be true is connected either with the consciousness of subjective necessity or with that of the contingent. In the latter case it is, if it is subjectively and objectively insufficient, opining, but if it is subjectively sufficient and objectively insufficient — believing. Hypotheses are opinions. In mathematics there is neither opining nor believing, but only knowing — or nothing — and in merely speculative metaphysics it is likewise.",["“Die Sätze des Fürwahrhaltens” is kept literally. Zufällig = “contingent”; verbunden = “connected”.","“Glauben” in “Glauben” (second case) rendered “believing”, the verbal noun."]],"dohna-s15-010":["[Something is a matter of opining if it cannot at all become an object of knowledge, neither objectively nor subjectively (e.g., whether there are inhabitants of the moon). — About the possibility of animal generation of its like we will never have more than opinions. — Matters of belief, where only a subjectively sufficient ground of taking to be true is for us. — Everything that we assume from ends is for us only subjectively sufficient — (i.e., I must assume that this is so, otherwise I do not get on with my reason here — this I can indeed not assume as holding objectively, yet it is sufficient for me.)",["The stray “f” after “ist” in the OCR is dropped as an OCR slip. The opening bracket is never closed in the passage as printed; this is left as it stands.","Sache des Meinens = “matter of opining” (Meinung = “opinion” elsewhere); Gegenstand = “object”; Zweck = “end”; Vernunft = “reason”.","“Wissens” as noun = “knowledge”."]],"dohna-s15-011":["Where knowledge is possible, this is already a matter of knowledge, e.g., geography. — Only objects of belief of reason can one call matters of belief.]",["The stray “f” after “Wo” in the OCR is dropped as an OCR slip. Vernunftglaube = “belief of reason” (compound kept literal); Gegenstand = “object”.","The closing bracket matches the opening bracket in the preceding passage."]],"dohna-s15-012":["What are objects of opinion, belief, and knowledge? Are objectively historical propositions objects of opinion, of belief, or even of knowledge? They are objects of knowledge. For there are cases where we can use the experience of others as our own. Are mathematical propositions objects of opinion etc.? They are all, because they are *a priori*, objects of knowledge. (Concepts are called transcendental when we have them *a priori*.) Can one not also accept propositions *a priori* on belief? Yes. Mathematics is also of this kind. [One believes the mathematicians, because it is not possible that they can err, since they would immediately run into false sequels. In philosophy, however, there is no belief.",["Folge = “sequel” per the glossary (not “consequence”, which is Folgerung). Erfahrung = “experience”; Weltweisheit is not in play here; “Philosophie” = “philosophy”.","“Meinens” etc. rendered as the nouns “opinion, belief, knowledge”; the German verbal nouns are not distinguished from Meinung/Glaube/Wissen in the English.","The bracket opened before “Man glaubt” is not closed in the passage; left as printed. “objektiv historische Sätze” is kept with its ambiguous adverb-adjective attachment."]],"dohna-s15-013":["Believing is he who is capable of a belief of reason. The morally practical rests solely on reason. What now, then, either is itself duty or stands in connate combination with it — this object of belief (practical taking to be true) — whoever does not etc. unbelieving, whoever believes everything else, credulous.]",["The construction “Was nun also . . . — dies Gegenstand des Glaubens” is left anacoluthic, as in the German. The bracket closes the one opened in the preceding passage.","Gläubig = “believing”; ungläubig = “unbelieving”; leichtgläubig = “credulous” (“easily believing”); connat = “connate”; Verbindung = “combination”; Pflicht = “duty”."]],"dohna-s15-014":["A matter of opining is that to the opposite of which one has no grounds. There are objects of opining that can become objects of belief and of knowledge, e.g., in physics what Newton opines about the origin of the planets, etc. There is a belief that in a practical (not in a speculative) relation is esteemed equal to knowledge, e.g., many propositions of the ancient philosophers — Probabilism. The certainty that (belongs) to the acquaintance with the command or prohibition of an action is the moral. One cannot (call) that which is certain according to the rules of prudence morally, but pragmatically certain. One misuses the concept of moral certainty when one often has nothing further [e.g., in wagers] than a practically sufficient ground of truth; it must not be theoretically sufficient. [Logical certainty (apodictic) is 1. philosophical, 2. mathematical.] Moral certainty relates to conscience. E.g., in the case of theorems of Catholicism, which many accept without consulting conscience. If one does something at the risk of erring, then one is never morally certain. With the word belief one always thinks a voluntary assent, with the word opining a decided assent. A matter of belief is that which is to be taken to be true and of which no knowledge takes place. It is regarded as a taking to be true that depends on my pleasure. Belief of reason is called simply *fides*. It is an involved concept. [For \"believing\" (Gläubig) we actually have no Latin word — *fidelis* — faithful — .] There are certain overreaching judgments (transcendent), which lie outside the boundaries of our cognition, which one can only believe. Whoever is not convinced even by moral grounds is an unbeliever. An unbeliever in the theological sense is one who does not see into and will not accept the supersensible, even if it is morally actually necessary, because it cannot be proved theoretically with reason (it is uncertain). One who does not accept even what is historically certain, merely because the certainty is not apodictic, one calls incredulous. Certainty is the objective sufficiency of taking to be true. In belief it is subjective. There I can only say: I am certain. But in the case of certainty: it is certain, this is universally valid.",["Gewißheit = certainty; Meinen/Meinung = opining/opinion; Glauben/Glaube = belief; Beifall = assent; Fürwahrhalten = taking to be true; Vernunftglaube = \"belief of reason\" (literal).","Kenntnis (\"acquaintance with the command or prohibition\") is the glossary's 'acquaintance'; Wissen is rendered 'knowledge'.","The parentheses around \"(gehört)\" and \"(nennen)\" are as printed; the word order of \"nicht moralisch, sondern pragmatisch gewiß (nennen)\" is kept.","Ungläubiger = unbeliever; ungläubisch = incredulous (two different German words). Im theologischen Verstände: \"Verstand\" here rendered 'sense'. Einsehen = 'see into'.","überschwenglich = 'overreaching', followed by the Latin-derived \"(transcendent)\" as in the text.","hinreichend (\"sufficient\") and Zulänglichkeit (\"sufficiency\") are both rendered with 'sufficient/sufficiency'.","OCR corrected silently: \"f ides\" to fides; \"Beheben\" to Belieben; \"hegen\" to liegen."]],"dohna-s15-015":["A reasonable cognition is different from a cognition of reason, the former must be the property of all cognitions. In the difference of mathematical certainty it is not a matter of degree but of quantity.",["vernünftig = 'reasonable'; Vernunfterkenntnis = 'cognition of reason'; Eigenschaft = 'property' (Beschaffenheit is also 'property' in the glossary).","The syntax of \"Beim Unterschied der mathematischen Gewißheit kommt es nicht auf ... an\" is kept as literally as possible."]],"dohna-s15-016":["All certainty is either",["The sentence breaks off; continued in the following passage."]],"dohna-s15-017":["1. empirical, is that which stems from experience, | or |",["The signs | are carried over as they stand."]],"dohna-s15-018":["2. rational, is that whose origin is *a priori*. It is again considered in a twofold respect:",["Betrachten = 'consider'."]],"dohna-s15-019":["objective a) logical, with respect to the theoretical faculty of cognition;",["Erkenntnisvermögen = 'faculty of cognition'."]],"dohna-s15-020":["subjective b) practical, with respect to moral use.",["Gebrauch = 'use'."]],"dohna-s15-021":["Rational logical certainty is called *generaliter* an apodictic one, for it is cognized from grounds *a priori*. [We have conviction of a matter when we cognize it as true with the consciousness that our judgment is objective.] One cannot say that mathematical certainty is greater than the philosophical. The difference lies only in this, that it is intuitive, but the philosophical only discursive. Philosophical certainty can indeed attain the same degree, but (never) the property that it <would> become intuitive. One is accustomed to say of philosophical propositions that they are as certain as 2 times 2 [is] 4, but this is not determinate, for the latter is mathematical, not philosophical certainty. [The degree of the two certainties is equal, but they are of different kind.]",["Überzeugung = conviction; Bewußtsein = consciousness; Grad = degree; Art = 'kind'; Eigenschaft = 'property'.","\"<würde)\" is printed in the OCR with mismatched brackets; I give \"<would>\" and keep \"(never)\" as printed. \"2 mal 2 4\" has no verb in the text; the bracketed \"[is]\" is inserted only because English requires it, and is not in the German.","\"hegt\" corrected silently to liegt."]],"dohna-s15-022":["[Hour, Monday]",["Stunde = 'hour' (lecture hour)."]],"dohna-s15-023":["Accepting is a contingent assent that has sufficient ground with regard to a certain aim. Settled — this presupposes a dispute, since the matter was uncertain. Of mathematical propositions one cannot say that they are settled; for who can dispute about them?",["Annehmen = 'accept' (used consistently); Ausgemacht = 'settled'; Absicht = 'aim'; zufällig = 'contingent'; Satz = 'proposition'."]],"dohna-s15-024":["[One sometimes says that something is indisputable. But this means only as much as undisputed — no one has ever been found who had refuted it.]",["OCR \"hatte\" retained literally as 'had' (the indicative); a subjunctive \"hätte\" may be intended."]],"dohna-s15-025":["Evidently no other cognition can be set forth than the mathematical. For nothing else can bring about an *intuitum a priori*.",["Augenscheinlich = 'evidently'; dargelegt = 'set forth'. *intuitum* is the Latin form as printed (for Anschauung, intuitus)."]],"dohna-s15-026":["[A cognition is called thorough insofar as it is secured against doubt by logically sufficient grounds.] A thorough cognition must be derived from secure principles. Thorough history can be acquired only through chronology.",["gründlich = 'thorough'; Prinzipien = 'principles' (as Grundsätze are)."]],"dohna-s15-027":["Cogent means as much as consequent, that everything stands *in nexu*, in interconnection. What is cogent is the highest in the use of our cognition — to be consequent. [Consequent means following correctly, when the connection of the sequel with the ground is in accordance with the logical laws. Cogent, this proposition hangs together with the preceding ones in the series of a system. — The more consequent a human being is in dangerous maxims, the more harmful he is. — ]",["bündig = 'cogent' (not in the glossary); konsequent = 'consequent'; folgerecht = 'following correctly'; Folge = 'sequel'; Zusammenhang = 'interconnection'; Verknüpfung = 'connection'.","Ending dash and spacing of the bracket are as printed."]],"dohna-s15-028":["When the mathematician demonstrates, he compels everyone who understands him to assent. There, then, we cannot actually say: we give him assent. [The expression: to give assent, seems not to stand in our will. Can we hold back our assent from a mathematical proof? Taking to be true belongs to the understanding, but investigation to choice.]",["Beifall = 'assent'; Beweis = 'proof'; Verstand = 'understanding'; Willkür = 'choice' (not in the glossary)."]],"dohna-s15-029":["In the *suspensio judicii* there is something of freedom. One can also give one's assent without deciding with the judgment — which still waits for several grounds — where one still accepts the opposite as possible. I am free when, in a practical aim, I accept the proposition on account of the good that can follow from it. One can therefore say: There are things in matters of religion, e.g., our eternal continuance, which we accept from just this good, for this life is still far from commensurate with the idea of the highest good.",["OCR \"Avartet\" corrected to wartet. Ambiguity preserved: \"was noch auf mehrere Gründe wartet\" may refer to the judgment or to the deciding; I have placed it after \"the judgment\" as in the German.","angemessen = 'commensurate'; Fortdauer = 'continuance'; annehmen = 'accept' throughout."]],"dohna-s15-030":["The *suspensio judicii* consists in this, that, when on both sides all grounds are equally insufficient, none is outweighing. It can be 1. *ob indifferentiam* — 2. *ob aequilibrium*. To maintain oneself by choice in *suspensione judicii* [it is the middle between taking to be true and rejecting — only a problematic judgment remains] testifies to a very great head and is therefore extremely difficult, because inclination mingles itself at once into the judgment of the understanding. To be and remain irresolute *ob aequilibrium* testifies to weak heads.",["OCR \"aequihbrium\" corrected to aequilibrium. willkürlich = 'by choice' (cf. Willkür). Kopf = 'head' (literal).","Verstandesurteil = 'judgment of the understanding'."]],"dohna-s15-031":["*Argumentum ad crumenam*, because he who has no full purse cannot wager along. [Whoever does not want to wager along is taken for this, that he is uncertain of his matter.]",["OCR \"kernen\" corrected to keinen. \"davor angenommen\" rendered literally as 'taken for this'; the German is elliptical."]],"dohna-s15-032":["*Argumentum ad verecundiam*. Because a great man has asserted it, and one will surely not want to know better. [A bad argument.]",["behaupten = 'assert'."]],"dohna-s15-033":["The best argument is surely always the one *ad tutum*, namely in the case that one accepts what is safest. It is cunning, but does not always go to what is true.",["das Wahre = 'what is true'."]],"dohna-s15-034":["As for the *suspensio judicii*, it is:",[]],"dohna-s15-035":["1. *critica* or *indagatoria*, a postponement of judgment until closer investigation [until a ground for taking to be true presents itself];",["Urteil = 'judgment'."]],"dohna-s15-036":["2. *sceptica*, is the renunciation of an assertoric judgment. [A complete renunciation of all certainty. The principle always to postpone one's judgment one calls ἐποχή — *Status indifferentiae*.]",["The Greek word is garbled in the OCR (\"ettoxt)\"); ἐποχή is a conjecture, not a secure reading.","Grundsatz = 'principle'."]],"dohna-s15-037":["We now come to the doctrine",["Lehre = 'doctrine'. The sentence continues in the heading that follows."]],"dohna-s15-038":["Of prejudices",["Von den Vorurteilen, a heading; Vorurteil = 'prejudice'."]],"dohna-s15-039":["A preliminary judgment [*Judicium praevium*] has in common with prejudice only that it is likewise pronounced before the investigation of the matter. [*Praejudicium* is not different from f both are one and the same. The latter is properly an appeal to a judgment, thus (through) experience; this, however, contributes to culture. *Judicium praevium* is a judgment that precedes reflection — without reflection there can be no judging at all, but indeed without investigation; what is immediately certain permits none either. — ] *Judicium reflectens*, where one posits a judgment straightaway as a problem, in order to investigate the truth. For searching too one must have a particular principle. To seek this out belongs to power of judgment. One cannot teach it. For if one wanted to give him rules, he would already be using power of judgment, in order to subsume under them. A *judicium praevium* therefore goes before the investigation; but with reflection it must always go along.",["Vorurteil = prejudice; vorläufig = preliminary; Überlegung = reflection; Untersuchung = investigation; Urteilskraft = power of judgment.","\"von f beides\": the \"f\" is an OCR residue and cannot be read; left as it stands. \"{durch)\" read as \"(durch)\".","\"Problem\" is the German word used here (not Aufgabe). Prinzip and Grundsatz are both \"principle\".","\"ihm\" (\"give him rules\") has no clear antecedent in the German; left as it stands.","\"gefällt\" rendered \"pronounced\"; \"gleichgehen\" rendered \"go along\" (literally \"go equally\")."]],"dohna-s15-040":["[Preliminary judgments arise from grounds insufficient with consciousness. — Skill in preliminary judgments is a kind of prudence. This chapter has until now been neglected in logic. Every inventor must judge preliminarily.]",["\"mit Bewußtsein unzureichenden Gründen\" kept literal; it could be read as \"grounds known to be insufficient\" or as \"insufficient grounds, with consciousness\".","Klugheit = prudence."]],"dohna-s15-041":["I reflect on something when I compare it with the laws of the understanding. There are propositions that, prior to all investigation, are already *iudicia determinantia* (determining judgments), e.g., between 2 points only one straight line is possible. One also calls them *judicia indemonstrabilia* — proofless, immediately certain, not unprovable — we will yet speak of this in what follows.",["OCR \"mdemonstrabilia\" corrected to *indemonstrabilia*.","Satz = proposition; überlegen = reflect (cognate of Überlegung); \"in der Folge\" rendered \"in what follows\"."]],"dohna-s15-042":["[The propensity to judge from subjectively universal causes, to make objectively universal principles, is prejudice. — ]",["Hang = propensity; \"allgemein\" = universal."]],"dohna-s15-043":["*Judicia reflectentia* are those that initiate the investigation, that show 1. whether a matter needs an investigation, 2. how I should investigate a matter. Prejudice is a maxim, to judge objectively from subjective grounds. A prejudice is never a single proposition, e.g., the apple does not fall far from the tree. This is not yet a prejudice, but only a sequel from another. For no prejudice is a proposition, but only a maxim, in order to draw propositions from it. A subjective principle is called a maxim. An objective one is called a principle. A rule that the subject makes a principle for itself is called a maxim. Thus many human beings make for themselves a rule that is objectively false subjectively into a maxim. There the maxim is grounded on an illusion, which we have already defined above in such a way. Prejudice, therefore, is a maxim, to take as true rules that are grounded on illusion. The formulas are either 1. sayings of thought, |or| 2. sayings of sense — sentences | or | 3. sayings of doctrine or | 4. saws, *proverbia*. [A saying is a proposition that is bound to a common expression, so that this facilitates recollection. — Sayings of doctrine or *canones*. — *Sentenzen* are aesthetic formulas. — *Canones* are classical — *Dicta* are sacred formulas.] One will best cognize the national spirit of a people from its proverbs. —",["OCR \"ans\" read \"aus\"; \"Sehern\" read \"Schein\" (doubtful, translated as \"illusion\"); stray \"1\" before \"Sprichwörter\" dropped.","Maxime = maxim; Grundsatz = principle and Prinzip = principle (one English word for two German words); Schein = illusion; für wahr halten = take as true.","\"Folge\" rendered \"sequel\" (glossary); \"aus\" with it is awkward in the German as well.","Denksprüche, Sinnsprüche, Lehrsprüche are rendered literally with \"sayings\"; \"Sentenzen\" is left as the German word in the bracketed gloss and rendered \"sentences\" in the main list; Sprichwörter = proverbs, Sprichwort in the main list rendered \"saws\" to keep apart from *proverbia*.","Erkennen = cognize; Spruch = saying."]],"dohna-s15-044":["The prejudice that we first named, like father, like son, or the like, is properly grounded on this, that one has assumed as generally valid the maxim that there is much similarity between human beings and animals. And this prejudice is grounded on the subjective ground that what all the world says is true.",["\"für allgemein-geltend\" rendered \"as generally valid\" (geltend; gültig = valid).","Grund = ground; Welt = world in \"alle Welt\"."]],"dohna-s15-045":["*Sentenzen* one could call aesthetic formulas, just as proverbs *formulas vulgares*. The sentences are usually formulas for wisdom. The *canones* are formulas for science. Classical formulas are called, in the juristic sense: *Brocardica*. [They have authority and declare all investigation superfluous. One calls the proverbs *loci communes*, because everyone speaks thus, so this is supposed to be true — proverbs are the concentrated (common) wisdom of the people. Hence those who always want to speak proverbs, vulgar.] *Dicta* are sayings κατ᾽ ἐξοχήν.",["OCR \"koct5 e£oxr|v\" read as κατ᾽ ἐξοχήν; \"geAvöhnlich\" read \"gewöhnlich\".","\"Daher die immer Sprichwörter reden wollen, pöbelhaft\" kept elliptical as in the German.","Wissenschaft = science; Weisheit = wisdom; gemein = common."]],"dohna-s15-046":["With all merely subjective grounds we are always passive, e.g., with inclination, with habit — with the propensity to imitation. The propensity to prejudice is propensity to mechanism in the use of reason. There are sentences that already announce themselves as prejudices.",["Gebrauch = use; Vernunft = reason; Hang = propensity."]],"dohna-s15-047":["One can divide prejudices in general",["Breaks off; the division continues in the following passages."]],"dohna-s15-048":["1. into the prejudice, *praejudicium*, *hominis*;",[]],"dohna-s15-049":["2. into that of the occasion of our (judgments) a) *loci vel* b) *temporis*.",["\"(Urteile)\" is parenthetical in the source."]],"dohna-s15-050":["The *praejudicium hominis* is again twofold:",[]],"dohna-s15-051":["1. *praejudicium vel personae* (for a person);",[]],"dohna-s15-052":["2. *praejudicium — multitudinis* (for the multitude) — it is peculiar to women and also suits them quite well. [What all the world says is true.] One who has the *praejudicium multitudinis* mistrusts his reason. [The prejudice of esteem, e.g., of antiquity, which has its ground e.g. in gratitude.] [Prejudices of esteem, e.g., for great men, often do much harm to the sciences, in that through their greatness they frighten others away from emulation — everyone who might want to choose this field for cultivation despairs of reaching this ideal or even of ever surpassing it. — We sometimes admire from contempt, in that for the slight representation that we had of someone, what he has done was always much. Thus Mercier admires the Germans, thus one admires women who deliver something scientific.",["Stray \"j\" in the OCR dropped; the opening bracket of the last section is closed only in the next passage (…\"Veränderungen.]\").","\"Frauenzimmer\" rendered \"women\"; Ansehen rendered \"esteem\"; Größe in \"große Männer\"/\"ihre Größe\" rendered \"great\"/\"greatness\" (not \"magnitude\").","\"indem für die geringe Vorstellung … immer viel war\" kept as literal as English allows; the syntax is obscure in the German. \"odergar\" read \"oder gar\"; \"Frauen-7.immer\" read \"Frauenzimmer\"; \"hefern\" read \"liefern\"."]],"dohna-s15-053":["A difference between admiring and wondering. We admire the ancients not on account of the degree of cognition, but how, relative to the few aids, they have got so far. — Learnedness. Acquaintance with the ancients. Of the respect that it deserves, we always let much fall upon the ancients themselves. — The prejudice for the ancients has many causes. One does not know the new, does not want to know it, from laziness. In matters of fine art the ancients are really still now models — in other objects much progress has been made since. In the aesthetic, in matters of sensation the ancients could bring it further than we because of their civil state. — Speeches to a cultivated (refined) people with regard to the constitution of the state had to attain a high degree of perfection <— energy —>. — Here is a foundation. The opinion thus does not rest on mere prejudice. If these works were lost, we would not get them back, because we cannot come into this state. Only a dead language can become a model of presentation, living ones undergo changes too often.]",["The closing bracket belongs to the bracket opened at the end of the preceding passage; OCR \"]!\" read \"]\".","Erkenntnis = cognition; Kenntnis = acquaintance; kennen = know; Gegenstand = object; Zustand = state; Grad = degree; Vollkommenheit = perfection; Meinung = opinion.","Bewundern/Verwundern = admire/wonder (be astonished); \"bürgerlichen Zustande\" = \"civil state\".","\"Von der Achtung, welche sie verdient\": \"sie\" has no clear antecedent (perhaps Gelehrsamkeit); kept as it stands. \"Darstellung\" = presentation."]],"dohna-s15-054":["The *praejudicium multitudinis* too is twofold",[]],"dohna-s15-055":["1. *praejudicium eruditorum* [the prejudice for the scholars], thus the common man trusts the scholars.",["gemein = common."]],"dohna-s15-056":["2. *praejudicium sensus communis*. The common understanding is a kind of sense (*praejudicium empirismi*) that to a certain extent has an intuition. [Often scholars too have a particular respect for it.] Yet in medicine it is the opposite, because they namely believe that it is dangerous to make use of learnedness at random f. [they believe namely that learned physicians do not devise anything at all from f experience f].",["The \"f\" signs are OCR residues (perhaps footnote or deletion marks); left in place, unread. Brackets (\"{(…))\") read as parentheses.","Verstand = understanding; Sinn = sense; Anschauung = intuition; Erfahrung = experience; \"auf gut Glück\" = \"at random\" (literally on good luck).","\"sie\" in \"weil sie nämlich glauben\" has no clear antecedent; kept."]],"dohna-s15-057":["The author now treats",["Breaks off; the heading follows in the next passage."]],"dohna-s15-058":["Of logical egoistry",["Egoisterei rendered \"egoistry\" to keep it apart from Egoism (\"egoism\") in the next passage."]],"dohna-s15-059":["[Logical egoism is a selfish prejudice.] This is not mere self-conceit, but a kind of logical principle, which holds as dispensable the criterion of truth, to compare one's opinions with those of other human beings. [The mathematician can never risk it — *Vitium subreptionis*, from subjective grounds. — ] The principle of the indifference etc. of the judgments of others in comparison with my own is the principle of logical egoism. It is unfair to condemn people for that they are supposed to keep all their judgments to themselves. For they must communicate themselves, if they are not to lose the strong criterion of truth, to compare their judgments with the judgments of others.",["OCR \"üire\" read \"ihre\". Grundsatz and Prinzip both rendered \"principle\".","\"dafür, daß sie … behalten sollen\" kept literal (\"for that they are supposed to keep\"); the German can mean \"for having to keep\" or \"for saying that they should keep\".","Kriterium = criterion; Meinung = opinion; Eigendünkel = self-conceit; unbillig = unfair; kondemnieren = condemn."]],"dohna-s15-060":["Further the author speaks of the prejudice in favor of another — this is exactly the opposite of the one mentioned before. For one sometimes elevates a great man in order to conceal one's own weakness from one's contemporaries — it is a means to comparison and to the removal of the difference. Such an idol, which one has raised to admiration, e.g., Aristotle, has harmed humanity extraordinarily and has always kept it bound. For no one believed that this man could ever be surpassed, whence one only ever imitated. Thus the greatness of one man harmed posterity [vid. also p. 57], which did not have the courage to surpass him.",["\"ander n\" in the OCR is read as \"anderen\" (\"another\"); the OCR break is silently corrected.","\"Vorurteil\" = prejudice; \"Mittel\" = means; \"Menschheit\" = humanity.","\"vid.\" (Latin, \"see\") kept as in the source; the square brackets are the editors' and are carried over."]],"dohna-s15-061":["We now come specifically to the prejudice of antiquity, *praejudicium antiquitatis*. We have the prejudice that at the time when the ancients lived, nature had not yet fallen into such decay. There are also still other causes. The high esteem for antiquity takes place preeminently among those who have studied the ancients, <without> which studying one cannot lay claim at all to the name of a scholar — among others from mere imitation. [A provisional judgment accepted as decisive is called a prejudice.] We also admire the ancients because in the childhood of the human race they had already brought it so far. It is further in keeping with gratitude that we praise them on account of their great merits and forget and overlook their weaknesses. — We ourselves may indeed someday become ancients, so to speak, with f, if we leave behind such products as they did, etc.",["\"vorläufiges Urteil\" = provisional judgment; \"Vorurteil\" = prejudice.","\"mit f\" in \"Alte sozusagen mit f werden\" is an OCR remnant, too garbled to read; left as \"with f\"; similar to the stray \"f\" elsewhere.","\"Verdienste\" rendered \"merits\" (not in the glossary)."]],"dohna-s15-062":["The prejudice of novelty springs from inclination. For the first who communicates something of the sort finds a merit in it. The true provisional judgment would be this, that writings which have preserved themselves through such long times merely on account of their inner worth and excellence must still have something that is lasting and durable.",["OCR \"etAvas\" corrected to \"etwas\"; \"Avas\" type errors for \"w\" are silently corrected throughout.","\"Neigung\" = inclination."]],"dohna-s15-063":["[If we have attained certain cognitions with effort and these, in interconnection with others in a certain combination into a whole, make up a system, then we have a predilection for it. — This flows from the nature of the human being, we f infer truth from the interconnection. An assumed ground can well serve for the explanation of many sequels without being the right ground.]",["\"Erkenntnisse\" = cognitions; \"Zusammenhang\" = interconnection; \"Verbindung\" = combination; \"Folgen\" = sequels (Folge); \"Grund\" = ground.","Stray \"f\" before \"schließen\" is an OCR remnant; left as it stands.","\"Erklärung\" rendered \"explanation\"; \"Vorliebe\" = predilection."]],"dohna-s15-064":["The prejudice of the accepted theorem, *praejudicium systematis*. Against this much is written. A system permits a provisional favorable judgment to its advantage. The fitness of a proposition for the whole system proves the truth <of it>.",["\"Lehrsatz\" = theorem, though the Latin heading says *systematis*; the German is kept as it stands.","\"Satz\" = proposition. Angle brackets carried over."]],"dohna-s15-065":["There are many *volontairs* in the sciences, who (so to speak) want to stand under no command of the school. We must not declaim too much against systems. Some French writers have done this. Afterward there is a prejudice of <lazy> trust, of ease.",["*volontairs* is the French-derived word in the source (\"volunteers\"), kept in italics.","\"Wissenschaften\" = sciences."]],"dohna-s15-066":["The universal validity of a rule can only be judged from rules *in abstracto*. E.g., one wants to have a principle about the concept of the lie. About this one must judge *in abstracto*. For if one wanted to look at individual cases, some lies could be useful, e.g., the proposal of Themistocles — Aristides. But every faithlessness is shameful.",["OCR \"in6i abstracto\" read as \"in abstracto\"; \"Avollte\" read as \"wollte\".","\"Allgemeingültigkeit\" = universal validity (from gültig = valid); \"beurteilen\" = judge; \"urteilen\" = judge; \"Begriff\" = concept; \"Prinzip\" = principle."]],"dohna-s15-067":["The prejudice of shallowness. It consists in one's not going into the depths of cognition — namely to subordinate marks. [For a good provisional judgment genius is absolutely required.]",["\"Merkmale\" = marks (*notae*); \"Erkenntnis\" = cognition."]],"dohna-s15-068":["Hereupon the author speaks of the opposed prejudices: namely, when one has distrust toward certain things. [Thus a prejudice against the old can be thought, likewise one against the new — distrust of all human cognition is called misology.] It can also have reference to oneself. It is then annihilating for the human being if it frightens his courage back from all and every labor; but if it is only so f, that in every business we distrust our own powers, then that is the most glorious situation of the human mind.",["\"Kräfte\" = powers (Kraft); \"Gemüt\" = mind.","\"so ist f ,\" contains an OCR remnant \"f\", left as it stands; the sense of the clause is therefore uncertain.","\"Mißtrauen\" = distrust."]],"dohna-s15-069":["Now we come to the doctrine",["OCR \"Avir\" read as \"wir\"; \"Lehre\" = doctrine. The sentence breaks off as printed, continued by the next passage."]],"dohna-s15-070":["Of the Probable",["\"Wahrscheinlichen\" = the probable (cf. Wahrscheinlichkeit = probability)."]],"dohna-s15-071":["[A chapter in which logic can never get far.] When I say: something is probable (even if it should be only subjective), this concerns quality. But if I ask: how great is the probability, then I consider it according to degree. The relation of the grounds for the truth to the grounds of the opposite (*verisimilitudo*). But the relation of the grounds for sufficient truth is the *probabilitas*. If I have only one ground of taking to be true and none for the proof of the opposite, then this is already *verisimilitudo*. [The *verisimile* is the apparent — *probabile* acceptable. The judgment about the *verisimilitudo* rests on the comparison of the judgments for the truth with the grounds of the opposite. In the case of probability the grounds of taking to be true make up more than half — they outweigh.] The sufficient ground is the sum total of all cases that can only occur. Only in mathematics can one specify the grounds of taking to be true determinately.",["OCR \"Avorin\" read as \"worin\"; \"Wahrscheinlich -keit\" rejoined.","\"Probabilität\" is given in German form in the source; rendered \"*probabilitas*\" in the Latin sense for consistency with the Latin forms; the German reads \"Probabilität\" (\"probability\" would also be possible).","\"Fürwahrhalten\" = taking to be true; \"zureichend\" = sufficient; \"Summe\" rendered \"sum total\" (Inbegriff) for consistency.","\"Vergleichung der Urteile für die Wahrheit zu den Gründen\" kept literally; \"Urteile\" may be a slip for \"Gründe\" but is not emended.","\"Grad\" = degree; \"Qualität\" = quality."]],"dohna-s16-001":["Hour, Monday < f >",["The sign \"f\" within angle brackets is an OCR remnant, left as it stands; the original may have been a date or hour mark."]],"dohna-s16-002":["[All probability we find *numerando* by counting together or *ponderando*. For the first way homogeneity is required, e.g., a throw of dice, just as easily 3 sixes as 3 eights. I am to throw 8 with 2 dice. Here 5 cases are possible. The probability can be counted off, is = g56 of certainty. But when something is to be guessed, e.g., a testimony, then the grounds are to be weighed, e.g., a human being of good upbringing, etc. Where the grounds are numbered, I can say: it is probable, but where they are weighed, only: it is probable to me. Likewise one can never say: this is morally certain, but <I am> morally certain, i.e., it rests with me on practical grounds, otherwise one may not use the word moral at all — otherwise one says better: certainty from physical grounds — theoretical.]",["\"g56\" is an OCR garble of a fraction (probably 5/36); I translate only what can be made out and do not correct it.","\"Gewißheit\" = certainty; \"Wahrscheinlichkeit\" = probability; \"Gründe\" = grounds.","\"<ich bin)\" has mismatched brackets in the OCR; rendered \"<I am>\".","\"Zeugnis\" rendered \"testimony\"; \"gleichartig\" = homogeneous."]],"dohna-s16-003":["A proposition can be called probable only insofar as it lies on the way to certainty and, increased by homogeneous grounds, produces certainty. Judgments that go to the supersensible (otherwise also to the overreaching, the transcendent, in opposition to the immanent, which is grasped with the senses, remains in the representation). Thus in metaphysical things, in general in all things *a priori*, there is no probability in the theoretical. Here we can only know or it is nothing. For all our conjectures cannot be brought to certainty on this way. Not even then when several different grounds are present. This namely indicates nothing further than that there are many insufficient grounds, which can never yield a universally valid proof, and it is always a proof of uncertainty when, instead of naming one sufficient ground, one gives several that are not so. Probability is approximation to certainty, and several grounds, if they are valid, ought to be combined together in one proof. In like cases, where we are on the way to certainty, there is lacking only still a *complementum*. —",["OCR \"hegt\" read as \"liegt\"; \"Wissen\" rendered \"know\" (verb *wissen*).","\"Übersinnliche\" = supersensible; \"Überschwengliche\" = overreaching (not in glossary); \"Vorstellung\" = representation; \"Beweis\" = proof; \"gelten/gültig\" = valid.","\"Urteile, die aufs Übersinnliche gehen (...)\" is a fragment without a main verb in the German; kept as it stands.","\"in den gleichen Fällen\" kept literally as \"in like cases\"; the sense of \"gleichen\" (same/like) is ambiguous."]],"dohna-s16-004":["[ad § 176 p. 55] The author still speaks of doubts. We obtain a doubt — *dubitatio* — subjectively, when our taking to be true is not determined (stands in *aequilibrio*). In the *suspensio judicii* the grounds must still leave a doubt. The calling into doubt (*dubium*) as objective ground of taking to be true is the judgment that I make about truth, or a relation of the judgment to the subject, insofar as it takes something to be true. — Calling into doubt is an objective ground for taking the opposite to be true, however small it may be. [The state of taking to be true with consciousness of the insufficiency is called the state of doubt.] An objection is a *dubium* of which I am conscious, that is, insofar as it is stated in concepts of the understanding — insofar as it is represented obscurely, a scruple. [From this scruple a determinate doubt can arise.] This can in some cases be raised to the clarity of an objection. There are frequent objections against judgments of the understanding that lack distinctness [one has answered them, but they are not yet answered], hence the treatment of a scruple requires development. One must show what the other takes offense at. [When this becomes clear, it contributes very much to the perfection of taking to be true.] Often one cuts the knot instead of untying it, e.g. in the dispute over the assertion that the world is the best, the opponents adduce that there is so much evil in it, especially for the human being, that the world could impossibly be called good, and one is accustomed, without uncovering their falsity, <to show that the world, (the work) of an All-wise, cannot be bad. [Leibniz answers the objection thus: there is once an author, whom I cannot think otherwise than as the best, wisest being.] *Difficultatem differre, non auferre*, when difficulties, even if removed through grounds of cognition, are subject to like difficulties. [E.g. the fall into sin through the devil. (Dice — 36 F.)] Another expression: *obnubilare, non diluere* — to obscure the truth still more.",["Fürwahrhalten rendered 'taking to be true' (Fürwahrhalten in glossary); 'für wahr halten' as the verb form 'to take to be true'.","Zweifel = doubt; Bezweifelung = 'calling into doubt' (Latin *dubium* given beside it); Einwurf = objection; Skrupel = scruple.","Gründe = grounds; Erkenntnisgründe = 'grounds of cognition' (Erkenntnis = cognition).","'suspensio judicü' in OCR read as *suspensio judicii*.","The angle bracket '<' before 'zu zeigen' is left as printed (editorial apparatus); '(zu)' appears to have been OCR-merged, so 'to show' is translated; no closing bracket appears in the passage.","'Würfel — 36 F.' is obscure, probably a note on a lost example; translated literally ('F.' left as is).","'Allweisen' = 'an All-wise'; 'Wesen' = 'being'."]],"dohna-s16-005":["[Hour, Tuesday]",[]],"dohna-s16-006":["We now come to the state of doubt — state of the mind, insofar as it is conscious that it is undetermined with regard to taking to be true. Dogmatism and skepticism are opposed to one another. [The skeptical method is that of postponing one's assent, since one is at once open to the counter-grounds.] General skepticism is the prejudice of assuming all cognition whatsoever as uncertain. Dogmatism is the prejudice of assuming the (complete) certainty of cognition, without holding the critique of our faculty of reason to be necessary [ — detrimental to reason]. [Dogmatic skepticism, which declares all certainty impossible.]",["Gemüt = mind; Vernunftvermögen = 'faculty of reason'; Erkenntnis = cognition.","Gegengründe = 'counter-grounds'; Vorurteil = 'prejudice'."]],"dohna-s16-007":["[There is no *logice dijudicata* — here there are not completely settled truths. We must at least always answer the possible doubts against them.",["The opening bracket is not closed in this passage; it presumably closes in the next (dohna-s16-008).","*logice dijudicata* kept in Latin; the German 'hier' refers to an unspecified place, left as is."]],"dohna-s16-008":["Criticism is the principle of not taking judgments a priori to be true before we have compared them with the laws of our faculty of reason. Only in cognitions a priori through concepts is this method applicable and necessary.]",["Kritizism = 'criticism'; *a priori* italicized as a Latin expression.","The closing bracket is carried over without an opening one in this passage."]],"dohna-s16-009":["j Mathematics and natural science do not need the critique of our reason. We do not need there to investigate where the boundaries of these cognitions lie, because everything can be demonstrated through intuition. — The prejudice of dogmatism is grounded on the success of many cognitions a priori without critique through reason. Here it succeeds splendidly for it, and hence one believes that it will succeed for it in other cases as well. One cannot hold it against Plato that through reflection on mathematics he came upon enthusiastic ideas — if only the Platonists afterward had not carried it too far. Our concepts are, as already mentioned above, mathematical and philosophical — the former confirm themselves by themselves through intuition, which cannot take place at all with the latter. To clarify means merely to analyze one's concepts, but to extend, when one goes beyond the common concepts. If one wants to extend one's cognition of reason through mere concepts, then one is already, if no further critique takes place in doing so, a dogmatist. [Hypothesis: inference from the sufficiency of a ground for given consequences that this ground is the correct one — this assumption, in order to be able to explain those consequences from it, ad p. 68.]",["Initial 'j' is an OCR remnant of uncertain value; left as it stands.","Anschauung = intuition; Grenzen = boundaries; Erläutern = 'to clarify' (Erläuterung = clarification); erweitern = extend.","Vernunfterkenntnis = 'cognition of reason'; Schluß = 'inference'; Zulänglichkeit = 'sufficiency' (cf. zureichend); Folgen = 'consequences'.","*ad p. 68* kept in Latin; 'schwärmerische' rendered 'enthusiastic'."]],"dohna-s16-010":["Very often the counter-proposition has as many grounds as the proposition itself. An example — Proposition: The world has a beginning; counter-proposition: The world can have no beginning. What then would the being that created it have — made through the whole of eternity? Metaphysics is extension of our cognition through concepts a priori — through construction of these — mathematics. Our reason in pure use is dialectical. —",["Gegensatz = 'counter-proposition' (Satz = proposition); Wesen = being; rein = pure; Gebrauch = use.","The sentence on metaphysics and mathematics is obscure and is left as it stands."]],"dohna-s16-011":["The strictest skeptic, who is no longer critical at all, can rightly be called a dogmatic skeptic, although it seems contradictory. One can easily catch them. They want to demonstrate that no truth can be demonstrated. They refute the arguments of the dogmatists and yet assume certainty — in the end they say: everything in the world is uncertain, even this, that everything in the world is uncertain; they called it a *cathartic* or purgative, which purges itself along with the rest. The skeptics usually had much dialectical acuteness, as one can see from their writings. [Persiflage — Plato's students Speusippus, Arcesilaus, Carneades skeptics — partial Pyrrho) — nothing {the academic doubt).]",["'Kathartikon' rendered 'cathartic' (Greek-derived technical word); 'Purganz' = 'purgative'.","The final bracketed note is garbled by OCR ('Piatos' read as Platos; unbalanced parentheses and braces); the wording 'partial Pyrrho' is uncertain and 'nothing' is as printed. Parentheses/braces carried over as they stand.","'sie' in 'Man kann sie leicht fangen' is left as 'them' without noun supplied."]],"dohna-s16-012":["The mathematician must never investigate why his propositions are a priori. Criticism is the middle between dogmatism and skepticism, the principle of a rightful trust in one's use of reason. Transcendent is that which can be demonstrated through no experience or sensible evidence. For the very same reason one can never come upon an error, only in the case when one can prove the opposite oneself. In these cases critique is therefore absolutely necessary, not in mathematics, because here there are actual facts. — Skepticism is therefore indeed reprehensible — but the skeptical method, of always testing and investigating a thing further, has very many advantages — the doubt of postponement — *indagare*. — [This is the doubt which has the most useful consequences for the investigation of truth.] — The academic doubt was dogmatic. For they wanted nothing to be certain. The Pyrrhonian doubt seems to have been more a doubt of postponement | | . At least it certainly was not so general as the academic.",["OCR 'kerne' read as 'keine'; 'verwerf lieh' as 'verwerflich'.","The sentence 'Man kann aus eben dem Grunde nie auf einen Irrtum stoßen, nur in dem Fall...' is kept literally and is obscure.","Aufschub = 'postponement'; *Facta* in German text rendered 'facts'.","The signs | in 'gewesen | zu | sein' are apparatus; carried over in the English at the corresponding place (the German word order differs: 'gewesen zu sein' = 'to have been')."]],"dohna-s16-013":["[| ad | § 181 P. 57] Hypothesis — presupposition. It is a judgment for inferring, from the sufficiency of a judgment for given consequences, to the actuality of the ground. Every hypothesis is in the end mere opinion — from the sufficiency of a ground for a given consequence one cannot yet infer to the truth of the proposition. For other grounds could indeed also be sufficient for the (consequences). [The sufficiency of the ground from certain given consequences yet gives no perfect inference to its truth.]",["Wirklichkeit = actuality; Meinung = opinion; zureichend = sufficient (Zulänglichkeit = 'sufficiency' also).","'Folge' (singular) = 'consequence'; Folgen = 'consequences'; the parenthesized '(Folgen)' is the editor's reading, kept.","'ad' is flanked by apparatus signs |; kept in place."]],"dohna-s16-014":["We need hypotheses and cannot dispense with them at all in natural science. [Hypothesis is more than opinion, actual presupposition.] Only, what is particular, metaphysics also has hypotheses — indeed not in theoretical respect, but yet in practical.",["'was besonders ist' rendered literally as 'what is particular' (besonder = particular); the clause is awkward in the German and is kept so.","Voraussetzung = 'presupposition'."]],"dohna-s16-015":["| 1. | The ground that I assume *per hypothesin*, the possibility of it must be certain [otherwise one goes entirely with empty fictions], e.g. central fire. [Cucumbers in the honey pot. Coming wants to have found Isis.]",["*per hypothesin* kept in Latin; 'Coming' is the OCR reading (name uncertain, possibly garbled); the bracketed remarks are obscure and left as printed.","Möglichkeit = possibility; Zentralfeuer = 'central fire'."]],"dohna-s16-016":["2. The consequence from the assumed ground must be given, this must therefore be actual — the cause need only be possible.",["Folge here = 'consequence'; Ursache = 'cause'; wirklich = actual."]],"dohna-s16-017":["3. The consequence (of the consequences) from its cause must likewise be entirely certain. How else can one infer from it?",["'Konsequenz' = 'consequence' and 'Folgen' also = 'consequences'; one English word stands for both German words (Konsequenz, Folge).","'(der Folgen)' parenthesis is the editor's, kept; 'schließen' = 'infer'."]],"dohna-s16-018":["The 4th *requisitum*, the unity of the assumed ground in its sufficiency with regard to the consequences. [— All consequences must flow from this one assumed ground. If it does not work, then one takes a new | hypothesis | to help, *hypothesis subsidiaria* — (thus one seeks . . .) e.g. certain medicines cure certain illnesses. The sufficiency of a ground for all consequences, however, remains uncertain, and so does every hypothesis.]",["Folgen rendered 'consequences' here (not 'sequels'), since they are what flows from a ground; Folgerung is also 'consequence' in the glossary.","Zulänglichkeit / Zulänglichkeit: 'sufficiency' (glossary: zureichend = sufficient); Zulänglichkeit is not itself in the glossary.","OCR garble in '(so sucht f man . . .f)': stray 'f' marks removed; the dots are as in the source.","The vertical bars | | are the Academy apparatus and are kept."]],"dohna-s16-019":["A hypothesis, so long as it remains a hypothesis, never attains complete certainty, i.e. none that is apodictic (where the opposite would be impossible); empirical certainty it can indeed attain. We speak here only of hypotheses of theoretical cognition. But there are also morally practical hypotheses, which are necessary, in that they constantly occur according to certain laws. But a *hypothesis* in a theoretical aim is never necessary. In a moral aim, however, e.g. duty is objectively and happiness subjectively necessary, etc., certain.",["'cl. h.' in the OCR corrected to 'd. h.' (i.e.).","Absicht rendered 'aim' (glossary).","The final sentence is syntactically broken in the German ('usw. gewiß'); kept as it stands. 'Gewißheit' = certainty, 'gewiß' = certain.","'Erkenntnis' = cognition; 'beständig' rendered 'constantly'; 'geschehen' rendered 'occur'. The clause 'indem sie beständig nach gewissen Gesetzen geschehen' is obscure in its reference and is kept so."]],"dohna-s16-020":["[ad §184 P. 58] On persuasion and conviction, [*persuasio male signif icat . . . .*]",["Überredung = persuasion; Überzeugung = conviction (glossary).","The Latin in brackets is garbled by OCR ('signif icat', 'j]'); kept as read, and the continuation is broken off in the source (dots)."]],"dohna-s16-021":["The distinction between persuasion and conviction is awkward. *Persuasio* is properly the taking to be true from grounds, without precisely investigating whether they are objective or subjective. The taking to be true of a proposition, without being able to distinguish whether it occurs from subjective or from objective grounds. Even an upright person as judge is unable to distinguish the subjective grounds of his judgment from the objective ones, if he is disposed favorably toward the cause of his friend.",["'Persuasion' is left in the German as 'Persuasion' and is rendered in italics Latin form here, as Kant's own word; Überredung is 'persuasion' too, so one English word stands for both.","Fürwahrhalten = taking to be true; Grund = ground; Satz = proposition; Urteil = judgment.","'mißlich' rendered 'awkward'; other options such as 'precarious' are possible.","'Rechtschaffner' rendered 'upright person'; 'vorteilhaft für die Sache ... gesinnt' is kept literal."]],"dohna-s16-022":["We have already spoken of science above. [A cognition is systematic if the combination which it has into a whole is necessary. A system is a whole, insofar as the consciousness of its totality is possible only according to principles. — ] It | namely, science | is properly the sum total of cognitions, insofar as they are combined in a system. It is not precisely required for this that they be rational. But one must also carefully distinguish art from science. It is *in sensu stricto*: that skill to produce something which, even if one knows it, one still cannot make. Mechanical art would be that which one already can do if one only knows it, or handicraft art. Art, however, in distinction from common skill, is that defined above *in sensu stricto*.",["Wissenschaft = science; Erkenntnis = cognition; Verbindung = combination; Bewußtsein = consciousness; Totalität (Allheit in glossary) rendered 'totality'; Inbegriff = sum total; Prinzipien = principles.","'seil,' in the OCR is read as 'scil.' (namely); editors' bracket | | kept.","'gemeine Geschicklichkeit' rendered 'common skill' (gemein = common).","The final sentence is elliptical in German ('die oben definierte') and is kept so; 'rational' stands for 'rational' as printed."]],"dohna-s17-001":["[Hour, Monday]",["'Stunde' (lecture hour) rendered 'Hour'; the bracket is the editors'."]],"dohna-s17-002":["Art is the system of heterogeneous skills. Besides the mathematical or intuitive and the philosophical or discursive, there is also a certainty that is not objective. The ground is properly subjective — it is namely a practically valid certainty, which one must call not precisely moral but rather pragmatic, if namely one assumes something not so according to rules of morality but more according to rules of prudence. A technical rule is a rule of art, a pragmatic one of prudence, a moral one of morality, of virtue — duty. Under practical certainty moral certainty also belongs. —",["anschaulich/intuitiv: 'intuitive' renders 'intuitiv' (Latinate); Anschauung is 'intuition' elsewhere in this set, so one English word covers both.","gültig = valid; Gewißheit = certainty; Klugheit = prudence; Sittlichkeit = morality.","'ungleichartiger' rendered 'heterogeneous'; the dash before 'es ist nämlich' and 'Pflicht' is kept."]],"dohna-s17-003":["The author speaks here of a mathematical certainty of the first and of the 2nd rank. But there is here no distinction other than that between mathematical and discursive certainty. [The merely absolute necessity is still far from mathematical certainty — 2-2=4. For although what is true cannot be still truer, there nevertheless always remains a difference in the degree of certainty, because there it is proved only through concepts, but here through intuition.]",["OCR 'kernen' read as 'keinen'. The formula '2-2=4' is as printed (presumably a slip for 2+2=4); left unemended.","Begriffe = concepts; Anschauung = intuition; Grad = degree; notwendig = necessary; bewiesen = proved (Beweis = proof).","'Rang' rendered 'rank'; 'dort ... hier' left unresolved as in the German."]],"dohna-s17-004":["*Hypothesis* signifies in most cases a merely arbitrary position. *Thesis*, a firm proposition.",["bedeutet = signifies (glossary); Position left as 'position' (cf. setzen = posit); Satz = proposition.","Hypothesis and Thesis kept in Latin italics as technical terms."]],"dohna-s17-005":["Now the author comes to the section",["The passage breaks off, continuing in the next heading."]],"dohna-s17-006":["On proofs",["Beweisen = proofs (Beweis = proof)."]],"dohna-s17-007":["The *constitutiva* of every proof are:",["'Constitutiva' kept as Latin in italics."]],"dohna-s17-008":["1. The conducting of the proof, *probatio*,",["Beweisführung rendered 'conducting of the proof' to keep the compound visible."]],"dohna-s17-009":["2. that which is to be proved, *probandum*. It is the *materiale* of the proof.",["*Materiale* kept in Latin form; Materie is 'matter' in the glossary but Materiale is not rendered 'matter' here."]],"dohna-s17-010":["3. The relation between the two, that a correct consequence follow from it: e.g. everything extended is divisible, all bodies are extended, therefore all bodies are divisible, and afterwards the further inferences, etc.",["Konsequenz = consequence; Schlüsse = inferences (Schluß = inference); ausgedehnt/Ausdehnung = extended/extension.","OCR 'fernem' read as 'fernern'."]],"dohna-s17-011":["Unprovable can be called, first, that which is wholly false and that which is immediately certain. The latter propositions are *indemonstrabel*, the mediate ones *demonstrabel*.",["'Unerweislich' rendered 'unprovable'. 'indemonstrabel / demonstrabel' kept as the transcript's Latinate forms, in italics.","unmittelbar / mittelbar = immediate / mediate."]],"dohna-s18-001":["[Hour]",["Editors' bracket."]],"dohna-s18-002":["To prove means to take something to be true mediately for the sake of a ground. We can divide all proofs into direct [or ostensive] and indirect or apagogic. The latter are those which one (derives) from the falsity of the opposite. The apagogic proofs have almost more *intuitus* in them than the direct ones, because the contradiction is always striking. [Apodictic proofs — with consciousness of necessity — *demonstratio*, a proof combined with intuition.]",["Fürwahrhalten = taking to be true; Widerspruch = contradiction; Anschauung = intuition, with Kant's Latin *intuitus* kept.","'apagogisch' rendered 'apagogic'; parentheses around 'herleitet' are as in the source."]],"dohna-s18-003":["One can prove much apagogically *ex concessis*, namely when the other has already conceded something. These are the *argumenta ad hominem*. There are many such proofs in mathematics. They are always excellent.",["'concediert' rendered 'conceded'."]],"dohna-s18-004":["Now we want to see how they fit with philosophy. If an assumed concept itself contains contradiction, then the predicate too can conflict with the concept, and the opposite of the predicate also. [The apagogic proofs are very awkward in philosophy.] One can prove quite different propositions at once apagogically. This occurs very often in philosophy. Someone can refute his opponent apagogically and be refuted by him just as forcefully, so that he too is brought *ad absurdum*. The human will is free. This proposition is indispensable in morals. For if human beings could not act otherwise than they act, then all laws would indeed be in vain. The opinion of the opposite of this proposition is called fatalism, that all human beings act only according to certain laws determined by nature. Priestley and Price had a long dispute over it. Each brought the other *ad absurdum* and yet nothing came to an end. [Neither proves his proposition directly, but always only the falsity of the opposite. That can easily deceive in philosophy, for one can just as well err oneself in the process, even if one proves that the other errs — very easily both can be wrong.] The given multitude of all parts is finite or infinite, i.e. all parts of a body taken together, and this we can never do, for every body is infinitely divisible, one would never be finished. Hence a taking-together of all parts is a *contradictio in adjecto*. —",["Begriff = concept; Prädikat = predicate; Meinung = opinion; Gesetz = law; Menge = multitude; Teil = part; endlich/unendlich = finite/infinite.","OCR 'directe' kept as the transcript's form, rendered 'directly'; 'Contradictio in adjecto' kept in Latin italics.","'ad absurdum' in Latin italics. 'Zusammennehmung' rendered 'taking-together'. 'Wille' = will.","'daß er auch ad absurdum gebracht wird' kept as a consecutive clause without comma resolution; the sense of 'wollen wir sehn' is literal."]],"dohna-s18-005":["The result of all this is that in philosophy one cannot make use of the apagogic proofs, because one cannot here, namely, exhibit them in intuition.",["OCR 'närnlich' read as 'nämlich'. 'bedienen' rendered 'make use of'; Gebrauch is 'use' in the glossary.","'dartun' rendered 'exhibit'; the literal sense is 'show / demonstrate'."]],"dohna-s18-006":["An aesthetic demonstration would be that which is called so, which occurs through a *bon mot* [but is not always true].",["The syntax 'die genannt werden' is awkward in the German and is kept so. 'Bonmot' kept in French italics."]],"dohna-s18-007":["Now we come to the section:",[]],"dohna-s18-008":["On the sources of our cognition",["Erkenntnis = cognition."]],"dohna-s18-009":["[The author has 3, experience, reason and belief.] They are not 3-fold, but only 2-fold: 1. rational, 2. empirical. [There is a belief of reason, but merely in practical respect — this is therefore no separate source but always only reason, which is either theoretical or practical. All experience is 2-fold, immediate or mediate. This latter way is historical belief.] As far as belief is concerned, one can also call the historical empirical: it is always grounded on the testimony and the experience of another. A rational belief goes to such things as one cannot know at all. But on the belief of reason no theoretical cognition is grounded: it is sufficient merely in practical aim. A comparison and combination of truth into a cognition. Experience is an operation of the understanding, hence perception is far from sufficing. Historical belief is connection of the experiences of others with my own. [What matters now is to determine what entitles me to esteem mediate experience just as much as immediate — on what authority rests.] The witness is authentic who is an eyewitness, *testis oculatus*, [who himself has immediate experience]. He is unimpeachable if he can know the truth and wants to tell it. Thus capacity and integrity are the two *requisita*.",["Glaube = belief; Vernunftglaube = 'belief of reason'; Absicht = 'aim'; Rücksicht = 'respect'; Art = 'way'.","hinreichend (sufficient) and zureichen (to suffice) are both rendered with 'sufficient/suffice'.","'Capazität' (Latinate) and 'Integrität' are rendered 'capacity' and 'integrity'; 'capacity' is also the glossary word for Fähigkeit/Vermögen.","'Eine Vergleichung und Verbindung der Wahrheit zu einer Erkenntnis' is left as obscure as the German; the syntax is elliptical and has no finite verb.","wissen = 'know'; Wahrnehmung = 'perception'; Verknüpfung = 'connection'; Verbindung = 'combination'."]],"dohna-s18-010":["*Testis auritus*. In the series of such witnesses there is often nothing but hearsay — *rumor* <(*sine capite*) — the more of them there are, the more uncertain is the hearsay. It is as the copy of the copy. [The longer the chain, the more uncertain. Tradition, where the first witness is unknown. If you believed, you would not investigate the hearsay back to the first ground. The competence is not at all to be ascertained. — ] But the greater the multitude of coordinated witnesses is, the more credible is the hearsay. — It is not the best means to assess the integrity of a person whose character one does not otherwise know from whether she has had an interest in it or not. Many sorts of rules of prudence belong here as well, which one may not, however, adduce in logic.",["Sage rendered 'hearsay'; the Latin *rumor* stands beside it in the text.","The sign '<(' before *sine capite* is carried over as printed; its function is not clear.","Tüchtigkeit = 'competence'; Menge = 'multitude'; Mittel = 'means'; beurteilen = 'assess'.","'Wenn du glaubtest' could be read as 'if you believed' (belief in the sense of Glaube) or merely 'if you took it to be so'; the English keeps the verb 'believed'.","'daraus zu beurteilen, ob' is kept as literal as possible; the sense of the construction is slightly loose in the German."]],"dohna-s18-011":["We now want to touch on the concept of belief, insofar as it is not a source of cognition, but a mere judgment of reason, insofar as it no longer has a theoretical, but merely a practical aim. [Unbelieving with regard to the practical — to belief of reason, incredulous with regard to the theoretical, to historical belief. For unbelieving there is no word in Latin. Unbelieving is he who out of moral grounds (which are sufficient as incentives of morality) does not want to assume something, e.g. the existence of a highest being. — ]",["OCR 'ungläu bisch' read as 'ungläubisch', rendered 'incredulous' to keep it distinct from 'ungläubig' ('unbelieving').","Triebfedern = 'incentives'; Sittlichkeit = 'morality' (moralisch = 'moral'); Wesen = 'being'; Dasein = 'existence'."]],"dohna-s18-012":["A hypothesis that one holds necessary in a practical aim, e.g. a merchant finds a ground that is sufficient to undertake something. E.g. a general must necessarily judge and resolve something when he stands before the enemy. Thus here the hypothesis is not arbitrary, in order to explain something, but it is necessary in a practical aim. In the former case, however, the aim would be theoretical. All practical aims ( — legislations of reason) are either 1. pragmatic or 2. moral, i.e. either rules of prudence or laws of morality. Thus the aim of that general is pragmatic. Belief, regarded as hypothesis, becomes necessary in a moral aim, can be called pure belief of reason. The concept of duty is entirely distinguished from that of advantage. The posited end of the fulfillment of all duties is promotion of the highest good, insofar as one can attain it here, happiness of human beings: the union of morality, worthiness to be happy, with happiness itself, stands in no human being's power. If we see that our sources of cognition do not suffice to cognize God as a moral being, we can nevertheless have a moral belief thereby. [Practical belief does not extend cognition, it is only the transition from the theoretical to the practical.]",["OCR 'Pf hellten' read as 'Pflichten' (duties); 'erstem' read as 'ersten'.","Glückseligkeit = 'happiness'; Gewalt = 'power' (the same English word as Kraft/Macht in the glossary).","'Der Glaube, als Hypothese angesehen, wird … notwendig, kann … genannt werden' is left with its asyndetic construction; the subject of 'kann' is not repeated.","erklären = 'explain'; Hypothesis kept as 'hypothesis'."]],"dohna-s18-013":["Our author now speaks of the perfection of practical cognition, a chapter that actually does not belong specially in logic.",[]],"dohna-s19-001":["On practical cognition",[]],"dohna-s19-002":["One sees that what he says about it does not quite belong in logic. It would be good if one left away the merely speculative things in religion. A speculative cognition is that which practically has no application at all. Practical propositions are either imperatives — then they are opposed to the theoretical ones — or they are grounds for possible imperatives and then they are opposed to the merely speculative ones. E.g. There is a God, this is no merely speculative, but a practical proposition. For it contains grounds for possible imperatives.",["'wegheße' is doubtful OCR; read as 'wegließe' ('left away').","'Avaspraktisch' is garbled OCR; read as 'was praktisch' ('that which practically').","'entgegengesezt' / 'Imperativen' are as printed; Satz = 'proposition'; Anwendung = 'application'."]],"dohna-s19-003":["[Practical propositions (are those which) contain grounds of cognition for possible imperatives. The proposition: There is another life, is practical, because it contains grounds for possible imperatives. — Theoretical propositions too are called practical, if they contain grounds for this. Speculative propositions are those which contain no grounds for this at all. Imperatives determine to acting.]",["The parentheses around 'are those which' are as in the source.","Erkenntnisgründe = 'grounds of cognition'; bestimmen = 'determine'."]],"dohna-s19-004":["Now we go to a new section:",[]],"dohna-s20-001":["On concepts",[]],"dohna-s20-002":["All cognition is through intuition or concept — representation is an elementary expression, which cannot be resolved further. With representation there can still be combined apperception — the consciousness of the representation.",["Vorstellung and Repräsentation are both rendered 'representation'.","A stray black-square mark after 'verbinden' in the OCR has been omitted as an OCR artifact."]],"dohna-s20-003":["[*ad* p. 14] A representation combined with consciousness is called perception, *perceptio*. This *perceptio* becomes *cognitio*, insofar as the representation is related with consciousness to an object. This representation is now again twofold:",["OCR 'Bewußtsem' read as 'Bewußtsein'.","Wahrnehmung = 'perception'; Gegenstand = 'object'."]],"dohna-s20-004":["1. *intuitus* — this is not yet a cognition,",["OCR 'kern' read as 'kein'."]],"dohna-s20-005":["2. *conceptus*, contains that which is common to several objects, *nota communis*. The concept, considered for itself, is:",["gemein = 'common'; nota = 'mark' in the glossary, but left in Latin here as printed."]],"dohna-s20-006":["a) *conceptus empiricus* — if there is sensation in its representation [for whose existence the preceding of experience is required. The material of all empirical representation is sensation.]",["Material = 'material' (cf. Stoff = 'material', Materie = 'matter'); Empfindung = 'sensation'."]],"dohna-s20-007":["b) *conceptus purus*, this is called *notio*. It can be called a pure concept of the understanding. The concept of the triangle is a *conceptus* |*a*| *priori*.",["The signs | | around 'a' are carried over as printed.","Triangel = 'triangle'; Verstandesbegriff = 'concept of the understanding'."]],"dohna-s20-008":["[Concept is *repraesentatio discursiva*. — The action by which we give to a concept the corresponding intuition is called exhibition, *exhibitio*. Cognition is more than *conceptus*, more than *intuitus*, it is both together. We seek objective reality, which we attain through application to intuition.]",["Darstellung = 'exhibition', following the author's Latin *exhibitio*.","Realität = 'reality'."]],"dohna-s20-009":["*Idea* is called a concept of reason to which no object can be given adequately in intuition.",["Idea is Latin in the source; Vernunftbegriff = 'concept of reason'."]],"dohna-s20-010":["The representation, if it arises from the understanding, is always a concept. But the intuition of red gives as yet no concept of the understanding. — The representation of something as cause can occur only through the understanding, is therefore a *notio*. [A cause one can never experience — for causality is *notio*.] All intuition is only representation of that which is given to us through the senses. Where it takes place, — e.g. with mathematical concepts — there are never *notiones* — pure concepts of the understanding, but certainly *conceptus a priori*. [*Conceptus purus*: 1. *intellectualis*, 2. *rationalis*. *Conceptus purus intellectualis* is called *notio* — e.g. virtue. *Conceptus purus* to which no intuition can be given corresponding, *idea*, e.g. of God. — Of virtue I can give examples. Yet it is no concept of experience. Ontology has to do with sheer notions. — Concepts are, according to their matter, *dati* or *factitii*. The former are called *notiones*, the latter empirical concepts. (*Conceptus arbitrarii*, arbitrary.) Every concept of experience is made. Perception is only subjective — not yet experience. This is cognition with consciousness of the relation to the object.]",["OCR 'kernen' read as 'keinen'; 'Ontologiehat' read as 'Ontologie hat'; 'erstem' as 'ersten'.","'Wo sie stattfindet': 'sie' (feminine) most naturally refers to Anschauung; 'it' is kept.","Materie = 'matter'; Objekt = 'object', as Gegenstand; 'Erfahrungsbegriff' = 'concept of experience'; erfahren = 'experience' (verb).","Tugend = 'virtue'; Ursache = 'cause'; Kausalität = 'causality'."]],"dohna-s20-011":["To sensibility belong two pieces, sense and imagination. There are merely logical concepts — the so-called aesthetic concepts are merely representations without concept. There are pure concepts of the understanding a priori, but also sensibly determined concepts of the understanding [*ad* § 255].",["'a priori' stands between 'Verstandesbegriffe' and 'rein' in the German word order, 'a priori reine Verstandesbegriffe'; it is left in the same position and the attachment remains as open as in the German.","Sinn = 'sense'; Einbildungskraft = 'imagination'; sinnlich = 'sensible', here adverbial 'sensibly'."]],"dohna-s20-012":["Experience is called empirical cognition. In an empirical representation something must be perceived (sensed with consciousness). Thus experience presupposes empirical intuition and concept. Sensation is indispensable for it. Transcendental philosophy, which must precede metaphysics, deals with the origin of the pure concepts of the understanding. Logic does not do that. It occupies itself only with the actual actions. For the use of a concept separation is required, but through that the concept is not yet made. The latter happens 1. through this, that something is considered as a partial representation that can be common to several, e.g. the red color. 2. when I consider the partial representation as *notam*, as ground of cognition of a thing, e.g. cognize by red blood, rose, etc. The 3rd action is abstraction, this partial representation as ground of cognition, insofar as I look away from all remaining partial representations. The concept is thus a partial representation, insofar as I abstract from all the remaining ones in doing so.",["Absonderung = separation (distinct from Abstraktion/abstrahieren, which are rendered abstraction/abstract); absehen = look away from.","Teilvorstellung = partial representation; Erkenntnisgrund = ground of cognition; Erkenntnis = cognition; Anschauung = intuition; Empfindung = sensation; wahrnehmen = perceive; Verstandesbegriffe = concepts of the understanding.","*notam* is Latin (accusative) in the source; the ungrammatical elliptical wording of the third action is kept as it stands."]],"dohna-s20-013":["[Abstracting is in the philosophical sense a negative concept — not attending. There are not abstract concepts. We can abstract only from something, from certain differences, marks of things. The difference between abstract and concrete lies not in the concept, but in the *usu conceptuum*.",["The opening bracket is closed only at the end of the next passage (dohna-s20-014).","Merkmal = mark; Unterschied = difference; attendieren = attend."]],"dohna-s20-014":["A concept can be contained in other concepts (a part of them), but the others can also at the same time be under it, it the ground of them. Both are only the different relations of the same concept, which can quite well be at the same time part and ground of something. Human being is the partial representation of the Negro, of the scholar (for a part of the concept of this, etc., is always that he is a human being), but at the same time this concept is also ground of cognition, under which belong the white, the Negro, the scholar.]",["Neger rendered Negro, the term of the source; Mensch = human being.","The closing bracket matches the opening one in dohna-s20-013.","Grund = ground; Erkenntnisgrund = ground of cognition; Beziehung = relation."]],"dohna-s20-015":["Through abstracting not only is nothing produced, but rather something is left out. One must not say: I abstract a representation, but I abstract from a representation. I can either abstract from the diversity of the things to which this concept is common, or I can attend to it in comparison with others. This is properly the use of the concept, in the first case *in abstracto*, in the second case *in concreto*.",["Verschiedenheit = diversity (Unterschied is rendered difference); Gebrauch = use.","darauf is ambiguous in the German (the diversity, or the concept); the English 'it' keeps this ambiguity.","OCR erstem corrected silently to ersten."]],"dohna-s20-016":["The faculty of the use of concepts *in concreto* is called common understanding, the faculty etc. *in abstracto* speculative understanding. The abstract concept {is in the) concrete contained and <holds> the concrete itself under itself. A higher concept is that which grasps several in itself. *Repraesentatio singularis* — has an *intuitum*, indicates it immediately, but is at bottom no *conceptus*. E.g. Socrates is no *conceptus*.",["The mismatched brackets {...) and the angle brackets <holds> are carried over as printed; the text is evidently corrupt or edited at this point.","Vermögen = faculty; gemeiner Verstand = common understanding; OCR singidaris corrected to singularis.","The Latin words are the source's own technical terms and stay in Latin."]],"dohna-s20-017":["[Hour, Monday]",["Stunde rendered hour; it may also be read as lesson."]],"dohna-s20-018":["As soon as I already make use of |the| words, the representation is a single concept. But if the concept is expressed quite particularly, then one considers it *in abstracto*. Then it is called no longer *conceptus universalis*, but *communis*. A *conceptus communis* can, with regard to the concepts that are contained in (it), be called *superior* [a higher concept], with regard to those in which it is contained, *inferior*. [The *inferior* (lower) always contains more in itself, that | i.e. the *superior* | more under itself, but less in itself. For the *conceptus superior* is precisely thereby, that it contains little in itself, entirely general, e.g. the concept human being under that of animal. Hence this latter a *conceptus superior*, in that it now contains more under itself, it contains nevertheless less in itself. We leave here the property of the understanding (in the human being) aside. The higher the concepts ■ — ■ the simpler. Living corporeal beings — still higher corporeal beings, simple. The highest: being, thing, the simplest. When we look to the content of the concept, we consider it *in concreto*. — If we look to the *sphaera*, we consider it *in abstracto*. — Of the first kind is the cognition of the practitioners — (with mole's eyes — merely on the spot).]",["The signs ■ — ■ are OCR marks of uncertain reading, kept as they stand; the text is possibly garbled there.","einfach and simpel are both rendered simple (the superlative einfachste: simplest).","Wesen = being; Inhalt = content; Art = kind; Praktiker = practitioners.","'it' (es) in 'considers it in abstracto' follows the neuter pronoun of the German."]],"dohna-s20-019":["Every *conceptus communis* is called, with regard to (relation to) the *conceptuum* under (which it) is contained, *species*, with regard to those that are contained under it, *genus*. [The concept of *genus* and *species* is different merely with regard to the relation.] Now I can call the same thing that in the one *respectu* was called *genus*, in the other *species*, e.g. human being in manifold respect *genus*, but in relation to animal nevertheless *species*. Can one find a *genus summum*? The *genus summum* is that which is contained under no other, is no partial concept, i.e. has no part further. If there now is a *genus summum*, an object in general, can one also find a *speciem infimam*? One must assume that there are *conceptus communes* under which one can subsume no other. But it remains nevertheless only arbitrary, and one therefore cannot say that a *species infima* is to be found.",["Relation and Verhältnis are both rendered relation; Rücksicht/Ansehung rendered regard/respect.","Gegenstand überhaupt = object in general; Teilbegriff = partial concept.","OCR genns corrected to genus; the Latin case forms (*respectu*, *speciem infimam*) are kept as in the source."]],"dohna-s20-020":["In the subordination of human concepts we begin from the lowest step, the *Individuo* — this is reckoned to the *conceptui singulari*. From there one goes to the *conceptui superiori* — or *communi*. E.g. oak trees [Hex in Spain, the splendid acorns, which taste like chestnuts] — growths — plants — bodies — substances. Finally one comes *per analysin* to the concept of a thing. The *conceptus superior* is always only a part of the *inferior*, so that the *conceptus summus* the very least in itself — object, since one has not determined at all what — but it contains everything else under itself.",["The clause 'so daß der conceptus summus das Allerwenigste in sich' has no verb in the source; none is supplied.","Hex (the holm oak) is kept as printed; Objekt = object (Gegenstand also = object, not occurring here).","Latin datives (*conceptui singulari*, etc.) are kept as printed."]],"dohna-s20-021":["Our cognition gains uncommonly thereby, if we always go to the *conceptus superior*. The multitude of things that are contained under the concept is called the logical *sphaera* of the concept. The greater this is, the smaller is its content. [*Sphaera* is *latissime patens*, the simpler and more abstract the concept is. One understands by it not the content, but the circle of application, a line that in itself has no breadth, but nevertheless encompasses a large space. A concept that has no *sphaera* at all, e.g. that of the individual Julius Caesar, is = a point.]",["Menge = multitude; Inhalt = content; an sich = in itself; Raum = space.","The reference of 'by it' (darunter) is left as ambiguous as in the German."]],"dohna-s20-022":["Some concepts have one and the same *sphaeram*, like the concept of the necessary and the unchangeable. Such concepts are called *conceptus reciproci*, exchange concepts. *Latior* | *conceptus* |, if | one | understands still more thereunder. If one wanted to set up a consideration and comparison between metal and wood, these are *conceptus heterogenei*, have no *respectum* at all with regard to their quantity, e.g. 100 years — German mile, [because none of these concepts, which are compared with one another, stands under the other. — ] *Conceptus reciproci* must be of the same kind. *Conceptus universalis* one should properly not say. Only the application can be used *in abstracto*, and then it is *quoad usum universalis* or *particularis* etc. A *conceptus* is always *repraesentatio communis*, others there are not in logic.",["Wechselbegriffe rendered 'exchange concepts' (literal); gleichartig = of the same kind (Art = kind).","The fragment 'Latior | conceptus |, wenn | man | noch mehr darunter versteht' is kept with its apparatus signs; its construction is incomplete in the source.","OCR universabs corrected to universalis.","Betrachtung = consideration; Anwendung = application."]],"dohna-s20-023":["There are different kinds of *differentia*. It is namely:",["Art = kind; OCR 'Dif ferentia' corrected to differentia."]],"dohna-s20-024":["1. *generica* — is the diversity of the genera, *generum*, e.g. the physicians call some illnesses — *materias sui generis*.",["Verschiedenheit = diversity; Gattung = genus; Mediziner rendered physicians."]],"dohna-s20-025":["2. *specifica* — is the distinction of the things that belong to a *genus* — in *specie* there must be something that was not contained in the *genus*.",["Unterscheidung = distinction."]],"dohna-s20-026":["3. *numerica* — i.e. the difference of the *conceptuum singularium*, insofar as they are not common to several. In the case of human beings we indicate them through *nomina propria*.",["Unterschied = difference; gemein = common."]],"dohna-s21-001":["On Definitions",["Definition kept as the source's own word (the glossary's Erklärung = explanation; definition is not used here)."]],"dohna-s21-002":["The logical perfection of the definition consists:",["Vollkommenheit = perfection."]],"dohna-s21-003":["1. in distinctness, it is the highest need of the definition.",["Deutlichkeit = distinctness; Bedürfnis = need."]],"dohna-s21-004":["2. in the *completudo* or in distinctness through sufficient marks.",["zureichend = sufficient; Merkmal = mark."]],"dohna-s21-005":["3. *praecisio* — the measuredness, i.e. the separation of everything else that is not required for sufficient distinctness.",["Abgemessenheit rendered literally as measuredness; hinlänglich and zureichend are both rendered sufficient; Absonderung = separation."]],"dohna-s21-006":["[Precision is a need of the 2nd rank for definition. Not more marks than necessary. The definition is the highest logical perfection of the concept.]",["OCR \"der Begriffs\" corrected silently to \"des Begriffs\". Merkmal = mark; Vollkommenheit = perfection; Begriff = concept."]],"dohna-s21-007":["*Conceptus adaequatus in minimis terminis* — *conceptus completus, praecise determinatus*. If the concept has these requirements, then it is a *conceptus rei adaequatus*.",[]],"dohna-s21-008":["We want now to show how definitions are made.",["OCR \"Defmitionen\" corrected to \"Definitionen\"."]],"dohna-s21-009":["All concepts are twofold, namely:",["OCR \"nämbch\" read as \"nämlich\"."]],"dohna-s21-010":["1. *conceptus dati*, and these are either",[]],"dohna-s21-011":["a) *empirici* — given *a posteriori*,",[]],"dohna-s21-012":["b) *rationati* — given *a priori*. — [E.g. concept of cause, of magnitude, substance, effect, time and space. We cannot observe space It is not an empirical representation. For this rests at all times on perception.]",["\"rationati\" kept as printed; the reading is not certain (possibly \"rationales\"). No full stop after \"observe space\" in the source.","Vorstellung = representation; Wahrnehmung = perception; Größe = magnitude; Wirkung = effect."]],"dohna-s21-013":["2. *conceptus facticii* ( — made — invented — ) — they can be given.",["OCR \"facticn\" read as \"facticii\". \"gedichtete\" rendered \"invented\". The clause \"sie können gegeben werden\" is kept as it stands."]],"dohna-s21-014":["a) *ex datis a posteriori*,",[]],"dohna-s21-015":["b) *ex datis a priori*.",[]],"dohna-s21-016":["All definitions of given concepts, if they are given a priori, are always analytic, all definitions of made concepts without distinction synthetic.",["The adverbial \"a priori\" stands next to \"gegeben\" and is kept in the same position."]],"dohna-s21-017":["[Synthetic definition is that which arises from many concepts taken together. I make for myself the concept of *quadratum* (*est rectangulum quadrilaterum aequilaterum*) through the definition. This goes therefore before the concept. So it is always in synthesis.",["OCR \"Avelche\" read as \"welche\". The opening square bracket has no closing one in this passage; kept as it stands."]],"dohna-s21-018":["Analysis. I seek out in the concept that I already have the marks. This requires: 1. (Exposition of the concept) (e.g. body. Through the exposition we obtain the elementary propositions for the definition. Here too the 3 *requisita*. Body. Extended, composite, divisible lies already therein.)",["\"Hegt\" read as \"liegt\". \"zusammengesetztes\" (neuter singular) is kept as odd as in the source, hence \"composite\". \"Exposition\" is left as in the German (cf. Erörterung = exposition).","The parenthesis opened after \"1.\" is closed only after \"Exposition des Begriffs\"; the nested parentheses are kept as they stand."]],"dohna-s21-019":["Mathematical definitions can never be false, because mathematics makes its concepts itself. — Definitions of concepts of experience are synthetic. They can properly never be defined, only described.]",["OCR \"sjmthetisch\" read as \"synthetisch\". The closing bracket has no opening one in this passage."]],"dohna-s21-020":["An empirical concept too can be given analytically. In that case not merely the concept is given, but also the object. If one wanted, e.g., to define water, one would define (only) one's concept of it, not the object itself. It goes likewise with all other concepts of experience. One can well define them themselves, but not the object, because we cannot find all the marks. With all concepts of experience the definition of the concept in and for itself is entirely dispensable. — (It is indeed entirely subjective.) The *conceptus dati* can usefully be defined only insofar as they are given a priori. — My definition will in regard to the object never become *complet* and *eo ipso* never a definition.",["\"Sie\" in \"Sie ist ja ganz subjektiv\" is kept as \"It\" (feminine, with Definition as the likely antecedent, but not stated).","\"complet\" left as in the German."]],"dohna-s21-021":["So far we have dealt with the given concepts and now come to the made ones. All definitions are here synthetic, for there always lie data at the ground, which are put together. All mathematical definitions are synthetic. All approximations to concepts of experience, observations, are synthetic. But the synthesis is not arbitrary, the concept therefore not *arbitrarie facticius*. The mathematician can never err, only fail in precision. E.g. Wolff in the definition of the circle, which one can put more briefly than he did. On that account he has not erred, but only failed in precision.",["OCR \"hegen\" read as \"liegen\". \"zum Grunde liegen\" rendered \"lie at the ground\" (Grund = ground).","Beobachtungen (observations) stands in apposition to Annäherungen, as in the German."]],"dohna-s21-022":["I [hour]",["\"Stunde\" in square brackets in the source (lecture-hour marker); rendered \"hour\"."]],"dohna-s21-023":["Definition is the declaration of the representations that I make for myself of the object. To the made concepts belong also those which arise from perceptions. If a definition of these is possible, it cannot be other than synthetic. But we can define concepts of experience only nominally [describe. The description is distinguished from the definition by this, that it does not have the requirements of *completudo* and precision.]",["Declaration = Declaration (Erklärung = declaration among its senses). The square bracket opens mid-sentence and is kept."]],"dohna-s21-024":["We go now to the concepts given a priori. All definitions of given concepts a priori are defined *per analysin*. [The beginning here is always the exposition — (not the definition).] E.g. the concept of right. It is to be sure not innate in us, (but) in order to bring it forth we had to reflect on it — in the concept of virtue is contained first — lawfulness to actions, then lawfulness to right actions, then also freedom thereto. [What stands in conflict with the inclinations to the contrary, — in this it is distinguished from holiness.]",["The OCR reads \"{sondern)\" with mismatched brace; restored as \"(sondern)\" and rendered \"(but)\". \"Recht\" (right) and \"rechten Handlungen\" (right actions) both rendered \"right\".","\"sie\" in the last bracket has no clear antecedent in the passage; kept as \"it\"."]],"dohna-s21-025":["In the analysis of a concept the 1st thing — the exposition — is to become aware little by little of the manifold that is contained in my consciousness, 2ndly — definition, if the exposition is *complet*. But how can we now become certain whether we have completely exposed a concept? One must be very cautious in such cases, e.g. in those of substance and cause — concepts that are purely a priori. [One can never be fully conscious of the *completudo* in philosophical definitions — it is very different: not to be conscious that something is lacking, and to be conscious that nothing is lacking.]",["OCR \"philosopisehen\" read as \"philosophischen\". \"Mannigfaltige\" = manifold; Bewußtsein = consciousness; \"bewußt\" rendered \"aware\"/\"conscious\".","\"rein a priori\": \"purely a priori\"; the adverb could go with \"rein\" or the phrase, left as in the German."]],"dohna-s21-026":["Remark. With given concepts one must never begin with the definition, but at all times with the exposition. But with made concepts — *conc. factitiis*, e.g. mathematical ones, the definition must always go before. The exposition starts from elementary propositions. In regard to the subsidiary concepts, if one takes the word strictly, no definition and demonstration at all can take place. These words belong properly only for mathematics. Nevertheless we call it in philosophy, insofar as we presume *completudo* — definition. It is not always necessary to define. —",["OCR \"factieiis\" read as \"factitiis\" (as printed, ablative form kept as the source has it); \"für f die\" read as \"für die\". \"Nebenbegriffe\" rendered \"subsidiary concepts\" (literally secondary/side concepts).","\"strict\" is Latinate in the German; \"strictly\" in English."]],"dohna-s21-027":["We go now further and want to deal",["Breaks off; continues with the heading in the next passage."]],"dohna-s21-028":["With the rules of definitions",["Heading; \"Von den Regeln der Definitionen\" rendered \"Of the rules of definitions\" would also be possible; the construction follows the preceding \"handeln\"."]],"dohna-s21-029":["To the judgment of definitions belong the following rules:",["Beurteilung = judgment (estimation) per the glossary."]],"dohna-s21-030":["1. whether the alleged definition be true as a proposition,",["Satz = proposition."]],"dohna-s21-031":["2. whether the definition as a concept be distinct. —",["deutlich = distinct."]],"dohna-s21-032":["Two pieces hinder the distinctness of a concept, namely",["\"Stücke\" rendered \"pieces\" literally."]],"dohna-s21-033":["a) when the marks are tautological, I explain *idem per idem*.",[]],"dohna-s21-034":["b) when I explain *obscurum per aeque obscurum*.",[]],"dohna-s21-035":["The 3rd criterion is whether the concept as a distinct concept be detailed [*Completudo*],",["\"ausführlich\" rendered \"detailed\" (the bracketed *Completudo* is the transcript's gloss)."]],"dohna-s21-036":["the 4th, whether the concept that I regard as detailed be precise. [Precision.] How are definitions to be made?",["\"präcis\" = precise; \"Präcision\" = precision."]],"dohna-s21-037":["1. Seek several elementary propositions (here it is a matter of analytic ones).",[]],"dohna-s21-038":["2. See to it that the predicate not be just as obscure as the concept that you want to explain. —",["\"dunkel\" = obscure."]],"dohna-s21-039":["3. Gather several such predicates, until you are certain that together they exhaust the whole concept.",["\"exhaurieren\" = exhaust."]],"dohna-s21-040":["4. See to it that one mark not already contain the other and thus can be dispensed with. (Through this one does not yet learn to make definitions.)",["\"kann\" has \"ein Merkmal\" or the other as its subject; the German is ambiguous and is left so."]],"dohna-s21-041":["The most useful of all rules is this, that one must begin with the exposition, whereby one obtains elementary propositions for a definition. With an analytic definition we always reserve to ourselves the extension of our cognition and perhaps also in future to think the opposite. If no concept lies at the ground, I cannot speak of the thing at all. But this always works then, when we have made the same clear. One must guard especially against this, that one not reciprocate, invert, the definition. All definitions in philosophy must be regarded as *tentamina* for philosophizing.",["\"denselben\" (\"the same\") has no clear antecedent; kept. \"meinen\" rendered \"to think\"; \"reciprociert, umkehrt\" rendered \"reciprocate, invert\".","Erkenntnis = cognition; Weltweisheit not in the passage (\"Philosophieren\" = philosophizing)."]],"dohna-s21-042":["The perfection of a definition, as far as the | quantity is concerned, rests on this, that the *definitio* reciprocates with the *definitum*, that one can put one for the other. [When the *sphaera* of the definitions is equal to the *sphaera* of the *definiti*.] *Definitio nec sit latior nec angustior definito.* A *conceptus* is *latior* than another if it contains less, *angustior* if it has more determinations. This is namely the sequel of the narrowing of the concept.",["OCR \"I die |\": the stray \"I\" is dropped, the sign | kept as apparatus. \"nee\" read as \"nec\"; \"coneeptus\" as \"conceptus\". \"mehrere Bestimmungen\" rendered \"more determinations\".","Folge = sequel (here \"die Folge von\")."]],"dohna-s21-043":["Quality in logic — distinctness. The logical requisite:",[]],"dohna-s21-044":["*Ne definiatur obscurum per aeque obscurum.* The",["Breaks off at \"Die\"; continues in the next passage."]],"dohna-s21-045":["2. logical relation is the relation of the marks in the definition to the concept of a *definiti*. Every ground *generaliter* must be something other than the sequel, without this it is not ground. The third rule is",["\"eines definiti\" kept in the Latin genitive as in the German. Breaks off at \"Die dritte Regel ist\".","Grund = ground; Folge = sequel."]],"dohna-s21-046":["3. *Non definiendum in circulo, circulo*, i.e. to explain *idem per idem*. If the identity is manifest, it is called tautological. [As in: *Ratio est id, per quod cognoscimus, cur aliquid sit.* But *cur* means *quam ob rationem*. The explanation is thus wholly tautological, says nothing new.] But if it is concealed, we call this method: to explain in a circle. The fourth rule:",["OCR garbled after the first 'circulo' (a stray 'f' and comma); I read the Latin as repeated 'circulo' and dropped the stray sign.","Erklärung is rendered 'explanation' here, per the glossary; erklären is 'to explain'."]],"dohna-s21-047":["4. Modality — Necessity is here the logical perfection. An empirical proposition does not contain it. — Empirical concepts cannot be defined at all.",["Satz = 'proposition'; Vollkommenheit = 'perfection'; definieren = 'define'."]],"dohna-s21-048":["Definitions can be divided into",["Einteilen = 'divide'; the sentence breaks off and continues in the next passage."]],"dohna-s21-049":["Nominal and — in distinction from others, real explanations, which are sufficient for the derivation of all predicates and concepts. Through explanations of the thing we see into the possibility of things. Nominal explanations serve merely | to | distinguish a thing. A nominal definition that would be sufficient to distinguish the thing from all others would be fully as good as a real definition. The mathematical definitions are all real. Description is distinguished from definition in that it contains only what is historical. The exposition of a concept, in which it remains undetermined whether it is complete or precise, is a description.",["'Nominal- und —' is broken off in the German; kept as it stands.","Sacherklärungen ('explanations of the thing') and Realerklärungen / Realdefinition are both rendered with 'real'/'of the thing' as the German varies; hinreichend = 'sufficient'.","'Description', 'complet', 'präcis' are Latinate in the German; Exposition = 'exposition' (cf. Erörterung, *expositio*)."]],"dohna-s22-001":["[Hour, Monday] On the Division of Concepts",["Einteilung = 'division' (as is Teilung in the next passage; both are 'division')."]],"dohna-s22-002":["[It consists in the distinct representation of all lower concepts, insofar as they are contained under a higher one and are opposed to one another.] Logical division is called Einteilung and is distinguished from the division of the concept, or analysis. By the *divisio logica* one understands not the division of the concept itself (*divisio realis*), but — of its *sphaera*. E.g. the concept of the human being has a *sphaeram* — no manifold in itself, but it takes many others under itself. [A division of trees, e.g., into crown, trunk, root would be *partitio*, not *divisio*.",["The opening bracket '[' is left unclosed, as in the source passage.","'Division' renders both Division (in 'logische Division') and Einteilung/Teilung; the German terms are given in the text where they are named.","Mannigfaltigkeit rendered 'manifold'; 'sphaeram' is the accusative as printed."]],"dohna-s22-003":["*Partitio* is different from *divisio* — the latter divides merely the concept. Logical division is always dichotomy, a or non a. All living beings mortal or non-mortal. This is the analytic division according to the principle of contradiction. The principle of all logical divisions is the *principium exclusi medii inter duo contradictoria*. Synthetic division into a and b. Polytomy presupposes real, not merely formal knowledge. — One can never become certain of the completeness of one's division. Properly one would have to be able to survey how much is possible. But they are concrete concepts, objects of experience. Here only *partitio* is possible.]",["OCR 'nona' corrected to 'non a'.","Satz des Widerspruchs = 'principle of contradiction'; Prinzip and Satz are both 'principle' here; Kenntnis = 'knowledge' (not Wissen).","'Billig' rendered 'Properly'; 'es sind' rendered 'they are', literally 'it is/there are'; the closing ']' has no opening bracket in this passage."]],"dohna-s22-004":["All division can occur in *opposita* or in *disparata*. The first can also be made a priori, but the empirical (experience-> division is only in *disparata*, e.g. the different human races, red-brown, yellow, etc. | one | in *opposita* would be if I said: all human beings are either white or non-white.",["'(Erfahrungs->' reproduced as in the OCR; the bracket is evidently garbled apparatus.","Menschenrassen rendered literally 'human races'; '| eine |' carried over as '| one |'."]],"dohna-s22-005":["Now we want to go through the pieces that belong to the division of the concept.",[]],"dohna-s22-006":["1. The divided concept, *conceptus divisus*, whose sphere can be divided.",[]],"dohna-s22-007":["2. The *membra dividentia*, the lower ones, which are contained under the higher ones. They are members of the division, which composed form a complete whole. One cognizes this from the fact, when they are *aequat* to the sphere. A division thus expresses this, that lower concepts subordinated to higher ones together are equal to the whole sphere of the concept.",["'aequat' kept as printed (Latin verb form); erkennen = 'cognize'.","The 'Dies ... daraus, wenn' construction is kept as awkward as in the German."]],"dohna-s22-008":["A given division can be considered in relation to its subdivisions, *Subdivisionen* (division of a member of the division), which go very far down to the individual, and to its side-divisions (*Codivisionen*), e.g. human beings, respectively to <1.> learnedness, <2.> to sex, <3., 4.> etc. The members of the side-division are called *membra codividentia*.",["OCR 'Subdi Visionen ^Einteilung' read as 'Subdivisionen (Einteilung'; the angle-bracket numerals were garbled ('<2.)', '<3., 4.)') and are normalized to '<2.>', '<3., 4.>'.","Untereinteilung and Subdivision are both 'subdivision'; Nebeneinteilung rendered 'side-division', Codivision kept in German; Geschlecht rendered 'sex'."]],"dohna-s22-009":["We come now to the proper rules. The first is:",[]],"dohna-s22-010":["1. The *conceptus* must not be *latior* than all *membra dividentia* taken together, the less in itself, the more under itself. [Then it contains too much.]",[]],"dohna-s22-011":["2. The *conceptus* must also not be *angustior* than the *membra dividentia*.",[]],"dohna-s22-012":["3. Between the members of the division there must be oppositions and these one takes together *disjunctive*, i.e. through either or. [The *membra dividentia* must be opposed to one another, otherwise it is not logical division.]",["OCR 'Opjwsitionen' corrected to 'Oppositionen'; entgegengesetzt = 'opposed'."]],"dohna-s22-013":["4. The divided concept must not contradict the concept of the division.",[]],"dohna-s22-014":["5. The members of the subdivision must not come under the members of the upper division, that would be contradictory. To this one must turn special attention. E.g. to the ignorant one must oppose not the learned, but the one who possesses science.",["Obereinteilung rendered 'upper division'; Wissenschaft = 'science'."]],"dohna-s22-015":["6. The number of the members of the division I must derive from the nature of the divided concept. All empirical division one can sooner call partition than *divisio logica*. [With this one must know that there are neither more nor fewer members of the division, with the empirical this need not be. — ]",["'darf dies nicht sein' is ambiguous ('need not be' / 'must not be'); rendered 'need not be'.","'bei dieser' refers either to *divisio logica* (nearest) or to division generally; rendered 'this'."]],"dohna-s22-016":["All logical division (which abstracts from all content) can be nothing other than dichotomy <2 members> — a — non a — according to the principle of contradiction. [It is the principle of all analytic judgments.] Polytomy, trichotomy etc. — divisions that are not made according to this principle are always synthetic. [Synthetic divisions — trichotomy, polytomy generally. — It is striking that trichotomy occurs so often, Subst. God: (holiness, goodness, justice). Here there is everywhere, as it were, a supreme principle or a condition <a conditioned> a sequel, unity the condition, multiplicity the conditioned, totality the sequel, the combination (totality — the multiplicity as unity) of both together.]",["Stray OCR signs 'f' dropped (before 'Gott' and before 'Einheit'); 'Subst' is garbled/abbreviated and left as it stands; '<2 Glieder)' and '<ein Bedingtes)' normalized to angle brackets.","Satz des Widerspruchs = 'principle of contradiction'; 'oberster Satz' = 'supreme principle' (Satz also 'proposition'); Vielheit = 'multiplicity'; Folge = 'sequel'; Verbindung = 'combination'.","The syntax of 'ein oberster Satz oder eine Bedingung <ein Bedingtes> eine Folge' is kept as unarticulated as in the German."]],"dohna-s22-017":["On Judgments",["Urteil = 'judgment'."]],"dohna-s22-018":["The representation that is universal through its consciousness as of a mark is a clear concept. The consciousness of a universal representation (*repraesentatio communis*) is thus called not merely concept but clear concept. The concept that becomes clear through a judgment is called a distinct concept, and a concept that becomes distinct through a syllogism is called a concept of reason (according to Wolff a complete one).",["'als eines Merkmals' is ambiguous (the consciousness 'as of a mark', or 'as a mark'); rendered 'as of a mark'.","Vernunftschluß = 'syllogism'; allgemein = 'universal'; klar = 'clear', deutlich = 'distinct'."]],"dohna-s22-019":["[Judgment is the representation of the unity of given concepts, insofar as one is subordinated to the other or excluded from the same. —",["The bracket is left unclosed, as in the source passage."]],"dohna-s22-020":["1. Clear concept, through consciousness of universality.",[]],"dohna-s22-021":["2. distinct concept, not merely through *apprehensio*, but — *Judicium*.",["The German breaks off after 'sondern —'; kept as it stands."]],"dohna-s22-022":["3. Concept of reason.",["OCR 'Vernunft begriff' read as Vernunftbegriff."]],"dohna-s23-001":["3 judgments:",[]],"dohna-s23-002":["1. where one is under the other like subject under predicate: categorical.",["A stray OCR 'f' after 'wie' was dropped."]],"dohna-s23-003":["2. The judgment where one concept is under the other as the conditioned under the condition, as sequel under the ground: hypothetical.",["Stray OCR 'f' after 'Bedingung' dropped; Folge = 'sequel', Grund = 'ground'."]],"dohna-s23-004":["3. The judgment that contains a sequel that can be divided, disjunctive: QPJ.]",["'QPJ' is a garbled OCR reading and is left as printed; a stray 'f' before 'disjunktiv' was dropped; the closing ']' has no opening bracket here."]],"dohna-s23-005":["To act means to separate the cause from the effect. If a concept is to become distinct, one must always pass a *judicium*. The mark becomes at once the predicate of the judgment, e.g. the human being is an animal. This distinct concept becomes only through a judgment. From this one sees that before one goes to the distinct concepts, one must treat of judgments.",["'Handeln' in the first sentence (also 'act' / 'treat', as in the last sentence 'handeln von') may be corrupt or elliptical; rendered 'To act'.","Merkmal = 'mark'; Urteil = 'judgment'; 'wird nur durch' rendered literally 'becomes only through'."]],"dohna-s23-006":["The concept of reason arises from a syllogism. E.g. All human beings are living beings, all living beings are animals, ergo all human beings are animals. Thus one can infer to a *notam remotam* only through a *notam mediam*, through a syllogism. Hence one should rather treat of inferences, should undertake the three operations of thinking right at first. This was Aristotle's manner — very stringent. — It was Wolff who did away with it.",["OCR 'Vernunft begriff' and 'smd' corrected; stray 'f' dropped before 'alle Menschen' and before 'eine notam'.","*nota* = 'mark'; Vernunftschluß = 'syllogism', Schluß = 'inference'; 'Daher man eher ... sollte, ... sollte' kept as in the German; 'abbrachte' rendered 'did away with'."]],"dohna-s23-007":["Judgment is the representation of the relation of concepts among one another, whereby a cognition becomes distinct. A great fault in this logic, that the author speaks right away of subject and predicate [namely before the more needed prior knowledge has been sent ahead, it is almost so in every logic.] The definition of our author of judgment fits only for the categorical ones. [We do not have merely one kind of judgments — ] But there are also hypothetical, problematic ones. [3 relations: 1. of inherence, 2. of dependence, 3. of the parts, among one another, which together make a whole.]",["Verhältnis = relation (Relation, in later passages, is also 'relation'); Vorkenntnisse = prior knowledge; nötigern = 'more needed' (nötig, not notwendig).","OCR 'unter -einander' read as 'untereinander'; stray 'f' marks (apparently the editors' daggers) omitted.","Art = kind."]],"dohna-s23-008":["All judgments let themselves be brought under 3 kinds: 1. categorical ones, which contain the relation of the predicate to the subject.",["Stray 'f' after 'kategorische' omitted as apparatus."]],"dohna-s23-009":["2. hypothetical ones, they contain the relation of the ground to the sequel. <(If the soul is not composed, then etc.)",["Folge = sequel (glossary); the angle bracket and parenthesis are left as the OCR gives them, unbalanced."]],"dohna-s23-010":["[What is not composed is not perishable. The soul is not composed, the soul is not perishable.]",["vergänglich = perishable; zusammengesetzt = composed."]],"dohna-s23-011":["3. disjunctive ones, they contain the relation of a divided concept to the whole sphere of the division <e.g. all human beings are learned or unlearned). These judgments are of a quite different kind, but have this in common, that <they) can always also be expressed categorically (yet it might not always work).",["Brackets kept as printed, including the mismatched '<' and ')'.","Sphäre = sphere; Einteilung = division."]],"dohna-s23-012":["[There are kinds of difference, numerically — specifically — different *toto genere*. — Thus we went from below upward. The learned are specifically one and the same and generically too and yet numerically different, as C. and J.]",["Gattung would be 'genus'; here 'generisch' = 'generically'. 'C.' and 'J.' are left as they stand."]],"dohna-s23-013":["We now go to the",["Breaks off; the heading follows in the next passage."]],"dohna-s23-014":["Categorical Judgments",[]],"dohna-s23-015":["Matter in logic is what is given. [Form in logic is the way and manner of the putting together and connection of given concepts.] In categorical judgments the subject and the predicate must be given. In all categorical judgments the negation <the sign of denial) must affect the *Copula* (not the predicate), otherwise they are not negative judgments. [*Anima non est* <(*Copula*) *mortalis*, would be a negative judgment. *A. est immortalis* <*non mortalis*) affirming.] Judgments whose predicates are merely negative [e.g. not m. — that which | outside | the sphere |of m. is|], one calls infinite judgments <*judicia infinita*) [because of such things as are *non a* there is no end, but a can be determined].",["Verknüpfung = connection; Verneinung = denial; bejahend = affirming; Art und Weise = 'way and manner'.","The fragment 'nicht m. — das was | außerhalb | der Sphäre |m. ist|' is rendered with its bars as printed; the position of the bars is uncertain in the OCR.","OCR '(Copula)' etc. keep the printed brackets."]],"dohna-s23-016":["On the Condition of Unconditioned Judgments",["'unbedingter' read as printed (sic)."]],"dohna-s23-017":["[Every judgment has the condition of truth — the ground why one ascribes a predicate to it.] In every judgment there must yet be something in the subject which makes it that the predicate is ascribed to it, and this one calls the condition. E.g. every body as an extended being is divisible. Or yet another example: all human beings as finite beings are fallible (*faillible*). This now again is called no conditioned but a restricted judgment. If this latter drops out, it becomes conditioned. The restriction one calls the determination of the judgment (*determinatio judicii*). — Sometimes these determinations lie in the concept, sometimes they are added.",["'im Subjektes' is as printed (grammatically irregular); 'hegen' read as 'liegen' (OCR).","Einschränkung = restriction (distinct from Schranke = limitation).","'faillible' is French in the source."]],"dohna-s23-018":["We come now to the division of judgments",["Breaks off; continues in the next passage."]],"dohna-s23-019":["1. according to quality, there they are either affirming or denying {and infinite), e.g. some human beings are not learned — a denying judgment,",["verneinend = denying (not 'negative', which renders negativ)."]],"dohna-s23-020":["2. according to quantity all judgments are either",[]],"dohna-s23-021":["a) *universalia*, are expressed through all,",["OCR 'universaha' read as 'universalia'."]],"dohna-s23-022":["b) *particularia*, instead of this expression rather pluralistic judgments.",[]],"dohna-s23-023":["We can consider every predicate in itself as *terminus major*. There are general judgments that one expresses not universally, only pluralistically, e.g. All rational beings are thinking human beings, some human beings are rational beings, some human beings are thinking etc. There are 3 sorts of judgments according to quantity, namely general, particular and singular. Every *judicium singulare* suffers no exception, because it has no *sphaeram*.",["allgemein = general; universal = universal (kept distinct); besondre = particular (the Latin is particularia).","OCR 'näinlich' read as 'nämlich'."]],"dohna-s23-024":["| [*Judicium generale*, in general, is different from the general {universal) judgment. It takes place where there are only few exceptions, whereby one comes least into the danger of erring with this judgment, one can therefore say it *generaliter*. Such judgments can take place only in the empirical.] |",["The OCR's closing ']!' is read as ']|'; the bars are kept.","'allgemeinen {universellen)': general (universal)."]],"dohna-s23-025":["A categorical judgment has either 2 subjects and one predicate, or 2 predicates and one subject or only one subject and only one predicate.",[]],"dohna-s23-026":["We come",["Breaks off; continues in the next passage."]],"dohna-s23-027":["3. to relation, according to it we divide the judgments into:",["Relation (here) = relation, as Verhältnis above; both are rendered 'relation'."]],"dohna-s23-028":["a) categorical. The relation of the subject to the predicate.",[]],"dohna-s23-029":["b) hypothetical. The relation of the ground to the sequel. [ — 2 judgments are in the relation that the one is ground of the other. If therefore the one is true, so is the other too. With the hypothetical judgment there can be only 2 propositions, — with the disjunctive there can be several.]",["Satz = proposition."]],"dohna-s23-030":["c) disjunctive. The relation of the members of the divided concept, of the parts of the sphere to the whole sphere.",["'Sphära' (as printed) = sphere."]],"dohna-s23-031":["The categorical {absolute) judgment contains only the relation of 2 concepts, the | hypothetical and | disjunctive however of two or several judgments. [*Propositio* or proposition is called an assertoric judgment.]",["'Propositio oder Satz': Latin and German given as 'Propositio' and 'proposition'."]],"dohna-s23-032":["What the ground is in a *judicio hypothetico* is called *antecedens*, the connection with the latter is called the *Copula*, the thereafter following {inferential sequel from) both judgments is called the consequence. The matter of the hypothetical judgment consists not of 2 concepts, but of two judgments, whose relation is the consequence. [A hypothetical judgment consists of two problematic ones — this is its matter. The sequel of the *consequens* from the *antecedens* is called *consequentia*. The hypothetical judgment does nothing except that it draws the consequence from the two problematic propositions.]",["Konsequenz = consequence; Folge = sequel; Schlußfolge = 'inferential sequel' (literal compound).","'die hernach folgende' kept literal."]],"dohna-s23-033":["Now an example of a *judicio disjunctivo*: All bodies are either fluid or solid. <If this world is not the best, then | God | has not known the better or | not | wanted to create or | not create | been able to etc.) [This together makes up the whole *sphaeram*.]",["The bars within the parenthesis are the editors' marks, kept where they stand; the clause is doubtful in the OCR."]],"dohna-s23-034":["4. Modality (determination of the concept of combination). The 3 determinations of the *Copula* [or of the concept of combination] (especially for categorical judgments) are:",["Verbindungsbegriff = concept of combination (Verbindung = combination)."]],"dohna-s23-035":["a) problematic judgments, contain logical possibility,",[]],"dohna-s23-036":["b) assertoric judgments, contain logical actuality [truth],",["Wirklichkeit = actuality."]],"dohna-s23-037":["c) apodictic judgments, contain logical necessity.",[]],"dohna-s23-038":["[Hour, Friday]",[]],"dohna-s23-039":["The author says: a *Judicium* without modality is *judicium purum*. A judgment is called *exponibile*, because a thought or concept is expressed as simple, although it is in fact composed, and indeed of an affirming and denying one, e.g. few human beings are virtuous. [This signifies: some human beings are virtuous, many human beings are not virtuous, thus an affirming and a denying judgment.] [A composed judgment, where 2 judgments have one predicate.] Not <pure> logical judgments are those that [are aesthetic] merely represent the subjective in reference to a faculty of cognition. E.g. if one makes a description of mountains and thereby recounts not generally valid concepts of their magnitude and situation, but merely the sensations produced thereby in us.",["Beziehung = reference (to keep apart from Verhältnis = relation); Erkenntnisvermögen = faculty of cognition.","OCR 'Empfindüngen' read as 'Empfindungen'; 'bedeutet' = signifies."]],"dohna-s23-040":["[Theoretical judgments are those that determine an object as possible. Practical ones designate the actions to make it actual.]",["wirklich = actual."]],"dohna-s23-041":["Practical propositions refer to the actions through which the proposition becomes possible.",[]],"dohna-s23-042":["[Unprovable judgment can yet be true. — All immediately certain propositions are unprovable.] To demonstrate can have twofold meaning. —",["OCR 'ünerweisliches' read as 'unerweisliches'; unerweislich = unprovable (demonstrieren = demonstrate)."]],"dohna-s23-043":["An empty judgment [which contains no ground of any cognition, such as tautological judgments] is a judgment that contains no concept, which is wholly indistinct, if I explain *obscurum per aeque obscurum*. An empty judgment can be manifest and concealed. [Identical judgments are therefore not yet empty. — All definitions are identical with the defined concept. The identity can also be cognized through dissection.]",["erklären = explain (as 'Erklärung' may be 'definition'); Definition = definition.","Zergliederung = dissection (literal)."]],"dohna-s23-044":["If the *identitas explicita*, then the judgment is tautological — if a mark is named twice. Not everything unprovable is an identical proposition. Synthetic propositions also belong there. If I have transformed *identitatem implicitam* into *explicitam*, then I have performed the analysis.",["OCR: \"löAnalysis\" read as \"die Analysis\"; \"explieitam\" read as \"explicitam\".","\"Wenn die Identitas explicita, so ist\" has no verb in the German; none is supplied in the English.","Merkmal = mark; Satz = proposition; Urteil = judgment; unerweislich = unprovable (cf. Beweis = proof)."]],"dohna-s23-045":["The author now speaks of unprovable judgments. Axioms are unprovable (immediately certain) propositions. [They are possible only in mathematics.] Even if much could be discovered *per analysin*, the mathematician does not want this. For it is not his business, since he brings everything forth through construction of concepts in intuition. [Axioms are always synthetic propositions, namely those that one can demonstrate in an intuition (through) *intuitum purum*. The analytic propositions are [ thereby | not identical.]",["OCR: \"smd\" read as \"sind\"; \"anatytischen\" as \"analytischen\"; \"Ließ\" read as \"Ließe\".","The brackets are carried over exactly as printed; the second \"[\" before \"damit\" is not closed before the final \"]\", and the first bracket is left unclosed in the OCR.","\"damit\" is ambiguous (\"thereby\" / \"with it\" / \"so that\"); \"thereby\" is chosen, and the clause is evidently incomplete or corrupt.","Anschauung = intuition (Latin intuitus, here *intuitum purum*); Begriff = concept."]],"dohna-s23-046":["A postulate is a practical proposition about what ought to be done, without showing the way and manner, because it assumes that everyone can do what he recommends as imperatives. [For in logic what matters is not the matter but always the form.]",["\"Art und Weise\" = way and manner.","\"als Imperativen\" is rendered as \"as imperatives\", keeping the German plural form without a determiner.","Materie = matter; Form = form (the German \"Materie\" and \"Form\" are used in the same sense in later passages)."]],"dohna-s23-047":["Judgments are either intuitive (insofar as it goes to an object that can be presented in intuition) or discursive (whose object is presented through concept).",["\"Objekt\" and \"Gegenstand\" are both rendered \"object\"; the first occurrence is Objekt, the second Gegenstand.","The singular \"es\" and \"dessen\" do not agree with the plural \"Urteile\"; this is preserved.","\"durch Begriff\" has no article in the German; kept."]],"dohna-s23-048":["A judgment of perception is intuitive (not discursive). An empirical judgment is intuitive, experience however already discursive. Judgments of experience are at all times discursive, because namely we always connect perceptions with them. A *vitium subreptionis*, if one takes for a judgment of experience what is only a judgment of perception.",["Wahrnehmung = perception; Erfahrung = experience; verknüpfen = connect.","The sentence \"Ein empirisches Urteil ist intuitiv, Erfahrung doch schon diskursiv\" is kept as is, with no verb in the second clause."]],"dohna-s23-049":["A *theorema* is a theoretical proposition, insofar as it is capable of a proof. [A consequence that one draws through an inference: *corollarium consectarium*.] A problem is a practical proposition, since one must assume what is commanded to be done.",["OCR: \"Mas\" read as \"was\".","Folgerung = consequence; Schluß = inference; Aufgabe = problem.","\"Theorema\" is capitalized and in roman in the source; it is italicized here as a Latin/Greek technical term."]],"dohna-s24-001":["[Hour, Monday]",["\"Stunde\" = hour (the lecture hour); no further specification in the source."]],"dohna-s24-002":["Axiom a proposition that comes immediately from the definition. It properly presupposes intuition, or at least immediate certainty *a priori*. The practical proposition that is in need of and capable of a solution is called *quaestio problematis* [the proposition that determines the action that is to happen]. The representation of the way the object is brought about is called *resolutio*. The proof that — if one has proceeded thus — that follows: *demonstratio*. A postulate is capable neither of a resolution nor of a demonstration. The solution of the problem is complete if everything is contained therein, if the relation is completely solved. This does not always work in mathematics, but one can approach it as much as one wants, e.g. the measurement of the diameter of the earth. — Exactness — precision presupposes that it contains completely ■ — not too little, nor too much.",["\"Axiom a proposition\" has no verb in the German; none supplied.","\"Auflösung\" = solution, \"aufgelöset\" = solved (the OCR \"auf gelöset\" is joined); \"Resolution\"/*resolutio* kept distinct as a Latin/Latinate word.","\"■\" is an OCR mark in the source after \"vollständig\"; the reading there is uncertain or something is missing.","Genauigkeit = exactness; Präzision = precision; Verhältnis = relation; Beweis = proof; Vorstellung = representation; Gegenstand = object."]],"dohna-s24-003":["In solutions in the practical one does not take it so very exactly and is content if one has the means, whose force one prefers to assume too strong than too weak. [E.g. lever, wheels, rather too much than too little force. — Test — that which one has analyzed — bring back again to the given. — ]",["OCR: \"sein*\" read as \"sehr\" (doubtful).","\"im Praktischen\" = \"in the practical\" (substantive).","Mittel = means; Kraft = force; Probe = test."]],"dohna-s24-004":["*Qualitas occulta*, a method customary in scholastic philosophy, according to which they took the name of the effect of the cause for the insight into the cause itself. [The tautology in determination of the cause — e.g. instinct, *horror vacui*, idiosyncrasy etc. — one is often deceived thereby. — Nothing is cause of itself — the cause must always be something other than the *causatum* — the effect.] ἐντελέχεια — the name of a cause unknown to us.",["OCR: \"kvrsXsyeia\" is garbled; read as the Greek ἐντελέχεια (entelechy), which is a reconstruction, not certain.","Wirkung = effect; Bestimmung = determination; Einsicht rendered \"insight\" (not in glossary).","The German \"Name der Wirkung der Ursache\" is kept as a chain of genitives (\"the name of the effect of the cause\")."]],"dohna-s24-005":["We are still at the naming of certain propositions in a system, [and indeed in the dogmatic system]. *Corollarium* is the proposition that can be concluded through a single inference from another proposition. The *corollaria* can be theoretical and practical. The author often presupposes information in the readers and makes corollaries that for beginners would still be theorems. This is always relative to the capacities. Wolff has translated *Corollar* as *Zusatz* (better *Folgerung*) and *Scholion* as *Anmerkung*. *Scholion* — a resting place, by which one understands that which is regarded not as a component of a system but as explanation. The *scholia* can also contain the utility, the reality and the history of the proposition, who first said it. Last there occurs here the term *Lemma* — loaned judgment [where one takes something from another system], does not belong to the territory of the science: *est peregrinum aliquid* [ — episode — ].",["OCR: \"Wolf f\" read as \"Wolff\"; \"Fälligkeiten\" read as \"Fähigkeiten\" (capacities).","\"Kenntnisse\" rendered \"information\" (glossary: Kenntnis = acquaintance; information; knowledge); Fähigkeit = capacity; Benennung = term/naming; Folgerung = consequence; folgern = conclude.","\"Lehnurteil\" rendered \"loaned judgment\"; the German word is left unglossed otherwise.","\"Corollar\", \"Zusatz\", \"Folgerung\", \"Scholion\", \"Anmerkung\" are mentioned, not used, hence italics; \"Territorio\" is in the Latin-German source form but rendered by the English \"territory\"."]],"dohna-s24-006":["Immediate inferences (*consequentiae immediatae*) are the consequences of one proposition from the other, both of which are distinguished from one another not according to matter but <(merely) according to form. They have no *medium terminus*. Immediate inferences are such inferences as cannot be transformed otherwise than through tautological subsumptions into syllogisms. [E.g. All human beings are mortal, some mortals therefore human beings, this is *consequentia immediata*. Syllogism: All human beings are mortal, is *major*, *atqui* Some mortals are mortal, this is the *minor*, tautological subsumption. *Ergo* some mortals are human beings, the conclusion. Since now tautological propositions are empty, one leaves out the *minor* and the conclusion follows immediately.] Tautological judgments can be regarded as empty, because they contribute nothing at all to distinctness, e.g. All human beings are mortal, some human beings are human beings (tautological), some human beings are mortal.",["OCR: \"Mensehen\" read as \"Menschen\"; \"Conclusion\" kept as \"conclusion\"; \"immediat\" rendered \"immediately\".","The angle bracket \"<\" before \"(bloß)\" is carried over as printed; it is not closed.","Vernunftschluß = syllogism; Schluß = inference; Deutlichkeit = distinctness; Materie = matter; Form = form; Folgerung = consequence.","\"Menschen\" is rendered \"human beings\" throughout."]],"dohna-s24-007":["The first *consequentia immediata* shall, according to our author, be called:",[]],"dohna-s24-008":["1. *per judicia aequipollentia*. I infer from one judgment to the other, which signifies precisely the same. The ground must at all times be distinguished from the sequel.",["Folge rendered \"sequel\" per the glossary (not \"consequence\", which is Folgerung).","bedeuten = signify; Grund = ground."]],"dohna-s24-009":["Now we want to go through the 4 *consequentias immediatas*. The first — *per judicia subalternata*, where I infer from the universal to the particular, [*quoad quantitate*]",["\"quantitate\" is as printed (the classical form would be *quantitatem*); left unchanged.","\"das Allgemeine\" = the universal; \"das Besondre\" = the particular (besonder = particular)."]],"dohna-s24-010":["2nd *per judicia opposita*, where I infer from | one of | 2 judgments opposed to one another to the other.",["The signs \"|\" are the editors' apparatus and are carried over where they stand; the German \"von | einem von | 2\" reads as corrupt or doubled."]],"dohna-s24-011":["3. *Per conversionem judicii*, where I invert the *termini* of the judgment, only the relation, not the quality.",["\"terminos\" in the source is read as the accusative plural of *terminus*, rendered *termini*.","The clause \"nur das Verhältnis, nicht die Qualität\" is elliptical in the German (no verb); kept so."]],"dohna-s24-012":["4. *Per contrapositionem*, where namely again now not the quantity is changed, only the quality. What was predicate in the first proposition becomes now subject, but the quantity, the affirmative and negative, remains. (*Metathesis*.) [In every affirmative judgment I can contrapose — one need only invert.]",["\"das Bejahende und Verneinende\" = \"the affirmative and negative\" (bejahen = affirm).","\"wieder nun\" kept as \"again now\"; the sense of the clause (quantity vs. quality) is as the German has it."]],"dohna-s24-013":["We (want to) now go through the individual consequences.",["\"Konsequenzen\" = consequences; \"einzelnen\" = individual."]],"dohna-s24-014":["1. *Per judicia subalternata* — what pertains to all pertains to some, e.g. all human beings are mortal, consequently some human beings are mortal.",[]],"dohna-s24-015":["2. All *oppositiones* in logic have 3 different ways. They are:",["Art rendered \"way\" (glossary: way; species; kind; manner)."]],"dohna-s24-016":["1. *contradictorie opposita* to one another — [contain everything that is required",["Fragment, continued in the next passage; the bracket is left open. The sentence is carried over mid-clause."]],"dohna-s24-017":["for conflict — ] or less than is required for conflict contain the *judicia*",["\"Widerstreit\" = conflict; \"zum Widerstreit\" kept literal.","The German word order (verb before subject) is kept: \"contain the *judicia*\"."]],"dohna-s24-018":["2. *subcontrarie opposita* [therefore they can both be true] and",[]],"dohna-s24-019":["3. *contrarie opposita* [contain more than belongs thereto, therefore can",["Fragment; the bracket is left open and the clause is completed in the next passage."]],"dohna-s24-020":["both be false.]",[]],"dohna-s24-021":["Now we go to their rules.",[]],"dohna-s24-022":["The rule of the first: No proposition is opposed *contradictorie* to the other, except the universally affirmative to the particularly negative. —",["\"entgegengesetzt\" = opposed; \"allgemein\" = universal(ly); \"besonders\" = particular(ly)."]],"dohna-s24-023":["One adopted certain signs. Universally affirmative with a, universally negative e, *particulariter* affirmative i, o particular negative. One expresses this thus:",["The German has no verb in \"allgemein verneinende e\" and \"o partikulär verneinende\"; kept elliptical."]],"dohna-s24-024":["*Asserit a, negat e, sed universaliter ambo* —",["Latin mnemonic kept; roughly, \"a affirms, e denies, but both universally\" (given here only for reference, not part of the translation)."]],"dohna-s24-025":["*Asserit i, negat o, sed particulariter ambo.* —",["Latin mnemonic kept; roughly, \"i affirms, o denies, but both particularly\" (reference only)."]],"dohna-s24-026":["If one of 2 contradictory judgments is true, then the other is false, and conversely.",["\"umgekehrt\" = \"conversely\"."]],"dohna-s24-027":["We come",["Fragment, continued in the next passage."]],"dohna-s24-028":["2. to the judgments *subcontrarie oppositae*. Particular judgments are opposed to one another *subcontrarie*. The",["Breaks off in mid-sentence; continued in the next passage."]],"dohna-s24-029":["3. judgments, *contrarie oppositae*, are those where two universal judgments are opposed to one another like a and e.",["\"Die\" at the end of the preceding passage is the subject of this sentence; the sentence is therefore continuous across the two passages."]],"dohna-s24-030":["In universal judgments one says more than is needed for opposition. For opposition, however, nothing more is required than that a and o, e and i are in conflict.",["\"nötig\" rendered \"needed\", \"erfordert\" rendered \"required\"; \"im Widerstreit\" = in conflict."]],"dohna-s24-031":["3. *Conversio judicii* consists in the inference of one judgment from the other through *metathesin terminorum* [in that one makes what was previously subject into the predicate], yet through retention of the quality of the judgment. The *judicium conversum* is given, the *convertens* must be concluded from the *consequentia immediata*. The *conversio simplex* is that in which the quantity remains the same. The concept of the subject is considered as if it stands under the predicate as *locus geometricus*. [Hence one cannot invert these propositions, that would bring the *latiorem* under the *angustiorem*.] Universal affirmative judgments can properly be inverted only *per accidens*, particular affirmative ones however (*simpliciter*). All negative judgments can be inverted *simpliciter* — the opposition is always reciprocal. All universal affirmative etc. Contraposition through *metathesin*.",["'f' after 'simplex' and 'Prädikat' stray OCR marks (or editors' sign); omitted. 'simphciter' read as *simpliciter*.","Schluß = inference; Urteil = judgment; Begriff = concept; Qualität/Quantität left as 'quality'/'quantity'.","'Alle allgemein bejahenden usw.' is as broken off in the German; 'Sätze' = propositions.","'als steht er' is rendered literally ('as if it stands'); the German construction is loose."]],"dohna-s24-032":["[NB. Tuesday, the 17th. Here Kant took a vacation of more than •4 weeks.]",["'•4' is an OCR reading of the numeral, uncertain; kept as it stands."]],"dohna-s24-033":["[Monday, the 20th of August]",[]],"dohna-s24-034":["An inference of the understanding is the sequence of one judgment from the other, *absque judicio intermedio*. An inference of reason is the cognition of the necessity of a proposition through the subsumption of its condition under a general rule. That is called *judicium intermedium*.",["Verstandesschluß = inference of the understanding; Vernunftschluß = inference of reason (glossary: also 'syllogism'); Folge = sequence; Erkenntnis = cognition; Satz = proposition."]],"dohna-s24-035":["[An inference of reason is the sequence of one inference of the understanding from the other through a *judicium intermedium*. Inferences of the understanding infer from the universal to the particular, inferences of the power of judgment from the particular to the universal. It substitutes: *atqui* that is the case from the general rule. Its inferences are never strict — only an attempt *in subsidium* — so analogy and induction.]",["Urteilskraft = power of judgment. 'Sie substituiert :' the feminine 'Sie' presumably refers to Urteilskraft; left unresolved. 'von der allgemeinen Regel' kept literally and is obscure."]],"dohna-s24-036":["All that is extended is divisible, consequently bodies are divisible, is no *consequentia immediata*. In a universal inference of reason it is necessary that it be brought under a general rule, and that secondly one subsume the condition under the general rule. E.g. All that is extended is divisible, *atqui* all bodies are (extended), *ergo* all bodies are divisible. Every inference of reason consists of 3 propositions:",["'Bedingimg' read as Bedingung (condition). Parentheses around 'ausgedehnt' are OCR braces in the original, rendered as parentheses.","'Allgemeinen Vernunftschluß' rendered literally as 'universal inference of reason'."]],"dohna-s24-037":["1. the general rule, i.e. the major premise, *major propositio*,",["Obersatz = major premise."]],"dohna-s24-038":["2. the condition of the subsumption of a certain proposition under a general rule, *minor*,",[]],"dohna-s24-039":["3. the proposition that is to be understood through subsumption under a general rule, *conclusio*.",["'verstanden' = understood (verstehen); the German is kept as it stands."]],"dohna-s24-040":["Analogy and induction are inferences of the power of judgment. In all inferences one can never infer from the particular to the universal (with certainty). An inference of (induction) is that when I infer from some things that belong to a certain genus to all things of the genus, that it belongs to all the remaining. [To know how one is to seek the diversity, how one reflects, cannot be taught. — ]",["Gattung = genus. 'Bei allen Schlüssen' is as printed; the sense is obscure and left so. Parentheses around 'Induktion' are as in the source. 'Verschiedenheit' = diversity."]],"dohna-s24-041":["According to the inference of analogy: If 2 things agree in as many [determinations] as I have become acquainted with, then I infer that they | in | the other | determinations | also agree. I infer therefore from some determinations that I cognize that the other ones also belong to the thing. It is the inference of a provisional judgment. One reserves to oneself to alter it. [Inference according to analogy that animals have a soul — Why . . . then? If wasps have drunk of forbidden honey, and now want to sting, they fall, but they have . . . faculty of desire. This also applies to plants, the fly-trap leaf. They seek air and sun and water.]",["The bars | | and the dots '. . .' are as printed; the stray 'f' marks are OCR slips and omitted; 'kennen lernen' = become acquainted with, 'erkennen' = cognize. 'Fliege nfangblatt' read as 'Fliegenfangblatt'.","Begehrungsvermögen = faculty of desire; 'Seele' = soul. The wasp sentence is obscure and kept so.","The text after 'aber sie haben' is gappy in the source (. . .); not filled."]],"dohna-s24-042":["No logician has yet properly worked out analogy and induction. This field is still open. A judgment that makes out what is true or false is called determining . . .",["bestimmend = determining; the passage breaks off with '. . .' as printed."]],"dohna-s24-043":["There are three kinds of inferences of reason, categorical, hypothetical and disjunctive. In the first there are only 3 *termini*. The ground of this lies in this, that in all inferences of reason a rule lies at the basis, under which one subsumes. The 3rd is the application — *conclusio*. In every inference of reason we have matter and form. The first consists of the premises [the premises one calls the matter of the inference of reason], the latter of the consequence. Logic has not at all to concern itself with falsity *quoad materiam*. Even if one has inferred correctly from false premises, the conclusion is nevertheless correct. No rational being is infallible. The human being is a rational being, therefore no human being is infallible. The conclusion is correct, the premises false. For logic only arguing and the *error aut veritas ratiocinii quoad formam* belong.",["'lvypothetische' read as hypothetische; 'Hegt' read as liegt; 'Kern' read as Kein; 'ratiocinn' read as *ratiocinii*; 'Mensch' = human being.","Grund = ground in 'Der Grund davon', but 'zum Grunde liegt' = 'lies at the basis'. Konsequenz = consequence; Materie = matter."]],"dohna-s24-044":["The *major propositio* is always an assertoric proposition. [The *major propositio* contains the rule, the *minor* the subsumption, the *conclusio* the sequence. Inference of reason the consciousness of the necessity of a proposition through subsumption under a general rule. — ]",["Bewußtsein = consciousness; Folge = sequence."]],"dohna-s24-045":["Each of these inferences of reason has its particular principle. The categorical inferences of reason infer according to the *dicto de omni et nullo*. The hypothetical inferences of reason according to the rule *a ratione ad rationatum valet consequentia* or *a negatione rationati* (*posita ratione ponitur rationatum*).",["'schheßen' and 'nuUo' read as schließen, *nullo*. Prinzip = principle; besonder = particular."]],"dohna-s24-046":["The *dictum de omni et nullo* is the sequence from the *principio contradictionis* [the proposition of contradiction is the principle of judgments, not of inferences of reason]. It is called:",["Satz des Widerspruchs = 'proposition of contradiction'; Folge = sequence. 'Heißt' = 'is called' (or 'reads'), ambiguous; kept as 'is called'."]],"dohna-s24-047":["*Quicquid valet de genere, valet etiam de omnibus sub genere aut specie — quicquid non valet de genere aut specie, non valet etiam de omnibus sub isto genere aut ista specie contentis.*",["Latin left untranslated; text as printed."]],"dohna-s24-048":["I can infer thus, for the concept of the whole contains the marks of the individual. Kant expresses this:",["Merkmal = mark (*nota*); Begriff = concept."]],"dohna-s24-049":["*Nota notae est nota rei ipsius* (the general principle of all affirmative inferences) — *Repugnans notae repugnat rei ipsi* (the principle of all negative inferences).",["The angle brackets in the OCR ('<clas', '<(') are read as parentheses; 'clas' read as 'das'."]],"dohna-s24-050":["[E.g. All bodies are alterable. I seek *notam intermediam* divisible, a nearer mark of all bodies. Alterable is a mark of divisible, divisible a mark of body, hence alterable a mark of body.",["The opening bracket is unclosed here; it closes in dohna-s24-056."]],"dohna-s24-051":["divisible",[]],"dohna-s24-052":["Body — alterable",["Diagram fragment; layout lost in OCR."]],"dohna-s24-053":["divisible — alterable",["Diagram fragment; layout lost in OCR."]],"dohna-s24-054":["Body — divisible",["Diagram fragment; layout lost in OCR."]],"dohna-s24-055":["— — alterable according to the principle of all affirmative inferences.",["Diagram fragment; layout lost in OCR."]],"dohna-s24-056":["Inferences of reason consist therefore in this, that we compare *notam notae* with the *nota rei ipsius* *per notam intermediam*.]",["Closing bracket matches the opening one in dohna-s24-050."]],"dohna-s24-057":["In disjunctive propositions the *membra disjunctionis*, however many of them there may be, must be considered as one member (according to the *principium exclusi medii inter duo contradictoria*), for in contradictory judgments there can only be 2 propositions, a and *non a*.",["'Prinzipium' as printed in the text; 'Glied' = member. Angle bracket read as parenthesis."]],"dohna-s24-058":["We come to the doctrine",["Lehre = doctrine; the sentence continues in the next heading."]],"dohna-s24-059":["Of the categorical inferences of reason",[]],"dohna-s24-060":["They go merely to the categorical propositions. — *Major terminus* is the predicate, *minor* the subject and [ in addition | the *medius*. There can only be 4 conclusions. *Medius terminus* is in the *major* (*propositione loco*) of the subject and in the *minor* (*loco*) of the predicate. Every *conclusio* consists of a subject and a predicate. The 4 different syllogistic figures we want to present here.",["The bars | and bracket are as printed. The Latin forms are obscure ('propositione loco' / 'loco') and left as they stand; literal 'in the *major*... of the subject' reflects 'loco subjecti'. 'Conclusionen' rendered 'conclusions' without explanation.","Possible gap between dohna-s24-060 and -062 (no -061); the passage begins -062 mid-sentence."]],"dohna-s24-062":["must represent this to oneself as a chain of 3 links.",["Begins mid-sentence; the subject/verb ('man') is partly missing in the OCR ('muß' stands without it) and is not supplied."]],"dohna-s24-063":["— Bodies are divisible.",[]],"dohna-s24-064":["The soul is not divisible.",[]],"dohna-s24-065":["bodily.",["Fragment from an example; context missing."]],"dohna-s24-066":["major is the rule. Minor is always affirmative. For I say",["The passage breaks off; continues in the next."]],"dohna-s24-067":["this is contained under the condition.] In the 2nd figure one always finds a negative conclusion.",["The closing bracket has no opening in this passage; kept as printed."]],"dohna-s24-068":["in it one never infers immediately, but one must",["Continues in the next fragment."]],"dohna-s24-069":["insert *conclusio immediata*.",[]],"dohna-s24-070":["The *major propositio* must always (in all categorical inferences of reason) be universal.",["Angle bracket read as parenthesis."]],"dohna-s24-071":["In the 3rd figure we can have merely a particular conclusion.",[]],"dohna-s24-072":["The 4th figure never has a universal affirmative or universal",["Continues in the next fragment."]],"dohna-s24-073":["negative conclusion. The inference of the 2nd, 3rd and 4th figure",["The stray '15' is a line number and is omitted."]],"dohna-s24-074":["must in thought always be brought into the first.",[]],"dohna-s24-075":["[Only the inferences according to the first figure are categorical. The others",[]],"dohna-s24-076":["are all *ratiocinia impura*. — The predicate of the *conclusio* must namely",[]],"dohna-s24-077":["be related to the *medium terminum* in the categorical inference of reason. *Ratiocinia hybrida*, where besides the premises and the *conclusio*",["The stray '20' is a line number and is omitted. 'terminum' as printed."]],"dohna-s24-078":["still a *consequentia immediata*. — ]",[]],"dohna-s24-079":["We must now go through the general rules of all inferences of reason",["'Vernunft Schlüsse' read as Vernunftschlüsse."]],"dohna-s24-080":["go through.",["'clurchgehn' read as 'durchgehn'; the fragment repeats the verb of the preceding line (completed in the German as 'durchgehn')."]],"dohna-s24-081":["In every syllogism there can be only 3 *termini*. If a *terminus* is taken in twofold meaning, then the inference in fact has 4 *termini*. For this the school expression is: the *terminus* goes on 4 feet. The *terminus medius* must lie in the premises.",["Vernunftschluß = syllogism; Schluß = inference (both rendered per the glossary). Bedeutung = meaning.","OCR \"Tat4terniini\" read as \"Tat 4 termini\"."]],"dohna-s24-082":["Now come some errors that are easily committed:",[]],"dohna-s24-083":["1. *Ex puris negativis <(praemissis> nil sequitur* [for the *minor propositio* must be affirmative,]",["The angle brackets and square brackets are carried over as printed; the unbalanced \"<(\" is as in the OCR."]],"dohna-s24-084":["2. *Ex puris particularibus nil sequitur* [follows from the concept of a syllogism. At least the *major* must be *universalis*.]",["Begriff = concept; Vernunftschluß = syllogism."]],"dohna-s24-085":["3. *Conclusio sequitur partem debiliorem*, that is to say: one of the premises is called *pars debilior* if it is negative or particular, then the *conclusio* too is particular or negative. [ — Only the *minor* can be particular. — ]",["verneinend rendered \"negative\"; bejahend (elsewhere) \"affirmative\"."]],"dohna-s24-086":["Negation is indeed opposition, contradiction.",["Widerspruch = contradiction."]],"dohna-s24-087":["We now come to the special rules of the syllogisms of the first figure. They are called",["besondern rendered \"special\" (glossary: particular; special), to keep \"particular\" for the logical quantity. The passage breaks off; the next passage continues it."]],"dohna-s24-088":["*Major sit universalis, minor affirmans* — this fits all categorical inferences. Now further *Minor sit affirmans* likewise for all. The *minor propositio* is indeed supposed to express that the condition pertains to this or that. All inferences in the remaining figures are not *ratiocinia pura*, but *hybrida* — i.e., those that actually consist not of 3 propositions, but in which a 4th has been mixed in *per consequentiam immediatam*. [Another *terminus* in the *ratiocinio* is called the 4th foot.] It is only a contrivance, by transposition of the *medii termini* nevertheless to produce a correct inference. [The transposition, *metathesis terminorum*, is twofold, *per contrapositionem, per conversionem*. The *Conclusio* in the 3 remaining figures is never universal affirmative — according to the first one has all 4 kinds (gen. <u.) part. affirmative and negative). This already shows that those go on stilts.]",["The repetition of \"Minor sit affirmans\" is as in the source. \"(allg. <u.)\" is carried over as printed; \"u.\" = \"and\" (und) is left abbreviated.","Schluß = inference; Satz = proposition; Bedingung = condition; Künstelei rendered \"contrivance\".","Square brackets carried over as printed."]],"dohna-s24-089":["In the 2nd figure, *Major <(sit)> universalis, conclusio negativa*.",["OCR \"iiniversalis\" read as *universalis*. Angle brackets kept as printed."]],"dohna-s24-090":["In the third figure, *Minor* (because it is subsumption) *affirmans, conclusio particularis*. [The alteration occurs in the *minori propositione*. If this was previously general <or particular) affirmative, as always, then it can only be converted *per accidens* (be) — had it been particular affirmative, then: *conclusio sequitur partem debiliorem*, so it is also particular.]",["The German pronoun \"er\" (referring to the *Minor*) is rendered \"it\", since English has no grammatical gender here.","The brackets and parentheses are as printed (\"<or particular)\" and \"(be)\")."]],"dohna-s24-091":["In the 4th figure: *Conclusio vel negativa vel particularis*.",[]],"dohna-s24-092":["a a e",["Marginal or figure notation as it stands; no context to interpret it."]],"dohna-s24-094":["Reusch's Logic. The conclusion of the 2nd figure is negative, because the syllogism cannot be made into the first figure. In the 3rd figure a *metathesis terminorum* must be undertaken with the *minor propositio*. A generally affirmative proposition can be converted only *per accidens*. The inference in the first figure is the only pure one. (*Ars non habet osorem nisi ignorantem.*)",["\"osorem\" is left as printed in the Latin."]],"dohna-s24-095":["The *modus* of a proposition is the quality and quantity.",["Satz = proposition."]],"dohna-s24-097":["p. 110]. One spells thus: Cel — ar — ent. In the syl-",["Fragment; breaks off in mid-word (\"Sil-\" = syllables). The unmatched \"]\" is as in the source."]],"dohna-s24-099":["The *modi* all come out at 64 different kinds",["Art = kind. The sentence lacks its end in the passage."]],"dohna-s24-101":["From each there are 16 different inferences. But 28 drop out according to the rule: *ex puris negativis nihil sequitur*. 18 drop out according to the rule: *conclusio sequitur* — — *partem debiliorem*. 8 fall according to the rule that a negative conclusion cannot follow from merely affirmative ones. There remain no more than 10 *modi*, of which only 8 *modi utiles*. —",["The OCR shows a stray apostrophe after the dashes in the second rule; it is omitted. The dashes mark a gap or interruption in the rule as printed."]],"dohna-s24-102":["The hypothetical syllogism is that in which the *major propositio* is a conditioned proposition. The *modus ponens* infers from the ground to the sequel. The *modus tollens* from the sequel to the ground. It cannot happen that I infer *a consequente sublato ad antecedentem*",["Folge = sequel (glossary); Grund = ground; bedingt = conditioned.","The passage breaks off without a period; it is not completed."]],"dohna-s24-103":["The dilemma is a hypothetical (judgment), whose consequence a disjunctive judgment. [E.g., if this world is not the best, then God either knew no better one, or he could not or did not want to create a better one.]",["The German has no verb in the first sentence (\"dessen Konsequenz ein disjunktives Urteil\"); the ellipsis is kept.","Urteil = judgment; Welt = world; Konsequenz = consequence."]],"dohna-s24-104":["A formal syllogism is now *Enunciation*, where something is expressed *explicite*. Under the *ratiocinia cryptica* belongs the enthymeme. [E.g., all animals are mortal, consequently also all human beings. The *medius terminus* is missing.]",["\"Enunciation\" is printed so in the source (not \"Enunciatio\"); italicized as a foreign word.","förmlich = formal; Vernunftschluß = syllogism."]],"dohna-s24-105":["A syllogism is cryptic *per transmissionem praemissarum* or *per omissionem praemissarum*.",[]],"dohna-s24-106":["The *syllogismus contractus* is when I infer *per medium terminum*.",["OCR \"terminuni\" read as *terminum*."]],"dohna-s24-107":["All syllogisms are supposed to give necessity in their conclusion. If 2 things that stand under the same genus agree in all respects that I know, I infer that this is so with the others too. I infer from the identity of some properties to the identity of all. An inference can never bring forth a cognition from empirical propositions. [The inferences from induction and analogy are only crutches of human reason.] Only from general premises can we infer. Yet no proposition of experience gives us the *universitas simpliciter*, but only *secundum quid*, so far as we know it. The inference, | if I | infer from some predicates of things to all the remaining, is an inference according to analogy. E.g., Mercury, Venus, Earth, Mars, Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus are dark planets. Now I infer by induction that the remaining planets, which perhaps can still be discovered, might also be dark. *Ob paritatem rationis* one also calls the inference according to induction.",["Stücken (\"in allen Stücken\") rendered \"respects\"; literally \"pieces\".","Eigenschaften = properties (not in the glossary). kennen = know; Erkenntnis = cognition; Gattung = genus; Ding = thing; Erfahrungssatz = proposition of experience.","The signs | are carried over where they stand (the German sentence is syntactically broken at this point).","\"Ob paritatem rationis\" is the Latin as printed; \"Z. B.\" rendered \"E.g.\""]],"dohna-s24-108":["*Exempla illustrant, non probant.* Thus there is no inference by example.",["Exempelschluß rendered \"inference by example\"."]],"dohna-s24-109":["A syllogism that is false in the <matter) is called a deceptive inference, and if it is false in <*forma*) — paralogism. *Sophisma* is an inference false in *forma*. [The word sophist has not always had the bad meaning of rationalizer.]",["The OCR shows \"{Materie)\" and \"-(forma)\", read as the editors' angle-bracket insertions \"<Materie)\" and \"<forma)\"; the reading is uncertain.","Trugschluß rendered \"deceptive inference\" (literal); Vernünftler rendered \"rationalizer\" (cf. vernünfteln = rationalize)."]],"dohna-s24-110":["One believes of many false inferences that they are so in *forma*, but very often they are so in *materia*. The old Megarian school, founded by Euclid the Megarian (not the geometer), occupied itself very much with such inferences, turned many nicely.",["\"wendete viele artig\" is left as obscure as in the German (the verb's sense, \"turned\", and the missing object are not resolved)."]],"dohna-s24-111":["*Achilles, Cretensis, Acervus.* [See Darjes, Logic.]",["OCR \"Daries\" corrected to Darjes."]],"dohna-s24-112":["[If you say, you are lying and in saying this speak the truth, then you speak the truth, meiern you lie — is false in *materia*: in that you say that you are lying. —",["\"meiern\" is garbled in the OCR and cannot be read with certainty; it is left untranslated. \"Wahrssheit\" corrected to Wahrheit.","The bracket opened here closes in the following passage."]],"dohna-s24-113":["Epimenides says: All Cretans are liars. *Ipse vero est Cretensis — ergo ipse est mendax*, so his assertion too would be a lie.]",["Behauptung = assertion."]],"dohna-s24-114":["One always adds more: how many grains belong to the heap? In the gradual increase there is no transition to another genus. [For concepts of comparative magnitude there is no specific difference at a determinate place. This serves mainly in morals to warn against defects of the *medii* <of mediocrity). For things that are specifically different, beware of making a difference according to degree.]",["\"{der Mediokrität)\" in the OCR is read as the editors' \"<der Mediokrität)\".","\"Überschritt\" rendered \"transition\" (possibly an OCR or transcript slip for Übergang; kept as read).","Gattung = genus; Begriff = concept; Größe = magnitude; Grad = degree."]],"dohna-s24-115":["*Cornutus. Quicquid non amisisti, habes. Cornua non amisisti. Ergo habes.*",[]],"dohna-s24-116":["*Calvus.* How many hairs away for a bald head? — I can define nothing by the more or less. —",["definieren rendered \"define\" (cf. Erklärung = explanation; definition)."]],"dohna-s24-117":["The *sophisma heterozeteseos* is called, if it concerns a question in dispute, *sophisma ignorationis elenchi*. — E.g., the proof of the immortality of the soul — one proved a future life.",["OCR \"Sophisrna\" and \"elenehi\" corrected to *sophisma* and *elenchi*. \"man bewiese\" is subjunctive; \"one proved\" is kept as ambiguous as the German."]],"dohna-s24-118":["*Ratiocinia* can also, insofar as they are subordinated to one another, be *composita*.",[]],"dohna-s24-119":["The *ratiocinatio episyllogistica* occurs when the conclusion of a syllogism is the premise of a given one. In all academic disputations it is inferred *per prosyllogismos*.",["OCR \"Conchision\" read as Conclusion; a stray quotation mark before \"wird\" is omitted."]],"dohna-s24-120":["[*Sorites*, a series of syllogisms according to episyllogistic order.] Some explanations are yet to be added here:",["Erklärungen = explanations (glossary also: definitions; declarations)."]],"dohna-s24-121":["A *saltus in probando*, when one combines a ground with a remote sequel, without an intermediate proposition. — [One can leap legitimately if the intermediate proposition understands itself. — ]",["\"sich von selbst versteht\" rendered literally (\"understands itself\"); Grund = ground; Folge = sequel; Satz = proposition."]],"dohna-s24-122":["*Circulus in probando*, when one cites as known in the ground of proof that which is to be proved.",["OCR \"Averden\" read as werden. Beweisgrund = ground of proof."]],"dohna-s24-123":["*Petitio principii* — to assume *gratis*. —",[]],"dohna-s24-124":["Of proofs that are faulty one says they say too much or too little, that one is *vitium*, this one *defectus*. He who proves too much proves nothing at all. Just as he who says less than all the true does not lie, but he who says more than the true certainly lies.",["\"jener ... dieser\" rendered \"that one ... this one\" (the former, the latter); \"Vitium\" is capitalized in the source, rendered *vitium*.","Beweis = proof; Wahre = the true."]],"dohna-s24-125":["[Topics, the science which shows how one is to determine logical places.] Topics is the art of bringing one's concepts under certain main concepts (compartments) | : *generalia capita argumentorum aut genera in quibus reperiuntur*. A particular maxim one calls a *locus topicus* or, since that is tautological, a *locus communis*. So one calls a canon such as: accustomed young, done old. *Locus grammaticus*, e.g. etymology, *locus logicus* like the *Dictum de omni et nullo* — inference *a toto ad partes* etc. *Locus metaphysicus*, *ethicus* according to whether I derive something from the *utile* or the *honestum*. *Argumentum ab utili* etc. *Locus physicus* — from the purposes of nature. From the *locus",["Sentenz rendered 'maxim' (not in glossary). 'jung gewohnt, alt getan' is a proverb, rendered literally; the German is elliptical.","Zwecke = 'purposes' (glossary: Zweck = end; purpose).","'Örter' rendered 'places'; Latin locus is kept in Latin. 'Fächer' = 'compartments'.","The passage breaks off mid-sentence; it continues in the next passage. The sign | is carried over as printed. Abbreviated 'utili oder honesto' is rendered with the Latin neuter forms as 'the *utile* or the *honestum*' (ablatives in the original)."]],"dohna-s24-126":["*geometricus* may well properly have",["Fragment; continues 'die Benennung hergekommen sein' in the next passage (sentence continues across ids). 'eigentlich' rendered 'properly'."]],"dohna-s24-127":["the term have come from, that is,",["Fragment; 'Benennung' = 'term' per glossary. Syntax continues from the preceding passage ('von dem locus geometricus mag wohl eigentlich die Benennung hergekommen sein')."]],"dohna-s24-128":["when a problem can be solved in infinitely different ways.",["Aufgabe = problem; Art = way (glossary)."]],"dohna-s24-129":["[Parallel, the *locus geometricus* of the",["Fragment; continues in the next passage."]],"dohna-s24-130":["equal triangles on one base.]",[]],"dohna-s24-131":["But it is impossible that philosophy can place itself on a par with mathematics in this.",[]],"dohna-s24-132":["According to our division we go to the last part of logic, which one can regard as a short appendix:",["Einteilung = division (glossary)."]],"dohna-s24-133":["II. The Doctrine of Method",["Methodenlehre rendered 'Doctrine of Method' (Lehre = doctrine)."]],"dohna-s24-134":["One also calls it the practical part. Properly, however, there is no practical logic. For it is not an organon, only a canon. This appendix serves for the critique of cognition.",["Organon and Canon are given in the German as the original foreign words; kept as such. Erkenntnis = cognition."]],"dohna-s24-135":["The doctrine of method is the prescription of the possibility of a system of cognition of the understanding and of reason. It is therefore the doctrine of method [*Methodus* — way in which a cognition can attain scientific form.] Method is combination of thoughts. — We can think it in two ways, as *methodus logica* [not merely way of teaching, but way of thinking], which one could call manner, it rests entirely on rules: the *methodus aesthetica* rests solely on taste and lets itself be brought under no rules.",["'Verstandes- und Vernunfterkenntnisses' rendered 'cognition of the understanding and of reason'; the German compound is ambiguous between 'cognition of understanding and reason' (as source or as object).","Verbindung = combination; Art = way; Manier = 'manner' (Art zu denken rendered 'way of thinking').","'wissenschaftliche Form' = 'scientific form' (Wissenschaft = science)."]],"dohna-s24-136":["The word *methodus* is not adequately translated by 'way of teaching'. For there is just as much a method as a way of thinking as of teaching.",["'Lehrart' rendered 'way of teaching'; Denk- |wie| als Lehrart: the apparatus |wie| is carried over in the German but the printed text is evidently garbled ('Denk- |wie| als Lehrart'); rendered with the corrected sense 'as a way of thinking as of teaching', 'wie' (as) taken as the editor's insertion. Doubtful reading."]],"dohna-s25-001":["[Presentation",["Vortrag = 'presentation' (lecture/delivery); fragment of a bracketed list continuing in the following passages."]],"dohna-s25-002":["1. systematic,",[]],"dohna-s25-003":["2. fragmentary,",[]],"dohna-s25-004":["1. methodical,",[]],"dohna-s25-005":["2. tumultuary.]",["'tumultuarisch' rendered 'tumultuary'."]],"dohna-s25-006":["Now the various methods come up.",["'kommen ... ran' is colloquial (herankommen); rendered literally."]],"dohna-s25-007":["The first division is into synthetic and analytic. The latter is that in which I proceed from the sequels to the grounds, the former that in which I proceed from the grounds to the sequels. [A division is unsystematic if one cannot see from it why there could not be still more members of the division.] In philosophizing one can proceed synthetically and analytically. The mathematical way of teaching is synthetic method. It distinguishes itself from all others in that it demonstrates through intuition. Way of thinking is always something moral. Yet one could say, way of thinking would be the *methodus*. The method is",["Folgen = sequels, Gründe = grounds (glossary: Folge = sequel; Grund = ground).","Denkungsart = 'way of thinking' and Denkart = 'way of thinking' (the two German words are both rendered 'way of thinking'; the OCR/printed 'Denkungsart ... Denkart' distinction is lost).","'dartut' = 'demonstrates' (not in glossary); Anschauung = intuition.","The passage breaks off; continues in the next passages."]],"dohna-s25-008":["1. popular — this is for the common understanding — *in concreto*.",["gemein = common; Verstand = understanding."]],"dohna-s25-009":["2. scholastic — belongs for sciences — *in abstracto*.",["'gehört für Wissenschaften' rendered literally."]],"dohna-s25-010":["To orient oneself means to put oneself in a certain standpoint, where one can conveniently consider things *in concreto* f.",["The stray 'f' after 'concreto' in the OCR is unclear and left as printed; probably an OCR artifact (possibly a footnote sign). Betrachtung = consideration."]],"dohna-s25-011":["*Methodus syllogistica*. Many have even taken pains to present mathematics in this way. — [But it is at bottom only a mere subtlety.]",["'Spitzfindigkeit' rendered 'subtlety'."]],"dohna-s25-012":["The way of teaching according to tables — a certain tree which has many main and subsidiary branches. —",["OCR 'Haupt -und' corrected to 'Haupt- und'."]],"dohna-s25-013":["Science is a cognition that is derived from certain principles and hangs together in a system.",["Prinzipien = principles; 'zusammenhängt' = 'hangs together'."]],"dohna-s25-014":["The Socratic way of teaching — the Platonic. —",[]],"dohna-s25-015":["*Methodus vel est acroamatica* [where one alone presents, without asking or answering] *vel erotematica* [in question and answer]. This is either catechetical or dialogical. In the first the questioner is at all times the teacher, in the second the one questioned. The acroamatic method is also the Platonic.",["'Diese' (this) is ambiguous in referring to the erotematic method; preserved. 'der Befragte' = 'the one questioned'."]],"dohna-s25-016":["Philosophical matters should not be presented catechetically. Catechesis can be mechanical or *judicieuse* [through association of representations — that cultivates the understanding].",["Materien = matters (Materie = matter); Vorstellungen = representations. 'judicieuse' kept in French form as in the source."]],"dohna-s25-017":["Can one present religion catechetically? [it is thereby already led astray into prejudices.] Properly this is not the best method, the dialogical would be. The common man cannot well grasp a connected presentation. The historical cognitions can be presented catechetically. —",["'wird ... verleitet': 'it' is Religion (sie); preserved as pronoun. 'zusammenhängenden Vortrag' = 'connected presentation'."]],"dohna-s25-018":["Socrates said: I am the midwife of the other's thoughts. He derived everything from their concepts, which they just could not properly develop. — [This way of the catechetical method is the only one that may be applied in philosophical objects. It is at bottom erotematic catechesis, although it is called dialogical. — Catechisms should be so arranged — first derive everything from reason and thereupon build positive religion — for the human being as he now is.]",["OCR 'entwickehi' corrected to 'entwickeln'. Gegenständen = objects. 'ihren Begriffen' (their concepts) — antecedent of 'ihren' left as in German."]],"dohna-s25-019":["One can think tumultuarily and methodically. The latter, when we want to bring forth a cognition, is called meditating. But before one thinks methodically, one must at all times also think tumultuarily, roam about, in order to seek out everything that is pertinent.",["'Das letztere' = 'the latter' (methodical thinking). 'einschlägt' rendered 'is pertinent'."]],"dohna-s25-020":["1. what do I want? — [Many who wrote books did not know it at all or only obscurely. — ]",["'dunkel' = 'obscurely'."]],"dohna-s25-021":["2. what does it come down to in this.",[]],"dohna-s25-022":["These 2 points indicate the *judicieuse* man. —",["'judicieusen' kept in French-derived form as in the source."]],"dohna-s25-023":["[*Homonyma*, expressions that signify many things, are proofs of the poverty of a language.] Designation of cognition, speaking, writing — But everything that the author says about this does not belong to logic. — *Termini technici* one must not forge without necessity. Yet they belong at times to scholastic rigor. One does not understand a thing before one can communicate it to others. Words with which one can combine no distinct concept (e.g. *entelechia*) are called *termini inanes*. *Conceptus deceptor*, deceptive concept, usually contains 2 concepts which in and for themselves are distinct — but together are not understandable. [| Concepts | that seem to signify something and in the end signify nothing, like apathy and idiosyncrasy.] Further *terminus familiaris* [familiar expression] — those that occur in common life.",["bedeuten = signify; deutlich = distinct; Ausdrücke = expressions; Begriff = concept.","'Bezeichnung der Erkenntnis' = 'Designation of cognition'; OCR stray quotation mark before 'was der Autor' removed.","'Idiosynkrasis' rendered 'idiosyncrasy'; '| Begriffe |' apparatus signs carried over."]],"dohna-s25-024":["[Logomachy, dispute through misunderstanding of words, dispute about words. Such a one cannot last long. One would have discovered it long ago, so e.g. the dispute between | defenders of | freedom and defenders of natural necessity.]",["Wortstreit = 'dispute about words'; notwendigkeit = necessity (notwendig = necessary). Apparatus signs | carried over."]],"dohna-s25-025":["Very often people believe they understand one another only when they are in fact furthest from one another.",["'gar am weitesten' rendered 'in fact furthest'; 'verstehen' = understand."]],"dohna-s25-026":["*Terminus ambiguus* [ambiguous], *vagus*, an expression of which one cannot quite make out what one is to think by it — happiness — such a one one can determine. Proposition — *Judicium verbis prolatum* — no — only assertoric {judgments are) propositions. [ — *Propositio* is a *judicium categoricum*, thus a kind of *judicium*, and *judicium* is not synonymous with *propositio*.]",["Satz = proposition (glossary); Glück = happiness. Urteil = judgment; the editorial braces { } and the missing opening parenthesis in '{Urteile sind)' are carried over as in the OCR ('Urteile' is the reading restored from 'Urteile').","Art = kind; 'gleichbedeutend' = 'synonymous'."]],"dohna-s25-027":["*Syllogismus* is called *ratiocinium formale*, not *verbis prolatum*. With words one must indeed express it every time, aloud or softly. [It depends only on the expression of the concepts {through thought or words).]",["Syllogismus is kept as in the source; braces carried over as in OCR. 'sachte' = 'softly'. Ausdruck = expression."]],"dohna-s25-028":["[What logic is with regard to thinking, style is with regard to the designation of thinking. Logical perfection of style is the scholastic — the aesthetic is opposed to it.]",["Vollkommenheit = perfection; Bezeichnung = designation."]],"dohna-s25-029":["The purity, elegance of style is called purism, if one exaggerates it. Concinnity. Hybrid words: f, longimetry, half Greek, half Latin. Not talkativeness — *ornatus* — (no sonority). A speech — *sermo* — *oratio*, if it is solemn. Eloquence suits every writing. Oratory, *ars persuadendi*, to win applause for the present moment.",["Doubtful: the stray 'f' after 'Zwitterwörter:' is an OCR artifact, left as printed. 'Longimetrie' kept as 'longimetry'.","Wohlredenheit = 'eloquence' and Beredsamkeit = 'oratory' (two German words distinguished); both are conventionally 'eloquence'/'oratory'; the choice is mine. 'Redseligkeit' = 'talkativeness'."]],"dohna-s25-030":["In scholarly presentation — descendence and coascendence. [Eloquence, *copia* of words, fluency — art of applying this *copia*. Oratory, the skill with this art of guiding others according to one's aims, of simulating the false as true.]",["Here Beredtheit = 'eloquence', Wohlredenheit = 'fluency', Beredsamkeit = 'oratory': the terms are not consistently distinguishable in English and the rendering is tentative. Absichten = aims (glossary)."]],"dohna-s25-031":["Sermons must be generally understandable. A force must prevail in them that works on every soul. They do indeed come together out of a common end. A. Hermann Francke says the sermons must be so constituted that if a human being has heard only one of them, he finds a perfect morality. *Condescendenz* and *Coascendenz* is a great gift, to distinguish, to choose what this and what that listener needs. It takes a great deal of genius to teach little to someone who can grasp little. In an auditorium, as in the writing of a book, one must *coascend*, rather providing for the greater capacities, out of which something does after all come.",["Kraft = force; Zweck = end; Fähigkeiten = capacities; gemeinschaftlich = common (Gemeinschaft = community, not used here).","fassen is rendered 'grasp' throughout (here and in 051 and 041); verständlich = 'understandable'.","'Condescendenz' and 'Coascendenz' are kept as in the source; the verb 'coascendieren' is rendered 'coascend' (the Latinate German form is carried over).","'Genie' rendered 'genius'; 'A. Hermann Francke' as printed (the initial is left as it stands)."]],"dohna-s25-032":["We have two kinds of presentation",["Art = kind; Vortrag = presentation. The sentence breaks off here; the next passage continues it."]],"dohna-s25-033":["1. didactic and 2. polemical. The first is instructive (dogmatic), the second merely freeing from errors.",["belehrend rendered 'instructive' (cf. Belehrung = teaching in the glossary)."]],"dohna-s25-034":["We refute another when we refute his assertion. (\"*antworten*\" and \"*beantworten*\" — different.) It is incumbent on him to prove who says that the other is in the wrong.",["'refutieren' (Latinate) and 'widerlegen' both rendered 'refute'.","OCR 'Aviderlegen' corrected to 'widerlegen'; 'hegt ob' to 'liegt ob'; the OCR braces around the parenthesis read as parentheses.","antworten / beantworten are mentioned words and left in German; both are 'answer' in English, so the distinction cannot be shown.","Behauptung = assertion. 'Unrecht haben' rendered 'to be in the wrong'."]],"dohna-s25-035":["In every dispute we have *thesin* and *antithesin* — *thesis* (*status*) *controversiae*. The determination of the question [this often has great difficulties] is called *forma controversiae*. Here one often discovers a logomachy — word-dispute. But a logomachy is never wont to last long. —",["Bestimmung = determination; 'Quaestion' (Latinate German form) rendered 'question'; Streit = dispute.","'Logomachie' is the German form of the Greek-derived term and is rendered 'logomachy'.","OCR braces around *status* read as parentheses."]],"dohna-s25-036":["The argumentations are divided into",["Einteilung = division; the sentence is continued in the following passages."]],"dohna-s25-037":["1. *argumentationes* κατ᾽ ἀλήθειαν, from objective,",["The Greek is garbled in the OCR ('koct ' aAnOeiccv'); read as κατ᾽ ἀλήθειαν.","'aus objektiven' stands without a noun; none is supplied (038 has 'Gründen')."]],"dohna-s25-038":["2. *argumentationes* κατ᾽ ἄνθρωπον, from subjective grounds [i.e. from the assertions of the subject, as e.g. in the Gospel, that spirits are, because they believed in ghosts. —]",["The Greek is garbled in the OCR ('Kcnr5 avöpcoiTov'); read as κατ᾽ ἄνθρωπον.","Grund = ground; Behauptung = assertion; 'Subjekt' = 'subject'.","'glaubten Gespenster' (accusative object) rendered 'believed in ghosts'; the English preposition has no counterpart in the German."]],"dohna-s25-039":["A wager is a union for punishment for him who has erred. The *argumentatio ad crumenam* is quite fair. The *argumentum a verecundia*, as the monk Abelard said: *Si omnes patres sic — ego non sic.* — Instance, a particular proposition that contradicts a general one. Retorsion, when someone proves conversely, namely makes out of my contested proposition a sequel that hits the opponent. —",["OCR 'sie' in the Latin read as 'sic' (twice); 'nämhch' read as 'nämlich'.","'billig' is ambiguous (fair / cheap); 'fair' is chosen, the other reading remains possible.","Satz = proposition; besonder = particular; Folge = sequel (glossary), not 'consequence' (Folgerung); Beweis/beweisen = proof/prove.","'Instanz' and 'Retorsion' kept as Latinate German forms ('Instance', 'Retorsion')."]],"dohna-s25-040":["*Consequentiarius*, he who draws from the theoretical assertions of another sequels that bring danger. [*Consequentiarius* is he who draws from my propositions consequences that are disadvantageous to someone's person. —]",["'Folgen' = sequels, 'Folgerungen' = consequences, following the glossary; the two German words are distinct here.","Behauptungen = assertions; Sätze = propositions."]],"dohna-s25-041":["Apology is defense against a maker of consequences. A formal disputation — there the actors are the respondent and the opponent. To make it at once graspable for the respondent, one speaks in inferences. The rule contains mainly this, that the conclusion of his argument directly contradicts the *thesis* of the respondent — *per prosyllogismos*. The *praeses*, as assistant of the respondent, seeks to settle the dispute. —",["'Konsequenzenmacher' (Latinate Konsequenz) rendered 'maker of consequences'; Folgerung is also 'consequence' (one English word for two German words).","Schlüssen = inferences; 'Conclusion' (Latin-derived) rendered 'conclusion'; 'acteurs' = 'actors'.","faßlich = 'graspable' (cf. fassen). OCR 'Avelcher'/'Aviderlegen' type slips corrected silently."]],"dohna-s25-042":["Pragmatic is called that which makes prudent. The estimation of effects from their causes.",["Beurteilung = estimation (glossary); Wirkung = effect; Ursache = cause; klug = prudent (not in glossary)."]],"dohna-s25-043":["*Compilator* is called he who makes one out of many books. [It is incomparably harder to express oneself briefly and coherently, e.g. in a letter — handbook — than prolixly.] *Epitomator*, who makes a small one out of a large one. [He must be a connoisseur, in order not to violate the spirit of the work even in the extract.] *Plagiarius*, a learned thief, who steals from the other his thoughts.",["zusammenhängend rendered 'coherently' (cf. Zusammenhang = interconnection); weitläuftig = 'prolixly'.","Kenner = 'connoisseur'; Geist = spirit; Gedanken = thoughts."]],"dohna-s25-044":["The author speaks last of the character of the scholar. A naturalist is he who acquires for himself cognitions that make up no system. Metaphysics and morals are the hobbyhorses for such people. [One can have naturalists in all fields. —]",["OCR 'Avelcher' read as 'welcher'.","Erkenntnisse = cognitions; Fächern = 'fields' (the OCR mark '■' before the dash is omitted as apparatus)."]],"dohna-s25-045":["*Autodidactos*, who has science without a teacher [like Lambert], who has either drawn everything from himself or at least, in the reading of scientific books, been his own explainer.",["Wissenschaft = science; wissenschaftlich = 'scientific'; Erklärer = 'explainer' (cf. Erklärung)."]],"dohna-s25-046":["There are properly in no language synonyms. For when the words were invented, one certainly wanted to designate with each a particular concept, which one will also certainly always find upon closer investigation of the word. E.g. \"Roß\" (rider), \"Pferd\" (*genus*), \"Gaul\", \"Mähre\" (work), (\"Kobbel\", whence comes *caballus*), each carries a particular concept with it.",["The German words are mentioned, hence in quotation marks and left untranslated; the parenthetical glosses are the source's own.","Begriff = concept; besonder = particular; 'Genus' (Gattung in the glossary) rendered *genus*; eigentlich = 'properly'."]],"dohna-s25-047":["We have earlier, in the enumeration of the methods, forgotten the mathematical. This is no other than the synthetic, which sets out from the first grounds of a cognition and stops at the last sequels. The first in this method is now the definition, then axiom, theorem, problem, etc.",["'die mathematische' has no noun in the German ('Methode' is not repeated); none supplied.","Folgen = sequels; Gründe = grounds; Erkenntnis = cognition; 'Definition' (Latinate) and Erklärung are both 'definition' in English; 'Problem' (Latinate) and Aufgabe are both 'problem'; 'Theorem' = Lehrsatz in the glossary."]],"dohna-s25-048":["[*Scholion*, *Corollar*. Wolff presented philosophy according to this method, which cannot be done.]",["'Corollar' kept in its German-Latin form; 'sich nicht tun läßt' rendered 'cannot be done'."]],"dohna-s25-049":["Remark. In conclusion Kant added still some things about meditating, methodical thinking. He said namely, it came down in this (as already mentioned above) mainly to the two pieces:",["'Zum Schluß' rendered 'In conclusion' (Schluß elsewhere = inference); 'käme ... an' is reported speech (subjunctive) and is rendered 'came down to'.","Stücke = 'pieces'. The sentence is continued in the following passages."]],"dohna-s25-050":["1. to know exactly what one actually wants, and afterwards",["'wissen' here is the verb ('to know'); Wissen = knowledge (scientia) in the glossary is not at issue."]],"dohna-s25-051":["2. what it comes down to in this. Now he cited, e.g., how much trouble it had cost him, when he was occupied with the thought of writing the *Critique of Pure Reason*, to know what he actually wanted. Finally he had found, everything could be grasped in the question: Are synthetic propositions *a priori* possible? — Yes. But it comes down to this, that we can give them a corresponding intuition. If this cannot happen, however, they do not have this property. From this one sees how much meditating is facilitated by this method.",["'ankommen auf' rendered 'come down to' in both places (also in 049); 'fassen' = 'grasp'.","'Eigenschaft' (not in glossary) rendered 'property'; Beschaffenheit, also glossed 'property', is not used here.","Anschauung = intuition; Sätze = propositions; 'a priori' left uncommitted as to attachment (it follows 'synthetische Sätze' directly)."]],"dohna-s25-052":["[NB. This happened in the *Repetitorio* on Saturday, the *Collegium* had already closed earlier on Friday. — ]",["'Sonnabends' and 'Freitags' are adverbial genitives and could be 'on Saturday' / 'on Saturdays'; the singular is chosen.","Repetitorio (Latin ablative) rendered *Repetitorio*; Collegium left as *Collegium*."]],"dohna-s25-053":["End of Kant's Logic,",[]],"dohna-s25-054":["Saturday, the . . .th of September 1792.",["The day of the month is left blank in the source ('den . . .sten')."]]};
